window.JG_DB["Shyvana"] = {"Bel'Veth":{"tldr":"Bel'Veth spikes hard once she has jungle takedowns stacking Death in Lavender's attack speed, then dives with Void Surge (Q) and locks a duel down with Royal Maelstrom (E)'s channel. Shyvana answers with Emberstrike (Q) auto-attack resets and the shield off Inferno Aegis (W), closing with Dragon's Descent (R) for the finish. Bel'Veth wants to avoid Shyvana's early duels and instead snowball off camps and picks until Endless Banquet (R) lets her transform and outscale.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Shyvana Favored","why":"Open with Inferno Aegis (W) for the shield, movement speed, and delayed AoE explosion, your only point at level 1 but enough to clear a camp safely. Bel'Veth's single point in Void Surge or Above and Below doesn't threaten a clean level-1 duel or camp contest. This establishes a fast, healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Shyvana Dominant","why":"Unlocking Emberstrike (Q) gives you an auto-attack reset that also hits nearby enemies, sharply raising your dueling damage. If Bel'Veth tries an early invade, Inferno Aegis's shield absorbs her Void Surge burst long enough for your Q resets to win the fight before her attack-speed stacking snowballs. This secures your early map pressure and punishes poor invades."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Shyvana Absolute Peak","why":"Access to Molten Burst (E) adds a slowing fireball that explodes on large targets, letting you lock Bel'Veth down for a full trade or speed through tougher camps. Landing the slow denies her Void Surge escape once she's already low. This gives you the tools to win most early brawls you commit to."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early farming thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive power-farming and dragon rushing. Keep constant tabs on Bel'Veth's jungle clearing lines and only intercept them if you have a clear advantage or counter-gank opportunity. Prioritize reaching your level 6 power spike over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Dragon's Descent Domination","why":"Dragon's Descent (R) grants massive bonus health, an unstoppable dash, and empowered basic abilities. Landing the dash to pin Bel'Veth against a wall lets you follow up with empowered Molten Burst's fire trail before she can root into Royal Maelstrom. This spike turns you into a genuine map-wide skirmish and teamfight threat, provided Bel'Veth hasn't already outscaled you through unanswered camps."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Spear of Shojin / Titanic Spike","why":"Completing your core item triggers a massive damage, health, and wave clear multiplier that completely overpowers traditional brawlers or isolated targets. Bel'Veth's first item turns the dash chain into sustained damage — she stops trading and starts running people down."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Juggernaut","why":"Shyvana transitions into a high-impact skirmish diver who relies on generating Fury and landing Dragon's Descent initiations. Your late-game damage scaling shifts into an AoE-focused line, so coordinate with your team before diving in rather than fighting alone into a transformed Endless Banquet Bel'Veth. Around objective pits, use Molten Burst's slow to keep her locked out of range while you secure the smite."}],"start":"Start at Red Buff or Raptors, using Inferno Aegis's shield and delayed explosion to clear safely at level 1. Track whether Bel'Veth shows near your side of the map before committing to a full clear, since Void Surge gives her a fast dash into your camps. Bel'Veth clears fast and lopsided — the four Void Surge directions let her leave a camp half-done and arrive somewhere she should not be.","scuttle":"Contest the scuttle crab using Molten Burst (E) to slow it while you finish it off with Emberstrike (Q) resets. Bel'Veth's Above and Below (W) can knock you up and slow you if she contests, so commit only once you've confirmed her position. Bel'Veth takes scuttle quickly with Death in Lavender attack speed and can dash out of the contest the moment it turns.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs, using Inferno Aegis's AoE explosion to clear the pack quickly. If Bel'Veth shows to contest, Molten Burst's slow lets you catch and punish her before Void Surge carries her out. Bel'Veth wants every Void Grub and Rift Herald for the coral, because true form is what her whole game is built on.","invade":"Walk into Bel'Veth's jungle once you confirm she's committed to a low-health camp. Lead with Molten Burst to slow her, then pop Inferno Aegis for the shield and AoE explosion before she can Void Surge away. Bel'Veth holds four charges of escape, so an invade only works if you cut the dash line rather than chase it.","watch":"Watch for Bel'Veth snowballing off unopposed camps and picks — every takedown stacks Death in Lavender's attack speed permanently, so a fed Bel'Veth's duel power climbs fast even before she uses Endless Banquet (R) to transform. Count her Void Surge charges — with all four spent she is a melee champion with no way out of the pit.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using wards to safely trace Bel'Veth's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming to reach your item and Dragon's Descent power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable frontline threat. Bel'Veth is weakest before she banks a coral, when her health pool is small and her stacks have not built.","split":"You can split push exceptionally well due to your massive wave clear, pulling enemies to side lanes while you generate Fury. In teamfights, you must be the primary engager, utilizing Dragon's Descent to dive straight into the enemy formation and unleash your AoE damage. Bel'Veth takes side waves happily, because the attack-speed stacks make her a fast pusher as well as a duellist.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies by launching your Dragon's Descent over walls to secure high-priority backline access. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely burning their frontline tanks and instantly shredding their backline carriers with empowered Molten Burst. Bel'Veth picks come from dashing through a wall onto someone already slowed, so watch the terrain behind you.","win":"Generate an unanswerable early-to-mid game gold lead through successful power farming and absolute maximum dragon control. Close the match by relentlessly driving through teamfights in dragon form, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable juggernaut before they can scale their defensive items. Bel'Veth wins by banking a coral early, entering true form on an objective, and stacking through a fight nobody can end quickly."},"Diana":{"tldr":"Diana leans on Pale Cascade (W)'s shield and Lunar Rush (E)'s reset-on-Moonlight dash to duel and reset chases. Shyvana answers with Emberstrike (Q) auto-attack resets and the shield off Inferno Aegis (W), then closes with Dragon's Descent (R). Diana has no hard CC of her own until Moonfall (R) pulls and slows, so she wants to land Crescent Strike's arc to reset Lunar Rush before you can pin her down.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Shyvana Favored","why":"Open with Inferno Aegis (W) for the shield, movement speed, and delayed AoE explosion, your only point at level 1 but enough to clear a camp safely. Diana's single point in Crescent Strike or Pale Cascade is poke and a shield, not enough to threaten a level-1 duel or camp contest. This establishes a fast, healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Shyvana Dominant","why":"Unlocking Emberstrike (Q) gives you an auto-attack reset that also hits nearby enemies, sharply raising your dueling damage. If Diana tries an early invade, Inferno Aegis's shield absorbs her Pale Cascade burst long enough for your Q resets to punish her before Lunar Rush can reposition her out. This secures your early map pressure and punishes poor invades."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Shyvana Absolute Peak","why":"Access to Molten Burst (E) adds a slowing fireball that explodes on large targets, letting you keep Diana in range even after her Lunar Rush dash resolves. Landing the slow denies her the follow-up reset she needs to disengage cleanly. This gives you the tools to win most early brawls you commit to."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early farming thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive power-farming and dragon rushing. Keep constant tabs on Diana's jungle clearing lines and only intercept them if you have a clear advantage or counter-gank opportunity. Prioritize reaching your level 6 power spike over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Dragon's Descent Domination","why":"Dragon's Descent (R) grants massive bonus health, an unstoppable dash, and empowered basic abilities. Landing the dash to pin Diana against a wall lets you follow up with empowered Molten Burst's fire trail before she can pull off a clean Lunar Rush reset. This spike turns you into a genuine map-wide skirmish and teamfight threat that Diana's lack of hard CC struggles to answer."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Spear of Shojin / Titanic Spike","why":"Completing your core item triggers a massive damage, health, and wave clear multiplier that completely overpowers traditional brawlers or isolated targets. Diana's first item turns the Q-dash-Moonfall rotation from a chunk into a kill on anything without an escape."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Juggernaut","why":"Shyvana transitions into a high-impact skirmish diver who relies on generating Fury and landing Dragon's Descent initiations. Your late-game damage scaling shifts into an AoE-focused line, so coordinate with your team before diving into a full Moonfall pull rather than fighting alone. Around objective pits, use Molten Burst's slow to keep Diana locked out of range while you secure the smite."}],"start":"Start at Red Buff or Raptors, using Inferno Aegis's shield and delayed explosion to clear safely at level 1. Track whether Diana shows near your side of the map before committing to a full clear, since Crescent Strike's 900-range arc can poke you from outside your own tools. Diana clears fast with Moonsilver Blade resets and Pale Cascade shields, and she leaves camps at close to full health.","scuttle":"Contest the scuttle crab using Molten Burst (E) to slow it while you finish it off with Emberstrike (Q) resets. If Diana contests, watch for Lunar Rush — it's free to recast on a target she's already hit with Moonlight, so a landed Crescent Strike lets her reposition for free. Diana contests scuttle with Crescent Strike poke rather than a fight, and commits only once Lunar Rush is up.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs, using Inferno Aegis's AoE explosion to clear the pack quickly. If Diana shows to contest, Molten Burst's slow lets you catch her before Lunar Rush gives her a reset out of range. Diana takes objectives quickly with the passive triple-strike, but her smite fight is weak until her ultimate is online.","invade":"Walk into Diana's jungle once you confirm she's committed to a low-health camp. Lead with Molten Burst to slow her, then pop Inferno Aegis for the shield and AoE explosion before Pale Cascade's shield can soak the trade. Diana can only dash to something she has marked, so invading before Crescent Strike lands takes away her escape.","watch":"Watch for Crescent Strike (Q) landing on you first — the Moonlight debuff it applies gives Diana a free, no-cooldown Lunar Rush dash onto you, letting her reposition for a duel or gank on her own terms. The whole kit keys off Crescent Strike — if the arc misses, the dash and the pull are both off the table.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using wards to safely trace Diana's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming to reach your item and Dragon's Descent power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable frontline threat. Diana is weakest before six, when her clear is excellent and her kill threat genuinely is not.","split":"You can split push exceptionally well due to your massive wave clear, pulling enemies to side lanes while you generate Fury. In teamfights, you must be the primary engager, utilizing Dragon's Descent to dive straight into the enemy formation and unleash your AoE damage. Diana pushes a side lane very fast off her passive and is a real threat to anyone who answers it alone.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies by launching your Dragon's Descent over walls to secure high-priority backline access. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely burning their frontline tanks and instantly shredding their backline carriers with empowered Molten Burst. Diana picks are a Moonfall pull on someone standing near a wall, which is why she wants the fight in a corridor.","win":"Generate an unanswerable early-to-mid game gold lead through successful power farming and absolute maximum dragon control. Close the match by relentlessly driving through teamfights in dragon form, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable juggernaut before they can scale their defensive items. Diana wins by reaching six and two items and then one-rotating whichever squishy steps a pace too far forward."},"Ekko":{"tldr":"Ekko threatens ganks with Parallel Convergence (W)'s delayed stun and can escape bad trades with Phase Dive (E) or a Chronobreak (R) rewind. Shyvana answers with Emberstrike (Q) auto-attack resets and the shield off Inferno Aegis (W), then closes with Dragon's Descent (R). Ekko wants to land Timewinder (Q) or the W anomaly on you before you're in his face, since he has little answer once you're already through Chronobreak's window.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Shyvana Favored","why":"Open with Inferno Aegis (W) for the shield, movement speed, and delayed AoE explosion, your only point at level 1 but enough to clear a camp safely. Ekko's single point in Timewinder or Parallel Convergence is poke or a slow setup, not enough to threaten a level-1 duel or camp contest. This establishes a fast, healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Shyvana Dominant","why":"Unlocking Emberstrike (Q) gives you an auto-attack reset that also hits nearby enemies, sharply raising your dueling damage. If Ekko tries an early invade, Inferno Aegis's shield absorbs his Timewinder poke long enough for your Q resets to punish him before Phase Dive can pull him back out. This secures your early map pressure and punishes poor invades."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Shyvana Absolute Peak","why":"Access to Molten Burst (E) adds a slowing fireball that explodes on large targets, letting you keep Ekko in range even after his Phase Dive roll. Landing the slow denies him the space he needs to reset a Parallel Convergence anomaly. This gives you the tools to win most early brawls you commit to."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early farming thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive power-farming and dragon rushing. Keep constant tabs on Ekko's jungle clearing lines and only intercept them if you have a clear advantage or counter-gank opportunity. Prioritize reaching your level 6 power spike over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Dragon's Descent Domination","why":"Dragon's Descent (R) grants massive bonus health, an unstoppable dash, and empowered basic abilities. Landing the dash to pin Ekko against a wall lets you follow up with empowered Molten Burst's fire trail before he can burn Chronobreak to escape. This spike turns you into a genuine map-wide skirmish and teamfight threat, though a well-timed Chronobreak can still bail him out of a bad trade."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Spear of Shojin / Titanic Spike","why":"Completing your core item triggers a massive damage, health, and wave clear multiplier that completely overpowers traditional brawlers or isolated targets. Ekko's first item is the point where his dive stops being a trade and becomes a kill, with the rewind as insurance."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Juggernaut","why":"Shyvana transitions into a high-impact skirmish diver who relies on generating Fury and landing Dragon's Descent initiations. Your late-game damage scaling shifts into an AoE-focused line, so coordinate with your team before diving in, since a well-timed Chronobreak can turn a bad Ekko engage back around. Around objective pits, use Molten Burst's slow to keep the contesting smiter locked down before he can Phase Dive to safety."}],"start":"Start at Red Buff or Raptors, using Inferno Aegis's shield and delayed explosion to clear safely at level 1. Track whether Ekko shows near your side of the map before committing to a full clear, since Timewinder's 1075 range and Parallel Convergence's 1600 range both threaten a camp from well outside your reach. Ekko clears at a fair pace with Timewinder and passive procs, but he takes real damage doing it before level four.","scuttle":"Contest the scuttle crab using Molten Burst (E) to slow it while you finish it off with Emberstrike (Q) resets. If Ekko contests, watch for a pre-placed Parallel Convergence anomaly — if you're caught inside it when he enters, its stun opens you up for a full combo. Ekko wants the scuttle fight only if Parallel Convergence is already drawn on the crab — without the stun he backs off.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs, using Inferno Aegis's AoE explosion to clear the pack quickly. If Ekko shows to contest, Molten Burst's slow lets you catch him before Phase Dive gives him a free reposition. Ekko is a poor early smite fighter, so objectives before six are usually contestable if you arrive with health.","invade":"Walk into Ekko's jungle once you confirm he's committed to a low-health camp. Lead with Molten Burst to slow him, then pop Inferno Aegis for the shield and AoE explosion before he can set up a Parallel Convergence anomaly to turn the fight. Ekko holds Phase Dive and Chronobreak, so a successful invade needs to land before six or with both of those tracked.","watch":"Watch for a pre-placed Parallel Convergence (W) anomaly near a camp you're about to clear — if Ekko lures you into it and enters behind you, the delayed stun opens a full combo before you can react. Watch for the Parallel Convergence ring on the ground — everything he does afterwards is timed to that stun.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using wards to safely trace Ekko's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming to reach your item and Dragon's Descent power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable frontline threat. Ekko is weakest between three and six, when his clear costs health and Chronobreak is not there to undo a mistake.","split":"You can split push exceptionally well due to your massive wave clear, pulling enemies to side lanes while you generate Fury. In teamfights, you must be the primary engager, utilizing Dragon's Descent to dive straight into the enemy formation and unleash your AoE damage. Ekko is one of the better side laners in the jungle, because he can dive the wave and rewind out of the answer.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies by launching your Dragon's Descent over walls to secure high-priority backline access. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely burning their frontline tanks and instantly shredding their backline carriers with empowered Molten Burst. Ekko picks come from a W stun placed behind you in a choke, so cross open ground rather than corridors when he is missing.","win":"Generate an unanswerable early-to-mid game gold lead through successful power farming and absolute maximum dragon control. Close the match by relentlessly driving through teamfights in dragon form, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable juggernaut before they can scale their defensive items. Ekko wins by taking dives nobody else can take and rewinding out of the punish until the lead compounds."},"Elise":{"tldr":"Elise threatens early picks with Cocoon (E)'s stun in Human Form, then finishes with Rappel's descent in Spider Form. Shyvana answers with Emberstrike (Q) auto-attack resets and the shield off Inferno Aegis (W), then closes with Dragon's Descent (R). Elise wants to land Cocoon before level 6, since Shyvana has zero hard CC of her own until Dragon's Descent unlocks, leaving early ganks and duels unanswered if the stun connects.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Shyvana Favored","why":"Open with Inferno Aegis (W) for the shield, movement speed, and delayed AoE explosion, your only point at level 1 but enough to clear a camp safely. Elise's single point in Neurotoxin or Cocoon threatens poke and range but not a level-1 duel or camp contest. This establishes a fast, healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Shyvana Dominant","why":"Unlocking Emberstrike (Q) gives you an auto-attack reset that also hits nearby enemies, sharply raising your dueling damage. If Elise tries an early invade without landing Cocoon, Inferno Aegis's shield absorbs her poke long enough for your Q resets to win the fight outright. This secures your early map pressure and punishes poor invades."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Shyvana Absolute Peak","why":"Access to Molten Burst (E) adds a slowing fireball that explodes on large targets, letting you close down Elise before she can kite in Spider Form. Since you still have no hard CC of your own at this stage, avoiding Cocoon range matters more than any tool you gain here. This gives you the tools to win most brawls you commit to, as long as the stun doesn't land first."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early farming thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive power-farming and dragon rushing. Keep constant tabs on Elise's jungle clearing lines and only intercept them if you have a clear advantage or counter-gank opportunity. Prioritize reaching your level 6 power spike over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Dragon's Descent Domination","why":"Dragon's Descent (R) grants massive bonus health, an unstoppable dash, and empowered basic abilities. Landing the dash to pin Elise against a wall lets you follow up with empowered Molten Burst's fire trail before she can Rappel to safety. This spike turns you into a genuine map-wide skirmish and teamfight threat, and finally gives you hard CC of your own to answer a Cocoon."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Spear of Shojin / Titanic Spike","why":"Completing your core item triggers a massive damage, health, and wave clear multiplier that completely overpowers traditional brawlers or isolated targets. Elise's first item keeps the spider-form burst relevant a while longer, but it is the last window where it one-shots."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Juggernaut","why":"Shyvana transitions into a high-impact skirmish diver who relies on generating Fury and landing Dragon's Descent initiations. Your late-game damage scaling shifts into an AoE-focused line, so coordinate with your team before diving in rather than eating a Cocoon setup alone. Around objective pits, use Molten Burst's slow to keep the contesting smiter locked down while you secure the smite."}],"start":"Start at Red Buff or Raptors, using Inferno Aegis's shield and delayed explosion to clear safely at level 1. Track whether Elise shows near your side of the map before committing to a full clear, since Cocoon's 1075 range can stun you from well outside your own reach. Elise clears well off spiderlings and human-form poke, and she is one of the healthiest junglers on the map at three.","scuttle":"Contest the scuttle crab using Molten Burst (E) to slow it while you finish it off with Emberstrike (Q) resets. If Elise contests, stay alert for Cocoon — a landed stun lets her switch to Spider Form and burst you down before you can fight back. Elise contests scuttle hard — a landed Cocoon at that timer is usually a kill and not just a crab.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs, using Inferno Aegis's AoE explosion to clear the pack quickly. If Elise shows to contest, Molten Burst's slow lets you close before she can land Cocoon and switch forms to fight you. Elise has poor objective damage for her power level, so a drake she takes alone is slow and interruptible.","invade":"Walk into Elise's jungle once you confirm she's committed to a low-health camp. Lead with Molten Burst to slow her, then pop Inferno Aegis for the shield and AoE explosion before she can land Cocoon to turn the fight around. Elise escapes an invade with Rappel, which makes her untargetable, so the punish has to land before that E is up.","watch":"Watch for Cocoon (E) landing on you mid-clear or mid-duel — it's Elise's only hard CC, but you have none of your own before Dragon's Descent unlocks at 6, so a connected stun before then goes fully unanswered. Cocoon is the whole matchup — if it misses, spider form has to walk into you with no way back out.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using wards to safely trace Elise's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming to reach your item and Dragon's Descent power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable frontline threat. Elise is weakest after the mid game, when the Cocoon still lands but the burst behind it stops killing.","split":"You can split push exceptionally well due to your massive wave clear, pulling enemies to side lanes while you generate Fury. In teamfights, you must be the primary engager, utilizing Dragon's Descent to dive straight into the enemy formation and unleash your AoE damage. Elise does not side lane; she wants to be near her team, where a pick turns straight into an objective.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies by launching your Dragon's Descent over walls to secure high-priority backline access. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely burning their frontline tanks and instantly shredding their backline carriers with empowered Molten Burst. Elise picks are a Cocoon from a bush at a river entrance, so sweep before you cross and never walk the same line twice.","win":"Generate an unanswerable early-to-mid game gold lead through successful power farming and absolute maximum dragon control. Close the match by relentlessly driving through teamfights in dragon form, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable juggernaut before they can scale their defensive items. Elise wins by cashing her level-three and mid-game power into objectives before her damage falls behind."},"Evelynn":{"tldr":"Evelynn is weak pre-6, unable to threaten a duel until Allure (W)'s charm and Camouflage stealth come online. Shyvana answers with Emberstrike (Q) auto-attack resets and the shield off Inferno Aegis (W), closing with Dragon's Descent (R). Evelynn wants to farm unseen until level 6, then use Camouflage to set up an Allure charm or a Last Caress (R) burst that Shyvana's true damage can't out-race in melee.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Shyvana Favored","why":"Open with Inferno Aegis (W) for the shield, movement speed, and delayed AoE explosion, your only point at level 1 but enough to clear a camp safely. Evelynn's single point in Hate Spike or Allure poses little duel threat this early. This establishes a fast, healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Shyvana Dominant","why":"Unlocking Emberstrike (Q) gives you an auto-attack reset that also hits nearby enemies, sharply raising your dueling damage. Evelynn has no charm or escape at this level, so if she tries an early invade, Inferno Aegis's shield plus your Q resets win the fight outright. This secures your early map pressure and punishes poor invades."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Shyvana Absolute Peak","why":"Access to Molten Burst (E) adds a slowing fireball that explodes on large targets, letting you close down Evelynn before her Whiplash dash can create distance. Still pre-6 and without Camouflage, she has little way to avoid this. This gives you the tools to win most early brawls you commit to."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early farming thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive power-farming and dragon rushing. Keep constant tabs on Evelynn's jungle clearing lines and only intercept them if you have a clear advantage or counter-gank opportunity. Prioritize reaching your level 6 power spike over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Dragon's Descent Domination","why":"Dragon's Descent (R) grants massive bonus health, an unstoppable dash, and empowered basic abilities, arriving at the same level Evelynn gains Camouflage and Last Caress. Landing the dash before she vanishes lets you follow up with empowered Molten Burst's fire trail. This spike turns you into a genuine map-wide skirmish threat right as Evelynn's own spike lands."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Spear of Shojin / Titanic Spike","why":"Completing your core item triggers a massive damage, health, and wave clear multiplier that completely overpowers traditional brawlers or isolated targets. Evelynn's first item plus camouflage is the spike that flips the matchup — the charm rotation starts killing outright."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Juggernaut","why":"Shyvana transitions into a high-impact skirmish diver who relies on generating Fury and landing Dragon's Descent initiations. Your late-game damage scaling shifts into an AoE-focused line, so coordinate with your team before diving into a fed, Camouflaged Evelynn rather than fighting her alone. Around objective pits, use Molten Burst's slow to keep the contesting smiter locked down while you secure the smite."}],"start":"Start at Red Buff or Raptors, using Inferno Aegis's shield and delayed explosion to clear safely at level 1. Evelynn has no stealth before level 6, so track her lane and jungle presence directly rather than assuming she's hidden. Evelynn clears slowly and unhealthily before six, so her early pathing is usually the safest, longest route she can take.","scuttle":"Contest the scuttle crab using Molten Burst (E) to slow it while you finish it off with Emberstrike (Q) resets. Pre-6, Evelynn has no way to contest this cleanly without visible risk; after 6, ward the bushes since Camouflage lets her approach unseen. Evelynn will not contest a scuttle before six unless she has numbers — an unstealthed Evelynn loses that fight.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs, using Inferno Aegis's AoE explosion to clear the pack quickly. Evelynn has little answer here before level 6 — if she shows to contest, Molten Burst's slow lets you punish her outright. Evelynn cannot solo objectives early, so anything before her ultimate is a timer you can take with confidence.","invade":"Walk into Evelynn's jungle once you confirm she's committed to a low-health camp. Lead with Molten Burst to slow her, then pop Inferno Aegis for the shield and AoE explosion — before level 6 she has no charm and no stealth to escape with. Evelynn is the best invade target in the jungle before six; after six she simply is not there to be invaded.","watch":"Watch for Allure (W) once Evelynn hits 6 — a delayed charm from up to 1600 range that sets up her Last Caress (R) burst. Ward key bushes, since Camouflage lets her approach that range unseen. From six on, treat every unswept bush as occupied — the charm is the start of everything she does.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using wards to safely trace Evelynn's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming to reach your item and Dragon's Descent power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable frontline threat. Evelynn is weakest from one to five, and it is not close; this matchup is decided by how hard you punish that.","split":"You can split push exceptionally well due to your massive wave clear, pulling enemies to side lanes while you generate Fury. In teamfights, you must be the primary engager, utilizing Dragon's Descent to dive straight into the enemy formation and unleash your AoE damage. Evelynn does not hold a side lane, she hunts one — a laner alone on that side is the bait, not the answer.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies by launching your Dragon's Descent over walls to secure high-priority backline access. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely burning their frontline tanks and instantly shredding their backline carriers with empowered Molten Burst. Evelynn picks are a charm out of camouflage onto whoever walked without a sweeper, and that is the whole late game.","win":"Generate an unanswerable early-to-mid game gold lead through successful power farming and absolute maximum dragon control. Close the match by relentlessly driving through teamfights in dragon form, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable juggernaut before they can scale their defensive items. Evelynn wins by surviving to six, then removing one person per fight before the fight has properly started."},"Graves":{"tldr":"Graves poses a ranged threat through End of the Line (Q) burst and Smoke Screen (W) vision denial, with Quickdraw (E) to dash away from a chase. Shyvana out-clears with Emberstrike (Q) resets and Inferno Aegis (W) shielding, closing distance to force the melee fight Graves wants to avoid. Graves wants to kite and poke from range before you can stick to him; Shyvana wants to shield through the poke and reach a duel range where his kit has no hard CC to stop her.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Shyvana Favored","why":"Open camps with Inferno Aegis (W) for its shield, movement speed, and delayed detonation, then extend with Emberstrike (Q) auto-resets once it's up. This establishes a fast, healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Shyvana Dominant","why":"Unlocking Emberstrike (Q) gives you an auto-attack reset that accelerates your single-target clear and dueling. Graves has no hard CC to punish an aggressive approach at this level — if he attempts an early invade, use Inferno Aegis's shield to close the gap and Emberstrike's reset to shatter his health bar before he can Quickdraw away."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Shyvana Absolute Peak","why":"Molten Burst (E) rounds out your kit as a slowing fireball that explodes on hitting a large target, giving you a way to close distance in a duel or clear high-health monsters faster. Against a kiting Graves this is your best tool to punish him for standing still to poke instead of disengaging with Quickdraw."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early farming thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive power-farming and dragon rushing. Keep constant tabs on Graves's jungle clearing lines and only intercept them if you have a clear advantage or counter-gank opportunity. Prioritize reaching your level 6 power spike over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Dragon's Descent Domination","why":"Dragon's Descent (R) transforms you into a Dragon with bonus health, an unstoppable dash, and empowered basic abilities, causing enemies it hits to flee in fear. Use it to close on Graves before he can Quickdraw away, then follow up with empowered Molten Burst and Emberstrike for the kill."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Spear of Shojin / Titanic Spike","why":"Completing your core item triggers a massive damage, health, and wave clear multiplier that completely overpowers traditional brawlers or isolated targets. Graves' first item is a large step in raw damage — the double-shot at point blank starts removing squishies outright."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Juggernaut","why":"Shyvana transitions into a high-impact skirmish diver who relies on generating Fury and landing Dragon's Descent initiations. Coordinate with your team before diving, since Graves's Collateral Damage (R) can burst a low-health target from range if the fight goes long. Around objective pits, use Molten Burst's slow to close the smiter duel against Graves before he can kite it out."}],"start":"Start your clear at the Raptor or Red Buff camp, using Inferno Aegis (W)'s shield, movement speed, and delayed detonation to speed through camps. Graves has one of the fastest clears in the game — End of the Line through two camps at once means he is level three early.","scuttle":"Contest the scuttle crab with Emberstrike (Q) resets, closing the gap fast since Graves has no hard CC to stop you once you're in range. You can solo early Dragon using your passive Scalemail stacks if Graves is visible ganking top lane. Graves takes scuttle fast and wins the contest at point-blank range, where both shells of a reload land on you.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the moment they spawn, using Inferno Aegis's detonation and Emberstrike resets to clear them rapidly. If Graves contests, close the gap quickly before his End of the Line poke and Smoke Screen vision denial can whittle you down at range. Graves is an excellent objective duellist because Smoke Screen blinds your smite fight and his burst is front-loaded.","invade":"Walk into Graves's jungle once you confirm he's committed to a low-health camp elsewhere. Lead with Inferno Aegis's shield to soak his End of the Line poke before he can Quickdraw away. Invade Graves in the open rather than in his buff pit, because True Grit armour makes him the stronger side of a close-quarters brawl.","watch":"Watch for Smoke Screen (W) cutting your vision mid-camp and slowing you if you're caught in the initial impact, and for Graves using Quickdraw (E) to reset the distance and keep poking with End of the Line (Q) instead of committing to a melee trade. Count his shells: a Graves mid-reload has no burst, and that is the only safe second to step into him.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, avoid contesting scuttle crabs where Graves can freely poke you down with End of the Line before you close the distance. Funnel into farming to reach your first item and Dragon's Descent power spike before forcing fights. Graves is weakest in open ground at range, where his pellets spread and he cannot make the auto-attacks count.","split":"You can split push exceptionally well due to your massive wave clear, pulling enemies to side lanes while you generate Fury. In teamfights, you must be the primary engager, utilizing Dragon's Descent to dive straight into the enemy formation and unleash your AoE damage. Graves is a strong side laner who holds a wave against most of the map and pushes it fast when left alone.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies by launching your Dragon's Descent over walls to secure high-priority backline access. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely burning their frontline tanks and instantly shredding their backline carriers with empowered Molten Burst. Graves picks come from a Smoke Screen dropped on a choke, so never walk a corridor he already has vision of.","win":"Generate an unanswerable early-to-mid game gold lead through successful power farming and absolute maximum dragon control. Close the match by relentlessly driving through teamfights in dragon form, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable juggernaut before they can scale their defensive items. Graves wins by taking your camps, holding a side lane, and shredding anyone who answers him in a tight space."},"Kayn":{"tldr":"Kayn adjusts your early tempo by requiring precise use of Molten Burst (E) slows and Inferno Aegis (W) uptime. Shyvana commands skirmishes through power farming, using Emberstrike (Q) for auto-attack resets and Dragon's Descent (R) for area-of-effect dives once she has enough Fury. Kayn seeks to out-gank your early farming tempo, kite your approach before you can transform, and exploit your lack of hard crowd control in human form.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Shyvana Favored","why":"Start with Emberstrike (Q) to strike the camp and the area around it, and use Inferno Aegis (W) for its shield and movement speed to stay healthy while clearing. This establishes a fast, healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Shyvana Dominant","why":"Emberstrike's (Q) auto-attack reset drastically accelerates your single-target clear and dueling potential. If Kayn attempts an early entry into your jungle, use Inferno Aegis (W) for the shield and speed to close the gap, then chain Emberstrike (Q) resets to shatter their health bar. This secures your early map pressure and punishes poor invades."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Shyvana Absolute Peak","why":"Access to Molten Burst (E) completes your dueling kit: a fireball that explodes on large targets and slows them. Use it to lock down duels or to accelerate your clear against high-health monsters. This gives you the tools to turn most early brawls if you land the slow."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early farming thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive power-farming and dragon rushing. Keep constant tabs on Kayn's jungle clearing lines and only intercept them if you have a clear advantage or counter-gank opportunity. Prioritize reaching your level 6 power spike over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Dragon's Descent Domination","why":"Dragon's Descent (R) transforms Shyvana, granting bonus health, an unstoppable dash, and empowered basic abilities. Land the dash to pin Kayn against a wall, then follow with an empowered Molten Burst (E) to finish the duel. This is a major power spike that turns you into a map-wide teamfight threat."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Spear of Shojin / Titanic Spike","why":"Completing your core item triggers a massive damage, health, and wave clear multiplier that completely overpowers traditional brawlers or isolated targets. Kayn's first item lands around the same time as his form, which is why that window is a sharp jump and not a slope."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Juggernaut","why":"Shyvana transitions into a high-impact skirmish diver and juggernaut who relies on generating Fury and landing Dragon's Descent initiations. Your late-game damage leans on Dragon Form's empowered abilities, so coordinate with your team before diving for burst damage. Around high-tier objective pits, use Molten Burst (E)'s slow to cleanly win the smite duel and secure the objective."}],"start":"Start your clear at the Raptor or Red Buff camp, using Emberstrike (Q) to strike the camp and its surrounding area and Inferno Aegis (W) for the shield and movement speed to keep the path fast. Kayn clears fast off Reaping Slash resets and spends every camp of it buying orbs toward a form.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using Molten Burst (E) to slow it and speed up the kill, establishing river control. You can solo early Dragons behind Scalemail's defensive bonuses if Kayn is visibly committed to a top-lane gank. Kayn contests scuttle only when the orb count needs it — before a form he would rather take the safe crab.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the moment they spawn, using Emberstrike (Q) to clear them rapidly. Use Molten Burst (E) to slow and run down Kayn if they contest, catching them off-guard mid-clear. Kayn is a weak early objective fighter, so pre-form pits are a timer you can take against him.","invade":"Walk boldly into Kayn's jungle the moment you confirm they are engaged with a low-health camp. Lead with Molten Burst (E) to slow them, then pop Inferno Aegis (W) for the shield and delayed detonation to force a high-damage melee duel. Kayn walks out of any invade through a wall with Shadow Step, so the only punish is catching him mid-camp.","watch":"Watch for sudden counter-invades if you burn Inferno Aegis (W) early while clearing a camp, leaving you without its shield and movement speed. This leaves you without your main defensive tool to cleanly turn a fight around. The form is the clock: check which orbs he is taking, because that decides whether he brawls or bursts.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using wards to safely trace Kayn's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming to reach your item and Dragon's Descent power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable frontline threat. Kayn is weakest pre-form, when his stats lag every jungler on the roster and his kit is only mobility.","split":"You can split push exceptionally well due to your massive wave clear, pulling enemies to side lanes while you generate Fury. In teamfights, you must be the primary engager, utilizing Dragon's Descent to dive straight into the enemy formation and unleash your AoE damage. Kayn side lanes very well once formed, and the wall-walk means answering him alone rarely ends with a kill.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies by launching your Dragon's Descent over walls to secure high-priority backline access. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely burning their frontline tanks and instantly shredding their backline carriers with empowered Molten Burst. Kayn picks come out of a wall you did not ward, so treat every piece of terrain near him as an entrance.","win":"Generate an unanswerable early-to-mid game gold lead through successful power farming and absolute maximum dragon control. Close the match by relentlessly driving through teamfights in dragon form, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable juggernaut before they can scale their defensive items. Kayn wins by surviving to his form and then either bursting your carry or brawling through your front line."},"Kha'Zix":{"tldr":"Kha'Zix adjusts your early tempo by threatening from stealth once Unseen Threat is up, forcing precise use of Molten Burst (E) slows and Inferno Aegis (W) uptime. Shyvana commands skirmishes through power farming, using Emberstrike (Q) for auto-attack resets and Dragon's Descent (R) for area-of-effect dives once she has Fury. Kha'Zix seeks to isolate and burst you from stealth before you build Fury, and exploit your lack of hard crowd control in human form.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Shyvana Favored","why":"Start with Emberstrike (Q) to strike the camp and the area around it, and use Inferno Aegis (W) for its shield and movement speed to stay healthy while clearing. This establishes a fast, healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Shyvana Dominant","why":"Emberstrike's (Q) auto-attack reset drastically accelerates your single-target clear and dueling potential. If Kha'Zix attempts an early entry into your jungle, use Inferno Aegis (W) for the shield and speed to close the gap, then chain Emberstrike (Q) resets to shatter their health bar. This secures your early map pressure and punishes poor invades."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Shyvana Absolute Peak","why":"Access to Molten Burst (E) completes your dueling kit: a fireball that explodes on large targets and slows them. Use it to lock down duels or accelerate your clear against high-health monsters. This gives you the tools to turn most early brawls if you land the slow."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early farming thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive power-farming and dragon rushing. Keep constant tabs on Kha'Zix's jungle clearing lines and only intercept them if you have a clear advantage or counter-gank opportunity. Prioritize reaching your level 6 power spike over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Dragon's Descent Domination","why":"Dragon's Descent (R) transforms Shyvana, granting bonus health, an unstoppable dash, and empowered basic abilities. Land the dash to pin Kha'Zix against a wall, then follow with an empowered Molten Burst (E) to finish the duel before Void Assault lets him disengage. This is a major power spike that turns you into a map-wide teamfight threat."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Spear of Shojin / Titanic Spike","why":"Completing your core item triggers a massive damage, health, and wave clear multiplier that completely overpowers traditional brawlers or isolated targets. Kha'Zix's first item plus the first evolution is the spike — an isolated squishy stops surviving the opener."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Juggernaut","why":"Shyvana transitions into a high-impact skirmish diver and juggernaut who relies on generating Fury and landing Dragon's Descent initiations. Your late-game damage leans on Dragon Form's empowered abilities, so coordinate with your team before diving for burst damage. Around high-tier objective pits, use Molten Burst (E)'s slow to cleanly win the smite duel against Kha'Zix and secure the objective."}],"start":"Start your clear at the Raptor or Red Buff camp, using Emberstrike (Q) to strike the camp and its surrounding area and Inferno Aegis (W) for the shield and movement speed to keep the path fast. Kha'Zix clears well with Void Spike healing him through it, and he reaches three without spending much health.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using Molten Burst (E) to slow it and speed up the kill, establishing river control. You can solo early Dragons behind Scalemail's defensive bonuses if Kha'Zix is visibly committed to a top-lane gank. Kha'Zix contests scuttle happily if you are alone at it, because the isolation bonus turns the crab into a kill.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the moment they spawn, using Emberstrike (Q) to clear them rapidly — its true damage and recast make it the stronger objective-race tool. Use Molten Burst (E) to slow and run down Kha'Zix if he tries to contest from stealth. Kha'Zix has strong single-target objective damage but a poor pit fight against anyone with crowd control.","invade":"Walk boldly into Kha'Zix's jungle the moment you confirm they are engaged with a low-health camp. Lead with Molten Burst (E) to slow them, then pop Inferno Aegis (W) for the shield and delayed detonation to force a high-damage melee duel. Kha'Zix escapes an invade with Leap and, once evolved, with the ultimate's stealth, so cut the escape angle first.","watch":"Watch for Kha'Zix using Unseen Threat and Leap (E) to isolate and burst you out of stealth while you're mid-camp, especially if you've already burned Inferno Aegis (W) and lack its shield for the trade. Isolation is the whole thing — near a camp or an ally his Q does far less, so never face-check alone.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using wards to safely trace Kha'Zix's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming to reach your item and Dragon's Descent power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable frontline threat. Kha'Zix is weakest in a grouped fight, where nothing is isolated and his burst has no preferred target.","split":"You can split push exceptionally well due to your massive wave clear, pulling enemies to side lanes while you generate Fury. In teamfights, you must be the primary engager, utilizing Dragon's Descent to dive straight into the enemy formation and unleash your AoE damage. Kha'Zix side lanes to look for a lone answer rather than to push, so the wave is bait as often as not.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies by launching your Dragon's Descent over walls to secure high-priority backline access. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely burning their frontline tanks and instantly shredding their backline carriers with empowered Molten Burst. Kha'Zix picks are an evolved leap onto whoever walked a corridor alone, which is why vision on side entrances matters.","win":"Generate an unanswerable early-to-mid game gold lead through successful power farming and absolute maximum dragon control. Close the match by relentlessly driving through teamfights in dragon form, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable juggernaut before they can scale their defensive items. Kha'Zix wins by finding an isolated target every fight and deleting it before the fight is joined."},"Kindred":{"tldr":"Kindred adjusts your early tempo by requiring precise use of Molten Burst (E) slows and Inferno Aegis (W) uptime against their ranged poke. Shyvana commands skirmishes through power farming, using Emberstrike (Q) for auto-attack resets and Dragon's Descent (R) for area-of-effect dives once she has Fury. Kindred seeks to kite and poke you down with Dance of Arrows (Q) and Mounting Dread (E) before you can close the distance.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Shyvana Favored","why":"Start with Emberstrike (Q) to strike the camp and the area around it, and use Inferno Aegis (W) for its shield and movement speed to stay healthy while clearing. This establishes a fast, healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Shyvana Dominant","why":"Emberstrike's (Q) auto-attack reset drastically accelerates your single-target clear and dueling potential. If Kindred attempts an early entry into your jungle, use Inferno Aegis (W) for the shield and speed to close the gap before their kiting Dance of Arrows (Q) can whittle you down, then chain Emberstrike (Q) resets to shatter their health bar. This secures your early map pressure and punishes poor invades."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Shyvana Absolute Peak","why":"Access to Molten Burst (E) completes your dueling kit: a fireball that explodes on large targets and slows them. Use it to close the gap on Kindred before they can kite with Dance of Arrows (Q), or to accelerate your clear against high-health monsters. This gives you the tools to turn most early brawls if you land the slow."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early farming thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive power-farming and dragon rushing. Keep constant tabs on Kindred's jungle clearing lines and only intercept them if you have a clear advantage or counter-gank opportunity. Prioritize reaching your level 6 power spike over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Dragon's Descent Domination","why":"Dragon's Descent (R) transforms Shyvana, granting bonus health, an unstoppable dash, and empowered basic abilities. Land the dash to pin Kindred against a wall before they can kite, then follow with an empowered Molten Burst (E) to finish the duel. This is a major power spike that turns you into a map-wide teamfight threat."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Spear of Shojin / Titanic Spike","why":"Completing your core item triggers a massive damage, health, and wave clear multiplier that completely overpowers traditional brawlers or isolated targets. Kindred's first item plus a few stacks is where the ranged trade stops being poke and starts being a duel they win."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Juggernaut","why":"Shyvana transitions into a high-impact skirmish diver and juggernaut who relies on generating Fury and landing Dragon's Descent initiations. Your late-game damage leans on Dragon Form's empowered abilities, so coordinate with your team before diving for burst damage — watch for Lamb's Respite (R) neutralizing a pick attempt. Around high-tier objective pits, use Molten Burst (E)'s slow to cleanly win the smite duel against Kindred and secure the objective."}],"start":"Start your clear at the Raptor or Red Buff camp, using Emberstrike (Q) to strike the camp and its surrounding area and Inferno Aegis (W) for the shield and movement speed to keep the path fast. Kindred clears fast at range with Dance of Arrows and Wolf's Frenzy, and takes almost no damage doing it.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using Molten Burst (E) to slow it and speed up the kill, establishing river control. You can solo early Dragons behind Scalemail's defensive bonuses if Kindred is visibly committed to a top-lane gank. Kindred wins scuttle contests by kiting rather than fighting — three dashes banked means the crab is theirs.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the moment they spawn, using Emberstrike (Q) to clear them rapidly. Use Molten Burst (E) to slow and run down Kindred if they try to contest, punishing their lack of hard engage. Kindred takes objectives quickly with the passive attack speed and can hold a pit with Lamb's Respite.","invade":"Walk boldly into Kindred's jungle the moment you confirm they are engaged with a low-health camp. Lead with Molten Burst (E) to slow them, then pop Inferno Aegis (W) for the shield and delayed detonation to force a high-damage melee duel before they can kite. Kindred is hard to catch in an invade, since every Q is a dash and the jungle terrain works in their favour.","watch":"Watch for sudden counter-invades if you burn Inferno Aegis (W) early while clearing a camp, leaving you without its shield and movement speed against Kindred's ranged poke. This leaves you without your main defensive tool to cleanly turn a fight around. Watch the mark: a live mark on your side of the map says exactly where Kindred is going next.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using wards to safely trace Kindred's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming to reach your item and Dragon's Descent power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable frontline threat. Kindred is weakest with no marks stacked and no dashes banked, which is when their damage is simply low.","split":"You can split push exceptionally well due to your massive wave clear, pulling enemies to side lanes while you generate Fury. In teamfights, you must be the primary engager, utilizing Dragon's Descent to dive straight into the enemy formation and unleash your AoE damage. Kindred side lanes safely because the kiting pattern beats anything that has to walk at them.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies by launching your Dragon's Descent over walls to secure high-priority backline access. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely burning their frontline tanks and instantly shredding their backline carriers with empowered Molten Burst. Kindred does not pick so much as collect, so the danger is chasing them into a Lamb's Respite you cannot leave.","win":"Generate an unanswerable early-to-mid game gold lead through successful power farming and absolute maximum dragon control. Close the match by relentlessly driving through teamfights in dragon form, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable juggernaut before they can scale their defensive items. Kindred wins by stacking marks early and turning every late fight into a kite their team fights inside."},"Lee Sin":{"tldr":"Lee Sin adjusts your early tempo by requiring precise use of Molten Burst (E) slows and Inferno Aegis (W) uptime against his mobility. Shyvana commands skirmishes through power farming, using Emberstrike (Q) for auto-attack resets and Dragon's Descent (R) for area-of-effect dives once she has Fury. Lee Sin seeks to out-gank your early farming tempo with Sonic Wave/Resonating Strike (Q) and kite your approach before you build Fury, exploiting your lack of hard crowd control in human form.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Shyvana Favored","why":"Start with Emberstrike (Q) to strike the camp and the area around it, and use Inferno Aegis (W) for its shield and movement speed to stay healthy while clearing. This establishes a fast, healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Shyvana Dominant","why":"Unlocking Emberstrike (Q) provides an impactful auto-attack reset that drastically accelerates your single-target clear and dueling potential. If Lee Sin attempts an uncalculated early entry into your jungle, activate Inferno Aegis to stick to them before taking an aggressive close-quarters duel using your Q reset to shatter their health bar. This completely secures your early map pressure and punishes poor invades."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Shyvana Absolute Peak","why":"Access to Molten Burst (E) completes your dueling kit: a fireball that explodes on large targets and slows them. Use it to prevent Lee Sin from disengaging with Sonic Wave (Q) or Safeguard (W), or to accelerate your clear against high-health monsters. This gives you the tools to turn most early brawls if you land the slow."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early farming thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive power-farming and dragon rushing. Keep constant tabs on Lee Sin's jungle clearing lines and only intercept them if you have a clear advantage or counter-gank opportunity. Prioritize reaching your level 6 power spike over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Dragon's Descent Domination","why":"Dragon's Descent (R) transforms Shyvana, granting bonus health, an unstoppable dash, and empowered basic abilities. Land the dash to pin Lee Sin against a wall, then follow with an empowered Molten Burst (E) to finish the duel before he can Sonic Wave (Q) away. This is a major power spike that turns you into a map-wide teamfight threat."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Spear of Shojin / Titanic Spike","why":"Completing your core item triggers a massive damage, health, and wave clear multiplier that completely overpowers traditional brawlers or isolated targets. Lee Sin's first item is his last real jump — after it his damage stays flat while everyone else keeps climbing."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Juggernaut","why":"Shyvana transitions into a high-impact skirmish diver and juggernaut who relies on generating Fury and landing Dragon's Descent initiations. Your late-game damage leans on Dragon Form's empowered abilities, so coordinate with your team before diving for burst damage — Lee Sin's Dragon's Rage (R) can knock you out of position. Around high-tier objective pits, use Molten Burst (E)'s slow to cleanly win the smite duel and secure the objective."}],"start":"Start your clear at the Raptor or Red Buff camp, using Emberstrike (Q) to strike the camp and its surrounding area and Inferno Aegis (W) for the shield and movement speed to keep the path fast. Lee Sin clears quickly with Flurry weaving and arrives at three with a full energy bar and a plan.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using Molten Burst (E) to slow it and speed up the kill, establishing river control. You can solo early Dragons behind Scalemail's defensive bonuses if Lee Sin is visibly committed to a top-lane gank. Lee Sin fights for every scuttle — the Q re-cast plus Flurry resets are at their strongest at exactly that timer.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the moment they spawn, using Emberstrike (Q) to clear them rapidly. Use Molten Burst (E) to slow and run down Lee Sin if he tries to contest, catching him off-guard mid-clear. Lee Sin is a strong early objective contester because Dragon's Rage can kick you out of the pit entirely.","invade":"Walk boldly into Lee Sin's jungle the moment you confirm they are engaged with a low-health camp. Lead with your Molten Burst to mark them, popping Inferno Aegis to secure an inescapable high-damage melee duel. Invade Lee Sin with his energy spent, because ward-hops let him reach — and leave — camps you did not expect him at.","watch":"Watch for sudden counter-invades if you burn Inferno Aegis (W) early while clearing a camp, leaving you without its shield and movement speed against Lee Sin's Dragon's Rage (R) kick. This leaves you without your main defensive tool to cleanly turn a fight around. Everything starts with Sonic Wave — stand behind a camp so it cannot connect and half his kit is idle.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using wards to safely trace Lee Sin's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming to reach your item and Dragon's Descent power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable frontline threat. Lee Sin is weakest from two items on, when his burst stops killing and his utility is all that is left.","split":"You can split push exceptionally well due to your massive wave clear, pulling enemies to side lanes while you generate Fury. In teamfights, you must be the primary engager, utilizing Dragon's Descent to dive straight into the enemy formation and unleash your AoE damage. Lee Sin side lanes poorly late; he wants the mid game, where a kick decides a fight before it starts.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies by launching your Dragon's Descent over walls to secure high-priority backline access. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely burning their frontline tanks and instantly shredding their backline carriers with empowered Molten Burst. Lee Sin picks are a Q from fog into a kick that separates you from your team, so hold your dash for the second hit.","win":"Generate an unanswerable early-to-mid game gold lead through successful power farming and absolute maximum dragon control. Close the match by relentlessly driving through teamfights in dragon form, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable juggernaut before they can scale their defensive items. Lee Sin wins by converting an early lead into objectives before the two-item mark takes his damage away."},"Naafiri":{"tldr":"Naafiri can out-poke and out-skirmish you early with Darkin Daggers (Q) bleed and her pack, so play around Inferno Aegis (W)'s shield and Molten Burst (E)'s slow rather than forcing extended trades. Shyvana out-scales the fight through Fury-fueled clearing and Emberstrike (Q) auto-attack resets, then Dragon's Descent (R) turns her into a real dive threat. Naafiri looks to out-gank your early tempo, kite you before you hit your power spikes, and exploit your lack of hard CC outside Dragon Form.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Shyvana Favored","why":"Start with Inferno Aegis (W) for the shield, movement speed, and delayed explosion to get through the first camp quickly and safely. This gives you a fast, healthy path through your starting camps while Fury builds toward Emberstrike (Q)."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Shyvana Dominant","why":"Unlocking Emberstrike (Q) gives you an auto-attack reset that speeds up single-target clears and dueling. If Naafiri makes an uncalculated early entry into your jungle, use Inferno Aegis (W) to stick to her before taking the duel and resetting your attacks with Q to shred her health bar. This backs up your early map pressure and punishes poor invades."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Shyvana Absolute Peak","why":"Access to Molten Burst (E) rounds out your dueling kit with a slowing fireball that explodes on large targets, letting you kite a melee duelist like Naafiri while chipping her down or speeding through big monster camps. Landing the slow lets you turn most early brawls in your favor."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early farming thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive power-farming and dragon rushing. Keep constant tabs on Naafiri's jungle clearing lines and only intercept them if you have a clear advantage or counter-gank opportunity. Prioritize reaching your level 6 power spike over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Dragon's Descent Domination","why":"Dragon's Descent (R) transforms you into a Dragon, granting bonus health, an unstoppable dash, and empowered basic abilities. Leap onto Naafiri with it and follow up with empowered Molten Burst (E) for a fight-ending burst. This spike turns you into a real teamfight threat who can dictate the shape of a fight through raw damage."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Spear of Shojin / Titanic Spike","why":"Completing your core item triggers a massive damage, health, and wave clear multiplier that completely overpowers traditional brawlers or isolated targets. Naafiri's first item is where the dagger bleed plus the pack becomes a full health bar rather than a heavy trade."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Juggernaut","why":"Shyvana becomes a high-impact skirmish diver who relies on generating Fury and landing Dragon's Descent initiations. Her late-game damage scaling is AoE-focused, so coordinate with your team before diving in. Use Molten Burst's slow to control the contesting jungler around objective pits and secure the takedown."}],"start":"Start your clear at the Raptor or Red Buff camp, using Inferno Aegis (W)'s shield and delayed detonation to get through camps quickly and safely. Naafiri clears fast because the packmates fight the camp with her, and she reaches three with the pack still up.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using Molten Burst (E) to slow and chip it down for a clean contest. You can solo early Dragons safely behind your Scalemail passive resistances if Naafiri is confirmed away ganking top lane. Naafiri wins a scuttle contest on numbers — the dogs make a level-two or level-three crab fight a 3v1.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the moment they spawn, using Inferno Aegis (W) to clear them quickly. Use Molten Burst (E) to slow and punish Naafiri if she contests, catching her mid-clear. Naafiri has solid single-target objective damage but no way to hold a pit against a champion with a knock-up.","invade":"Walk into Naafiri's jungle once you confirm she is committed to a low-health camp. Lead with Molten Burst (E) to slow her, then use Inferno Aegis (W)'s shield and burst to win the duel. Naafiri answers an invade with Hounds' Pursuit, which is untargetable, so kill the packmates before you commit.","watch":"Watch for counter-invades if you burn Inferno Aegis (W) early while clearing a camp — you lose its shield and movement speed and have no other escape or engage tool until it's back up. Count the dogs — a Naafiri with no packmates up has lost most of her damage until they respawn.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using wards to safely trace Naafiri's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming to reach your item and Dragon's Descent power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable frontline threat. Naafiri is weakest with the pack dead, which is why clearing them first changes the fight more than any cooldown.","split":"You can split push exceptionally well due to your massive wave clear, pulling enemies to side lanes while you generate Fury. In teamfights, you must be the primary engager, utilizing Dragon's Descent to dive straight into the enemy formation and unleash your AoE damage. Naafiri side lanes fast and is a genuine 1v1 threat, but she gives up the fight around objectives to do it.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies by launching your Dragon's Descent over walls to secure high-priority backline access. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely burning their frontline tanks and instantly shredding their backline carriers with empowered Molten Burst. Naafiri picks are a W dash onto anyone she can already reach with a slow, so break the slow before the dash lands.","win":"Generate an unanswerable early-to-mid game gold lead through successful power farming and absolute maximum dragon control. Close the match by relentlessly driving through teamfights in dragon form, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable juggernaut before they can scale their defensive items. Naafiri wins by picking one target per fight and having the pack finish it before your team can rotate."},"Nidalee":{"tldr":"Nidalee can spear you from range and burst you with Cougar form before you reach her, so play around Inferno Aegis (W)'s shield and Molten Burst (E)'s slow rather than walking into her Javelin Toss (Q). Shyvana out-scales the fight through Fury-fueled clearing and Emberstrike (Q) auto-attack resets, then Dragon's Descent (R) turns her into a real dive threat. Nidalee looks to out-gank your early tempo, poke and kite you before you hit your power spikes, and exploit your lack of hard CC outside Dragon Form.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Shyvana Favored","why":"Start with Inferno Aegis (W) for the shield, movement speed, and delayed explosion to get through the first camp quickly and safely. Nidalee's own Javelin Toss and auto-reset also clear fast, so this is a solid start rather than a clean win — Fury and Emberstrike (Q) still need to build."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Shyvana Dominant","why":"Unlocking Emberstrike (Q) gives you an auto-attack reset that speeds up single-target clears and dueling. If Nidalee makes an uncalculated early entry into your jungle, use Inferno Aegis (W) to stick to her before taking the duel and resetting your attacks with Q to shred her health bar — just be wary of her closing the gap with Javelin Toss first. This backs up your early map pressure and punishes poor invades."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Shyvana Absolute Peak","why":"Access to Molten Burst (E) rounds out your dueling kit with a slowing fireball that explodes on large targets, letting you close distance on a Cougar-form Nidalee while chipping her down or speeding through big monster camps. Landing the slow lets you turn most early brawls in your favor."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early farming thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive power-farming and dragon rushing. Keep constant tabs on Nidalee's jungle clearing lines and only intercept them if you have a clear advantage or counter-gank opportunity. Prioritize reaching your level 6 power spike over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Dragon's Descent Domination","why":"Dragon's Descent (R) transforms you into a Dragon, granting bonus health, an unstoppable dash, and empowered basic abilities. Leap onto Nidalee before she can disengage and follow up with empowered Molten Burst (E) for a fight-ending burst. This spike turns you into a real teamfight threat who can dictate the shape of a fight through raw damage."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Spear of Shojin / Titanic Spike","why":"Completing your core item triggers a massive damage, health, and wave clear multiplier that completely overpowers traditional brawlers or isolated targets. Nidalee's first item makes the spear a genuine execute threat rather than chip damage on the way to a fight."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Juggernaut","why":"Shyvana becomes a high-impact skirmish diver who relies on generating Fury and landing Dragon's Descent initiations. Her late-game damage scaling is AoE-focused, so coordinate with your team before diving in — Nidalee can still poke you down from range before you close. Use Molten Burst's slow to control the contesting jungler around objective pits and secure the takedown."}],"start":"Start your clear at the Raptor or Red Buff camp, using Inferno Aegis (W)'s shield and delayed detonation to get through camps quickly and safely. Nidalee clears extremely fast in cougar form with pounce resets, and she is usually the first jungler to level three.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using Molten Burst (E) to slow and chip it down for a clean contest. You can solo early Dragons safely behind your Scalemail passive resistances if Nidalee is confirmed away ganking top lane. Nidalee decides scuttle contests with a max-range Javelin before either of you is standing on the crab.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the moment they spawn, using Inferno Aegis (W) to clear them quickly. Use Molten Burst (E) to slow and punish Nidalee if she contests, catching her mid-clear. Nidalee has excellent objective damage from the hunt bonus but cannot hold a pit against a real duellist.","invade":"Walk into Nidalee's jungle once you confirm she is committed to a low-health camp — watch for Javelin Toss (Q) poke on the way in. Lead with Molten Burst (E) to slow her, then use Inferno Aegis (W)'s shield and burst to close the duel before she can kite you as a cougar. Nidalee cannot be caught in an invade — the pounce reset means she leaves through terrain you cannot follow.","watch":"Watch for counter-invades if you burn Inferno Aegis (W) early while clearing a camp — you lose its shield and movement speed and have no other escape or engage tool until it's back up, which is exactly when a Cougar-form Nidalee punishes you. The Javelin is the tell: a landed spear means the pounce is coming, so break line of sight instead of walking back.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using wards to safely trace Nidalee's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming to reach your item and Dragon's Descent power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable frontline threat. Nidalee is weakest at close range with her cooldowns spent, where a cougar with no pounce has to fight honestly.","split":"You can split push exceptionally well due to your massive wave clear, pulling enemies to side lanes while you generate Fury. In teamfights, you must be the primary engager, utilizing Dragon's Descent to dive straight into the enemy formation and unleash your AoE damage. Nidalee does not hold a side lane; she poke-clears it and leaves before anyone can arrive.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies by launching your Dragon's Descent over walls to secure high-priority backline access. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely burning their frontline tanks and instantly shredding their backline carriers with empowered Molten Burst. Nidalee picks are a spear out of fog followed by a pounce, which is why the spear line matters more than her position.","win":"Generate an unanswerable early-to-mid game gold lead through successful power farming and absolute maximum dragon control. Close the match by relentlessly driving through teamfights in dragon form, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable juggernaut before they can scale their defensive items. Nidalee wins by poking a target below the hunt threshold and executing it before the fight begins properly."},"Nocturne":{"tldr":"Nocturne matches your skirmish power early with Umbra Blades sustain and can fear you down with Unspeakable Horror (E) once he's ahead, so respect Inferno Aegis (W)'s shield and Molten Burst (E)'s slow rather than trading blind. Shyvana out-scales the fight through Fury-fueled clearing and Emberstrike (Q) auto-attack resets, then Dragon's Descent (R) turns her into a real dive threat. Nocturne looks to snowball off ganks and Paranoia (R) global pressure before you hit your power spikes.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Shyvana Favored","why":"Start with Inferno Aegis (W) for the shield, movement speed, and delayed explosion to get through the first camp quickly and safely. This gives you a fast, healthy path through your starting camps while Fury builds toward Emberstrike (Q)."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Shyvana Dominant","why":"Unlocking Emberstrike (Q) gives you an auto-attack reset that speeds up single-target clears and dueling. If Nocturne makes an uncalculated early entry into your jungle, use Inferno Aegis (W) to stick to him before taking the duel and resetting your attacks with Q to shred his health bar through his Umbra Blades sustain. This backs up your early map pressure and punishes poor invades."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Shyvana Absolute Peak","why":"Access to Molten Burst (E) rounds out your dueling kit with a slowing fireball that explodes on large targets, letting you shut down Nocturne's Duskbringer speed boost or speed through high-health monsters. Landing the slow lets you turn most early brawls in your favor."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early farming thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive power-farming and dragon rushing. Keep constant tabs on Nocturne's jungle clearing lines and only intercept them if you have a clear advantage or counter-gank opportunity. Prioritize reaching your level 6 power spike over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Dragon's Descent Domination","why":"Dragon's Descent (R) transforms you into a Dragon, granting bonus health, an unstoppable dash, and empowered basic abilities, giving you a fear-on-path engage of your own. Follow it up with empowered Molten Burst (E) for a fight-ending burst. Nocturne's own Paranoia (R) is a real global engage tool too, so this is a strong spike for you rather than a clean sweep."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Spear of Shojin / Titanic Spike","why":"Completing your core item triggers a massive damage, health, and wave clear multiplier that completely overpowers traditional brawlers or isolated targets. Nocturne's first item is where the Duskbringer trade stops being a chunk and becomes a kill on any squishy."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Juggernaut","why":"Shyvana becomes a high-impact skirmish diver who relies on generating Fury and landing Dragon's Descent initiations. Her late-game damage scaling is AoE-focused, so coordinate with your team before diving in. Use Molten Burst's slow to control the contesting jungler around objective pits and secure the takedown."}],"start":"Start your clear at the Raptor or Red Buff camp, using Inferno Aegis (W)'s shield and delayed detonation to get through camps quickly and safely. Nocturne clears fast off Umbra Blades sustain and Duskbringer, and he is at full health almost every camp.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using Molten Burst (E) to slow and chip it down for a clean contest. You can solo early Dragons safely behind your Scalemail passive resistances if Nocturne is confirmed away ganking top lane. Nocturne contests scuttle on the Duskbringer trail, where the movement speed and the fear make the trade one-sided.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the moment they spawn, using Inferno Aegis (W) to clear them quickly. Use Molten Burst (E) to slow and punish Nocturne if he contests, catching him mid-clear. Nocturne is a good single-target objective taker with the passive heal but has no zone control in the pit.","invade":"Walk boldly into Nocturne's jungle the moment you confirm they are engaged with a low-health camp. Lead with your Molten Burst to mark them, popping Inferno Aegis to secure an inescapable high-damage melee duel. Nocturne's spell shield makes an invade awkward — bait it with a low-value ability before committing the real one.","watch":"Watch for counter-invades if you burn Inferno Aegis (W) early while clearing a camp — you lose its shield and movement speed and have no other escape or engage tool until it's back up, which is when Nocturne's Duskbringer (Q) speed boost lets him run you down. Watch the spell shield: while it is up your engage is refunded, and while it is down he is an ordinary melee.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using wards to safely trace Nocturne's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming to reach your item and Dragon's Descent power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable frontline threat. Nocturne is weakest against a team that stays grouped, since the fear tether breaks on distance and needs isolation.","split":"You can split push exceptionally well due to your massive wave clear, pulling enemies to side lanes while you generate Fury. In teamfights, you must be the primary engager, utilizing Dragon's Descent to dive straight into the enemy formation and unleash your AoE damage. Nocturne side lanes well and threatens to leave it at any moment, because his ultimate reaches the whole map.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies by launching your Dragon's Descent over walls to secure high-priority backline access. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely burning their frontline tanks and instantly shredding their backline carriers with empowered Molten Burst. Nocturne picks are a Paranoia dive onto someone he has already chunked, so hug terrain near allies on the cast.","win":"Generate an unanswerable early-to-mid game gold lead through successful power farming and absolute maximum dragon control. Close the match by relentlessly driving through teamfights in dragon form, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable juggernaut before they can scale their defensive items. Nocturne wins by darkening the map and arriving on the one person your team cannot afford to lose."},"Rengar":{"tldr":"Rengar adjusts your early mechanical tempo by requiring high precision over Molten Burst (E) slows and Inferno Aegis (W) shield timing. Shyvana commands skirmish velocities through Emberstrike (Q) auto-attack resets and true-damage bursts, deploying Dragon's Descent (R) to force area-of-effect fights once she has Fury. Rengar seeks to out-gank your early farming tempo from brush with Unseen Predator, kite your approach with Bola Strike, and exploit your lack of hard crowd control before Dragon Form.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Shyvana Favored","why":"Shyvana's Emberstrike (Q) hits the target and the surrounding area with true damage on her next attack, giving her a real area-of-effect clear tool at level 1, while Rengar's Savagery (Q) is single-target only and just generates Ferocity. Inferno Aegis (W) also gives Shyvana a shield to soak camp damage that Rengar has no early answer to. That's a kit-grounded clear-speed edge for Shyvana, not Rengar."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Shyvana Dominant","why":"Unlocking the recast on Emberstrike (Q) provides an auto-attack reset that accelerates your single-target clear and dueling potential. If Rengar attempts an uncalculated early entry into your jungle, activate Inferno Aegis (W) for the shield and speed to stick to him before taking an aggressive close-quarters duel using your Q reset to shatter his health bar. This secures your early map pressure and punishes poor invades."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Shyvana Absolute Peak","why":"Access to Molten Burst (E) completes your dueling kit by adding a fireball that explodes on large targets and slows them. Use it to lock down Rengar in a duel or burst through large monsters to accelerate your clear speed. Landing it turns most early brawls in your favor by denying his escape."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early farming thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive power-farming and dragon rushing. Keep constant tabs on Rengar's jungle clearing lines and only intercept them if you have a clear advantage or counter-gank opportunity. Prioritize reaching your level 6 power spike over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Dragon's Descent Domination","why":"Dragon's Descent (R) transforms Shyvana into a larger Dragon Form with empowered basic abilities and a dash that scatters enemies in her path. Land it to close on Rengar and follow with empowered Molten Burst, which now passes through and trails fire on large targets, for a hard-hitting duel loop. This is Shyvana's power spike; Rengar still has Thrill of the Hunt but loses the pure 1v1 once she has Dragon Form online."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Spear of Shojin / Titanic Spike","why":"Completing your core item triggers a massive damage, health, and wave clear multiplier that completely overpowers traditional brawlers or isolated targets. Rengar's first item is the point where an empowered Savagery stops chunking and starts one-shotting squishier targets."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Juggernaut","why":"Shyvana transitions into a high-impact skirmish diver and unstoppable juggernaut who relies entirely on generating Fury and landing massive Dragon's Descent initiations. Your late-game raw damage scaling shifts into a purely AoE-focused line, making it imperative to coordinate with your team before diving into extreme burst damage. Maintain extreme technical precision around high-tier objective pits to cleanly melt the contesting smiter with your E mark and secure the objective."}],"start":"Start your clear at the Raptor or Red Buff camp, using Inferno Aegis (W) for its shield and burst explosion against grouped monsters to keep your clear healthy. Rengar clears well off Battle Roar healing and Savagery resets, and he arrives at three with ferocity ready to spend.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using Molten Burst (E) to slow it and drop its health bar for river control. You can solo early Dragons using your Scalemail passive resistance stacks if Rengar is visible ganking top lane. Rengar contests scuttle from a brush — a leap opener at that timer is usually a kill rather than a trade.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the exact moment they spawn, using Inferno Aegis (W) to shield through their damage while you clear. Use Molten Burst (E) to slow and run down Rengar if he dares to contest, catching him off-guard mid-clear. Rengar has good single-target objective damage but nothing that holds a pit if the fight goes long.","invade":"Walk boldly into Rengar's jungle the moment you confirm he is engaged with a low-health camp. Lead with Molten Burst to slow him, popping Inferno Aegis for the shield and movement speed to close an inescapable melee duel. Rengar invades through brush lines, so the bushes on your side of the jungle are the ones that matter.","watch":"Watch for sudden counter-invades if you exhaust Inferno Aegis early while clearing a camp, leaving you without its shield and movement speed. This leaves you without your primary defensive and gap-closing tool right as Rengar can leap from brush with Unseen Predator. The ferocity count is the tell — at four stacks the next ability is the empowered one, and that is the burst.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using wards to safely trace Rengar's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming to reach your item and Dragon's Descent power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable frontline threat. Rengar is weakest in the middle of an open corridor, where there is no bush to open from and no reset to chain.","split":"You can split push exceptionally well due to your massive wave clear, pulling enemies to side lanes while you generate Fury. In teamfights, you must be the primary engager, utilizing Dragon's Descent to dive straight into the enemy formation and unleash your AoE damage. Rengar side lanes to hunt whoever answers rather than to push, so treat the wave as bait until proven otherwise.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies by launching your Dragon's Descent over walls to secure high-priority backline access. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, burning through their frontline tanks and shredding their backline carriers with empowered Molten Burst in Dragon Form. Rengar picks are a stealth leap out of Thrill of the Hunt, so control wards do more for you than health does.","win":"Generate an unanswerable early-to-mid game gold lead through successful power farming and absolute maximum dragon control. Close the match by relentlessly driving through teamfights in dragon form, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable juggernaut before they can scale their defensive items. Rengar wins by removing a squishy at the start of every fight from a bush your team did not sweep."},"Shaco":{"tldr":"Shaco adjusts your early mechanical tempo by requiring high precision over Molten Burst (E) slows and Inferno Aegis (W) shield timing. Shyvana commands skirmish velocities through Emberstrike (Q) auto-attack resets and true damage, deploying Dragon's Descent (R) to force fights once she has Fury. Shaco seeks to open with Deceive from stealth, plant Jack in the Boxes to fear you off camps, and pick off a low-health clear before you build Fury.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Shyvana Favored","why":"Start with Emberstrike (Q) for a true-damage attack that hits both the target and the surrounding area, and use Inferno Aegis (W) for its shield to absorb camp damage. This gives Shyvana a fast, healthy path across her starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Shyvana Dominant","why":"The recast on Emberstrike (Q) gives you an auto-attack reset that accelerates your single-target clear and dueling potential. If Shaco attempts an uncalculated early entry into your jungle, activate Inferno Aegis (W) for the shield and speed to stick to him before taking a close-quarters duel using your Q reset to shatter his health bar. This secures your early map pressure and punishes poor invades."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Shyvana Absolute Peak","why":"Access to Molten Burst (E) completes your dueling kit with a fireball that explodes on large targets and slows them. Use it to keep Shaco from disengaging on his Deceive cooldown, or burst through large monsters to accelerate your clear speed."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early farming thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive power-farming and dragon rushing. Keep constant tabs on Shaco's jungle clearing lines and only intercept them if you have a clear advantage or counter-gank opportunity. Prioritize reaching your level 6 power spike over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Shyvana Favored","why":"Dragon's Descent (R) is a real gap-closer that fears enemies in Shyvana's path and transforms her into Dragon Form, with true-damage Emberstrike and a slowing Molten Burst to chase Shaco down. Shaco's Hallucinate is a decoy and damage tool with no dash or hard CC of its own, so it can't contest Shyvana's ultimate directly — this breakpoint favors Shyvana."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Spear of Shojin / Titanic Spike","why":"Completing your core item triggers a massive damage, health, and wave clear multiplier that completely overpowers traditional brawlers or isolated targets. Shaco's first item is where the Deceive opener plus box damage becomes a one-rotation kill on squishier targets."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Juggernaut","why":"Shyvana transitions into a high-impact skirmish diver and unstoppable juggernaut who relies entirely on generating Fury and landing massive Dragon's Descent initiations. Your late-game raw damage scaling shifts into a purely AoE-focused line, making it imperative to coordinate with your team before diving into extreme burst damage. Maintain extreme technical precision around high-tier objective pits to cleanly melt the contesting smiter with your E mark and secure the objective."}],"start":"Start your clear at the Raptor or Red Buff camp, using Inferno Aegis (W) for its shield and burst explosion against grouped monsters to keep your clear healthy. Shaco clears very fast with boxes pre-placed, and the first clear is usually finished before you have seen him once.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using Molten Burst (E) to slow it and drop its health bar for river control. You can solo early Dragons using your Scalemail passive resistance stacks if Shaco is visible ganking top lane. Shaco contests scuttle with boxes already down, which makes the crab a 3v1 rather than a duel.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the exact moment they spawn, using Inferno Aegis (W) to shield through their damage while you clear. Use Molten Burst (E) to slow and run down Shaco if he dares to contest, catching him off-guard mid-clear. Shaco has strong single-target objective damage off Backstab but folds in a pit fight he cannot open from stealth.","invade":"Walk boldly into Shaco's jungle the moment you confirm he is engaged with a low-health camp. Lead with Molten Burst to slow him, popping Inferno Aegis for the shield and speed to close a melee duel before he can Deceive away. Shaco invades level one more than almost anyone, so the fear is the boxes at your buff rather than the champion.","watch":"Watch for sudden counter-invades if you exhaust Inferno Aegis early while clearing a camp, leaving you without its shield and speed right as Shaco can Deceive in from stealth for a backstab-boosted opening hit. Boxes are the map: a cleared box line tells you exactly which entrance he intends to fight at.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using wards to safely trace Shaco's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming to reach your item and Dragon's Descent power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable frontline threat. Shaco is weakest in an even fight with no boxes and no clone, which is the only version worth taking.","split":"You can split push exceptionally well due to your massive wave clear, pulling enemies to side lanes while you generate Fury. In teamfights, you must be the primary engager, utilizing Dragon's Descent to dive straight into the enemy formation and unleash your AoE damage. Shaco side lanes to threaten a backdoor rather than to duel, and the clone makes answering him a guess.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies by launching your Dragon's Descent over walls to secure high-priority backline access. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, burning through their frontline tanks and shredding their backline carriers with empowered Molten Burst in Dragon Form. Shaco picks are a Deceive from behind onto someone alone, so keep a wall at your back near his side of the map.","win":"Generate an unanswerable early-to-mid game gold lead through successful power farming and absolute maximum dragon control. Close the match by relentlessly driving through teamfights in dragon form, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable juggernaut before they can scale their defensive items. Shaco wins by making every second of the game confusing and cashing one clean pick per fight."},"Talon":{"tldr":"Talon adjusts your early mechanical tempo by requiring high precision over Molten Burst (E) slows and Inferno Aegis (W) shield timing. Shyvana commands skirmish velocities through Emberstrike (Q) auto-attack resets and true damage, deploying Dragon's Descent (R) to force fights once she has Fury. Talon seeks to leap in with Noxian Diplomacy, vault walls with Assassin's Path to gank from an unexpected angle, and burst you down before you build Fury — he has no hard CC of his own, so a clean duel favors whoever lands first.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Shyvana Favored","why":"Start with Emberstrike (Q) for a true-damage attack that hits both the target and the surrounding area, and use Inferno Aegis (W) for its shield to keep your clear healthy. Talon's kit has no AoE clear tool this early, giving Shyvana the faster opening."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Shyvana Dominant","why":"The recast on Emberstrike (Q) gives you an auto-attack reset that accelerates your single-target clear and dueling potential. If Talon attempts an uncalculated early entry into your jungle, activate Inferno Aegis (W) for the shield and speed to stick to him before taking a close-quarters duel using your Q reset to shatter his health bar. Talon has no hard CC to punish a bad trade this early, which favors Shyvana's sustained duel. This secures your early map pressure and punishes poor invades."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Shyvana Absolute Peak","why":"Access to Molten Burst (E) completes your dueling kit with a fireball that explodes on large targets and slows them. Use it to keep Talon from disengaging with Assassin's Path, or burst through large monsters to accelerate your clear speed."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early farming thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive power-farming and dragon rushing. Keep constant tabs on Talon's jungle clearing lines and only intercept them if you have a clear advantage or counter-gank opportunity. Prioritize reaching your level 6 power spike over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Dragon's Descent Domination","why":"Dragon's Descent (R) is a real gap-closer that fears enemies in Shyvana's path and transforms her into Dragon Form, with true-damage Emberstrike and a slowing Molten Burst to run Talon down. Talon's Shadow Assault is a stealth-and-damage tool with no hard CC or escape dash of its own once engaged, so once Shyvana closes with her ultimate, this breakpoint favors her."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Spear of Shojin / Titanic Spike","why":"Completing your core item triggers a massive damage, health, and wave clear multiplier that completely overpowers traditional brawlers or isolated targets. Talon's first item is the point where the bleed rotation goes from a heavy chunk to a full kill on a squishy."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Juggernaut","why":"Shyvana transitions into a high-impact skirmish diver and unstoppable juggernaut who relies entirely on generating Fury and landing massive Dragon's Descent initiations. Your late-game raw damage scaling shifts into a purely AoE-focused line, making it imperative to coordinate with your team before diving into extreme burst damage. Maintain extreme technical precision around high-tier objective pits to cleanly melt the contesting smiter with your E mark and secure the objective."}],"start":"Start your clear at the Raptor or Red Buff camp, using Inferno Aegis (W) for its shield and burst explosion against grouped monsters to keep your clear healthy. Talon clears fast with Rake and Noxian Diplomacy bleeds, and the wall-hops mean his path is shorter than yours.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using Molten Burst (E) to slow it and drop its health bar for river control. You can solo early Dragons using your Scalemail passive resistance stacks if Talon is visible ganking top lane. Talon contests scuttle by arriving over a wall you did not expect, and leaves the same way if it turns.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the exact moment they spawn, using Inferno Aegis (W) to shield through their damage while you clear. Use Molten Burst (E) to slow and run down Talon if he dares to contest, catching him off-guard mid-clear. Talon has decent single-target objective damage but no way to hold a pit against anyone with crowd control.","invade":"Walk boldly into Talon's jungle the moment you confirm he is engaged with a low-health camp. Lead with Molten Burst to slow him, popping Inferno Aegis for the shield and speed to close a melee duel before he can vault a wall with Assassin's Path. Talon cannot really be trapped — Assassin's Path takes him over terrain no chase can follow.","watch":"Watch for sudden counter-invades if you exhaust Inferno Aegis early while clearing a camp, leaving you without its shield and speed right as Talon can vault in over a wall with Assassin's Path or leap onto you with Noxian Diplomacy for a burst opening. Track his wall-hop cooldowns per wall: a wall he has just crossed is one he cannot cross again immediately.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using wards to safely trace Talon's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming to reach your item and Dragon's Descent power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable frontline threat. Talon is weakest in a grouped fight, where the burst has no isolated target and the stealth has nowhere to end.","split":"You can split push exceptionally well due to your massive wave clear, pulling enemies to side lanes while you generate Fury. In teamfights, you must be the primary engager, utilizing Dragon's Descent to dive straight into the enemy formation and unleash your AoE damage. Talon side lanes to find a solo kill and rotate over walls, so he is a threat to two lanes at once.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies by launching your Dragon's Descent over walls to secure high-priority backline access. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, burning through their frontline tanks and shredding their backline carriers with empowered Molten Burst in Dragon Form. Talon picks are a wall-hop into a full rotation on whoever is furthest forward, so never be that person.","win":"Generate an unanswerable early-to-mid game gold lead through successful power farming and absolute maximum dragon control. Close the match by relentlessly driving through teamfights in dragon form, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable juggernaut before they can scale their defensive items. Talon wins by removing an isolated carry and leaving over terrain before anyone can trade back."},"Viego":{"tldr":"Viego's real early threat is Spectral Maw (W), a dash that stuns the first thing it hits and is live from level 1 — that's his gank and his invade tool. Shyvana clears with Emberstrike (Q) resets and Inferno Aegis (W) AoE, and Molten Burst (E) slows him before he can close with Spectral Maw. His passive, Sovereign's Domination, lets him possess anything he kills, so avoid feeding him a body during a skirmish.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Shyvana Favored","why":"Start with Inferno Aegis (W) to secure a fast area-of-effect clear, utilizing the movement speed and continuous magic damage to melt the camp. Execute basic attacks to extend Inferno Aegis's duration, ensuring maximum efficiency. This establishes an exceptionally fast and healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Shyvana Dominant","why":"Unlocking Emberstrike (Q) provides an impactful auto-attack reset that drastically accelerates your single-target clear and dueling potential. If Viego attempts an uncalculated early entry into your jungle, activate Inferno Aegis to stick to them before taking an aggressive close-quarters duel using your Q reset to shatter their health bar. This completely secures your early map pressure and punishes poor invades."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Shyvana Absolute Peak","why":"Access to Molten Burst (E) completes your lethal dueling portfolio by introducing a versatile fireball that marks enemies for percent-health damage. Use this tool to instantly secure your damage trades or melt through high-health monsters to accelerate your clear speed. This grants an elite operational flexibility allowing you to turn almost any early brawl if you land your mark accurately."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early farming thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive power-farming and dragon rushing. Keep constant tabs on Viego's jungle clearing lines and only intercept them if you have a clear advantage or counter-gank opportunity. Prioritize reaching your level 6 power spike over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Dragon's Descent Domination","why":"Dragon's Descent (R) introduces an immense combat transformation that provides massive bonus health, an unstoppable dash, and empowered area-of-effect abilities. Execute a precise ultimate activation to drag Viego into a wall, unleashing your empowered Molten Burst for a clean kill. This massive mechanical spike turns you into an absolute map-wide teamfight threat who dictates the shape of every fight through raw damage."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Spear of Shojin / Titanic Spike","why":"Completing your core item triggers a massive damage, health, and wave clear multiplier that completely overpowers traditional brawlers or isolated targets. Viego's first item is where the sustained damage becomes a duel he wins outright rather than a trade he survives."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Juggernaut","why":"Shyvana transitions into a high-impact skirmish diver and unstoppable juggernaut who relies entirely on generating Fury and landing massive Dragon's Descent initiations. Your late-game raw damage scaling shifts into a purely AoE-focused line, making it imperative to coordinate with your team before diving into extreme burst damage. Maintain extreme technical precision around high-tier objective pits to cleanly melt the contesting smiter with your E mark and secure the objective."}],"start":"Start your clear at the Raptor or Red Buff camp, using Inferno Aegis's AoE detonation to speed up the clear. Viego clears fast off the Blade of the Ruined King passive and sustains through it, so his early health is never a problem.","scuttle":"Contest the scuttle crab as it spawns, but respect Spectral Maw's stun range — if Viego lands it during the crab fight he can follow into a clean kill. If he's committed to a top-lane gank, that's a window to solo an early Dragon behind Scalemail's stacked resistances. Viego contests scuttle with the Spectral Maw stun, and the crab fight is decided by whether that charge lands.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the moment they spawn; Inferno Aegis clears them quickly. If Viego contests, watch his Spectral Maw cooldown before committing — Molten Burst's slow helps you close if it's down. Viego is a good objective duellist with sustained damage but has no way to zone you off the pit.","invade":"Invade only when you can confirm Spectral Maw is down — it's his primary counter-engage. Lead with Molten Burst to slow him, then use Inferno Aegis's shield and speed to close out the trade. Viego escapes an invade through Harrowed Path, so the mist trails on the map are the routes he actually uses.","watch":"Watch for Spectral Maw the moment you engage — it's a dash-stun with no level gate, so Viego can punish an aggressive trade at any point in the game, not just after 6. Dodge the Maw charge-up — without the stun his early all-in is a set of ordinary auto-attacks.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, give up contested scuttle crabs and ward to trace Viego's routes — if he's picked up a kill he can possess that body and snowball fast. Prioritize farming to your first item and level 6 so Dragon's Descent's tankiness and true damage let you re-enter fights. Viego is weakest in a fight where nothing dies, since every reset he gets depends on a body being available.","split":"You can split push exceptionally well due to your massive wave clear, pulling enemies to side lanes while you generate Fury. In teamfights, you must be the primary engager, utilizing Dragon's Descent to dive straight into the enemy formation and unleash your AoE damage. Viego side lanes well and the possession makes answering him with one person risky if a wave is dying nearby.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies by launching your Dragon's Descent over walls to secure high-priority backline access. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely burning their frontline tanks and instantly shredding their backline carriers with empowered Molten Burst. Viego picks come out of the mist onto someone already low, so treat any mist trail as an occupied corridor.","win":"Build a gold and Dragon-control lead by out-clearing Viego and denying him easy possession targets in skirmishes. Close games by forcing fights in Dragon Form, where your true-damage Q and zone control outlast his single-target burst — and watch for Heartbreaker executing a low teammate mid-fight. Viego wins by turning one won fight into three through resets, which is why the first kill matters most."},"Wukong":{"tldr":"Wukong has no hard CC before level 6 — Warrior Trickster (W) is a stealth dash with a decoy clone, and Nimbus Strike (E) is a targeted dash that hits nearby enemies. His real threat is dueling stats: Crushing Blow (Q) shreds armor on-hit and resets his attack, and Stone Skin stacks armor and regen the longer a fight drags on. Shyvana answers with Emberstrike (Q) resets, Inferno Aegis (W) shield, and Molten Burst (E) to punish his dash-ins with a slow before Cyclone (R) becomes a real knockup threat at 6.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Shyvana Favored","why":"Start with Inferno Aegis (W) to secure a fast area-of-effect clear, utilizing the movement speed and continuous magic damage to melt the camp. Execute basic attacks to extend Inferno Aegis's duration, ensuring maximum efficiency. This establishes an exceptionally fast and healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Shyvana Dominant","why":"Unlocking Emberstrike (Q) provides an impactful auto-attack reset that drastically accelerates your single-target clear and dueling potential. If Wukong attempts an uncalculated early entry into your jungle, activate Inferno Aegis to stick to them before taking an aggressive close-quarters duel using your Q reset to shatter their health bar. This completely secures your early map pressure and punishes poor invades."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Shyvana Absolute Peak","why":"Access to Molten Burst (E) completes your lethal dueling portfolio by introducing a versatile fireball that marks enemies for percent-health damage. Use this tool to instantly secure your damage trades or melt through high-health monsters to accelerate your clear speed. This grants an elite operational flexibility allowing you to turn almost any early brawl if you land your mark accurately."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early farming thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive power-farming and dragon rushing. Keep constant tabs on Wukong's jungle clearing lines and only intercept them if you have a clear advantage or counter-gank opportunity. Prioritize reaching your level 6 power spike over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Dragon's Descent Domination","why":"Dragon's Descent (R) introduces an immense combat transformation that provides massive bonus health, an unstoppable dash, and empowered area-of-effect abilities. Execute a precise ultimate activation to drag Wukong into a wall, unleashing your empowered Molten Burst for a clean kill. This massive mechanical spike turns you into an absolute map-wide teamfight threat who dictates the shape of every fight through raw damage."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Spear of Shojin / Titanic Spike","why":"Completing your core item triggers a massive damage, health, and wave clear multiplier that completely overpowers traditional brawlers or isolated targets. Wukong's first item is where the armour shred plus Crushing Blow becomes a duel he wins rather than survives."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Juggernaut","why":"Shyvana transitions into a high-impact skirmish diver and unstoppable juggernaut who relies entirely on generating Fury and landing massive Dragon's Descent initiations. Your late-game raw damage scaling shifts into a purely AoE-focused line, making it imperative to coordinate with your team before diving into extreme burst damage. Maintain extreme technical precision around high-tier objective pits to cleanly melt the contesting smiter with your E mark and secure the objective."}],"start":"Start your clear at the Raptor or Red Buff camp, using Inferno Aegis's AoE detonation to speed up the clear. Wukong clears quickly with Crushing Blow and Nimbus Strike, and the armour shred keeps camps dying at a steady rate.","scuttle":"Contest the scuttle crab as it spawns; Molten Burst's slow punishes Wukong for using Nimbus Strike to close in. If he's committed to a top-lane gank, that's a window to solo an early Dragon behind Scalemail's stacked resistances. Wukong contests scuttle with a clone bluff as often as with damage — the crab is won on the guess, not the duel.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the moment they spawn; Inferno Aegis clears them quickly. If Wukong contests, watch for Warrior Trickster's stealth dash and decoy clone before committing to a duel. Wukong takes objectives at a fair pace and can knock you out of the pit twice with a double-cast ultimate.","invade":"Invade while Wukong is mid-camp before his Stone Skin stacks build up. Lead with Molten Burst to slow him, then use Inferno Aegis's shield and speed to close out the trade before he can Nimbus Strike away. Wukong escapes an invade by leaving a clone behind, so commit your key ability only after the real one is confirmed.","watch":"Watch for Warrior Trickster (W) counter-invades — the stealth dash plus decoy clone can mask which direction Wukong actually goes, so don't assume you know his position after he casts it. The clone walks in a straight line and does not react; the real Wukong is the one that changes direction.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, give up contested scuttle crabs and ward to trace Wukong's routes — his stealth dash makes blind fights risky. Prioritize farming to your first item and level 6 so Dragon's Descent's tankiness and true damage let you re-enter fights. Wukong is weakest with the clone spent, when he is a melee bruiser with one dash and no bluff left.","split":"You can split push exceptionally well due to your massive wave clear, pulling enemies to side lanes while you generate Fury. In teamfights, you must be the primary engager, utilizing Dragon's Descent to dive straight into the enemy formation and unleash your AoE damage. Wukong side lanes well and the clone makes a 1v1 answer into a coin flip you would rather not take.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies by launching your Dragon's Descent over walls to secure high-priority backline access. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely burning their frontline tanks and instantly shredding their backline carriers with empowered Molten Burst. Wukong picks are a Nimbus Strike into a double knock-up on a group, so spread before the spin rather than during.","win":"Build a gold and Dragon-control lead by out-clearing Wukong and avoiding drawn-out duels where Stone Skin's stacking regen catches up. Close games by forcing fights in Dragon Form, where your true-damage Q and zone control outlast his dash-heavy kit. Wukong wins by catching two or more people in the ultimate at the moment his team is ready to follow."},"Briar":{"tldr":"Briar wants to lock a duel down early with Head Rush (Q) and Blood Frenzy (W) stuns and out-sustain you through Crimson Curse's bleed lifesteal. Shyvana answers with Emberstrike (Q) auto-attack resets and the shield off Inferno Aegis (W), then closes with Dragon's Descent (R). Briar has no ranged poke, so she has to commit into melee to use any of her tools, which is exactly where your kit fights back hardest.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Shyvana Favored","why":"Open with Inferno Aegis (W) for the shield, movement speed, and delayed AoE explosion, your only point at level 1 but enough to clear a camp safely. Briar's single point in Head Rush or Blood Frenzy is a real gap-close threat but not enough to beat you outright in a level-1 duel. This establishes a fast, healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Shyvana Dominant","why":"Unlocking Emberstrike (Q) gives you an auto-attack reset that also hits nearby enemies, sharply raising your dueling damage. If Briar tries an early invade, Inferno Aegis's shield absorbs her Head Rush stun burst long enough for your Q resets to out-trade her bleed lifesteal. This secures your early map pressure and punishes poor invades."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Shyvana Absolute Peak","why":"Access to Molten Burst (E) adds a slowing fireball that explodes on large targets, letting you keep Briar in range once she's committed her Head Rush or Blood Frenzy dash. Landing the slow denies her the chance to reset the frenzy chase on someone healthier. This gives you the tools to win most early brawls you commit to."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early farming thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive power-farming and dragon rushing. Keep constant tabs on Briar's jungle clearing lines and only intercept them if you have a clear advantage or counter-gank opportunity. Prioritize reaching your level 6 power spike over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Dragon's Descent Domination","why":"Dragon's Descent (R) grants massive bonus health, an unstoppable dash, and empowered basic abilities. Landing the dash to pin Briar against a wall lets you follow up with empowered Molten Burst's fire trail before her Chilling Scream can knock you back. This spike turns you into a genuine map-wide skirmish and teamfight threat who can match her all-in commitment head-on."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Spear of Shojin / Titanic Spike","why":"Completing your core item triggers a massive damage, health, and wave clear multiplier that completely overpowers traditional brawlers or isolated targets. Briar's first item is a step change: the frenzy lifesteal starts out-healing full combos rather than just chip damage."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Juggernaut","why":"Shyvana transitions into a high-impact skirmish diver who relies on generating Fury and landing Dragon's Descent initiations. Your late-game damage scaling shifts into an AoE-focused line, so coordinate with your team rather than soloing a Briar who has committed her Certain Death ult onto someone else. Around objective pits, use Molten Burst's slow to keep the contesting smiter locked down before her frenzy closes the gap."}],"start":"Start at Red Buff or Raptors, using Inferno Aegis's shield and delayed explosion to clear safely at level 1. Track whether Briar shows near your side of the map before committing to a full clear, since Head Rush gives her a stunning gap-close into a low-health camp. Briar clears fast and healthy because Blood Frenzy heals her through the camps, so she is usually first to the river.","scuttle":"Contest the scuttle crab using Molten Burst (E) to slow it while you finish it off with Emberstrike (Q) resets. If Briar contests, expect Head Rush's stun the moment she's in range, so hold Inferno Aegis's shield to absorb it rather than burning it early. Contest scuttle against Briar with the health lead or not at all, because the frenzy lifesteal out-sustains most trades at that timer.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs, using Inferno Aegis's AoE explosion to clear the pack quickly. If Briar shows to contest, Molten Burst's slow lets you punish her before her Blood Frenzy pursuit gets going. Briar takes objectives fast but cannot walk away from one: once the W is spent she is locked into the fight.","invade":"Walk into Briar's jungle once you confirm she's committed to a low-health camp. Lead with Molten Burst to slow her, then pop Inferno Aegis for the shield and AoE explosion — she has no ranged answer to being caught before Head Rush is up. Invade Briar only with her frenzy on cooldown — Head Rush and the healing turn a fair 1v1 in her jungle into a kill for her.","watch":"Watch for Head Rush (Q) or Blood Frenzy (W) stunning you the moment Briar closes distance — both are dashes with hard CC attached, and Crimson Curse's bleed heals her back up fast if the fight drags out at low resources. Watch her frenzy state; while she is frenzied she cannot be told where to go, and that is the moment to leave.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using wards to safely trace Briar's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming to reach your item and Dragon's Descent power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable frontline threat. Briar is weakest when her frenzy is on cooldown and she has no health to heal back from a fight she cannot leave.","split":"You can split push exceptionally well due to your massive wave clear, pulling enemies to side lanes while you generate Fury. In teamfights, you must be the primary engager, utilizing Dragon's Descent to dive straight into the enemy formation and unleash your AoE damage. Briar side lanes well because the sustain holds a wave through poke, but she gives up the rest of the map to do it.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies by launching your Dragon's Descent over walls to secure high-priority backline access. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely burning their frontline tanks and instantly shredding their backline carriers with empowered Molten Burst. Briar picks are Certain Death from across the screen, so stay near a wall and a teammate while her ultimate is up.","win":"Generate an unanswerable early-to-mid game gold lead through successful power farming and absolute maximum dragon control. Close the match by relentlessly driving through teamfights in dragon form, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable juggernaut before they can scale their defensive items. Briar wins by starting a fight her team follows into and healing through the answer until the health bars flip."},"Jax":{"tldr":"Jax threatens with Counter Strike (E) dodge-into-stun and a Grandmaster-at-Arms (R) that scales his own late-game duels hard. Shyvana out-clears with Emberstrike (Q) resets and Inferno Aegis (W) shielding, and answers with Dragon's Descent (R) leap and fear into empowered Emberstrike true damage. Jax wants to bait out your engage before Counter Strike's dodge window, then punish you when it lands; Shyvana wants to force fights before his Grandmaster-at-Arms ramps up.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Shyvana Favored","why":"Open camps with Inferno Aegis (W) for its shield, movement speed, and delayed detonation, then extend with Emberstrike (Q) auto-resets once it's up. This establishes a fast, healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Shyvana Dominant","why":"Unlocking Emberstrike (Q) gives you an auto-attack reset that accelerates your single-target clear and dueling. Emberstrike relies on basic attacks, which Counter Strike hard-counters once Jax has it up, so time an early invade for before he's off cooldown rather than into an active dodge window."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Shyvana Absolute Peak","why":"Molten Burst (E) rounds out your kit as a slowing fireball that explodes on hitting a large target, giving you a way to close a duel or clear high-health monsters faster. It's a projectile, so save it for after Counter Strike's dodge window ends rather than feeding Jax free information."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early farming thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive power-farming and dragon rushing. Keep constant tabs on Jax's jungle clearing lines and only intercept them if you have a clear advantage or counter-gank opportunity. Prioritize reaching your level 6 power spike over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Grandmaster-at-Arms","why":"Jax's Grandmaster-at-Arms (R) also comes online at 6, adding every-third-hit magic damage on top of an active-use armor/MR buff, and his kit is built to win extended duels once it's up. Dragon's Descent still gives you bonus health, a dash, and empowered abilities, but you need to force the fight quickly with Molten Burst and Emberstrike rather than let a long trade play into Jax's ramping attack pattern."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Spear of Shojin / Titanic Spike","why":"Completing your core item triggers a massive damage, health, and wave clear multiplier that completely overpowers traditional brawlers or isolated targets. Jax's first item is a real threshold — the Empower-leap burst goes from a trade to a kill on squishier junglers."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Juggernaut","why":"Shyvana transitions into a high-impact skirmish diver who relies on generating Fury and landing Dragon's Descent initiations. Coordinate with your team before diving, since a scaled Jax can win an extended 1v1 off Grandmaster-at-Arms's stacking damage if the fight drags out. Around objective pits, use Molten Burst's slow and Emberstrike's true damage to close the smiter duel against Jax quickly rather than trading blow-for-blow."}],"start":"Start your clear at the Raptor or Red Buff camp, using Inferno Aegis (W)'s shield, movement speed, and delayed detonation to speed through camps. Jax clears well with Counter Strike eating camp attacks, and he arrives at three with health most junglers do not have.","scuttle":"Contest the scuttle crab with Emberstrike (Q) resets, but be ready to hold back once Counter Strike is up — dodging into it while stunned turns the fight against you. You can solo early Dragon using your passive Scalemail stacks if Jax is visible ganking top lane. Jax will take almost any scuttle contest, because the E stun turns a contested crab into a won duel.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the moment they spawn, using Inferno Aegis's detonation and Emberstrike resets to clear them rapidly. If Jax contests, watch for Leap Strike closing the distance followed by a Counter Strike stun. Jax is a slow objective taker early but a strong smite fighter, since the stun interrupts your attempt entirely.","invade":"Walk into Jax's jungle once you confirm he's committed to a low-health camp elsewhere. Lead with Inferno Aegis's shield, since Counter Strike can dodge your follow-up damage and stun you back if he catches the timing. Invade Jax between camps rather than at one, since a camp beside him feeds Counter Strike just as your own attacks do.","watch":"Watch for Counter Strike (E) — Jax dodges all incoming attacks for its duration and then stuns everyone around him, so committing your Emberstrike resets into an active Counter Strike window trades badly. Counter Strike has a two-second dodge window; the answer is to stop attacking and step out, not to trade through it.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, avoid contesting scuttle crabs where a Counter Strike stun can catch you overextending. Funnel into farming to reach your first item and Dragon's Descent power spike before forcing fights. Jax is weakest with his E down and no camp beside him, which is the only version of this fight worth taking.","split":"You can split push exceptionally well due to your massive wave clear, pulling enemies to side lanes while you generate Fury. In teamfights, you must be the primary engager, utilizing Dragon's Descent to dive straight into the enemy formation and unleash your AoE damage. Jax is the best side laner in the jungle at full build and will take a 1v2 on that wave if you let him.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies by launching your Dragon's Descent over walls to secure high-priority backline access. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely burning their frontline tanks and instantly shredding their backline carriers with empowered Molten Burst. Jax does not pick people, he waits for one to walk into range, so respect his leap distance around walls.","win":"Generate an unanswerable early-to-mid game gold lead through successful power farming and absolute maximum dragon control. Close the match by relentlessly driving through teamfights in dragon form, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable juggernaut before they can scale their defensive items. Jax wins by getting to full build and holding a side lane that costs you two people to answer."},"Master Yi":{"tldr":"Master Yi adjusts your early tempo by requiring precise use of Molten Burst (E) slows and Inferno Aegis (W) uptime against his Alpha Strike (Q) untargetability. Shyvana commands skirmishes through power farming, using Emberstrike (Q) for auto-attack resets and Dragon's Descent (R) for area-of-effect dives once she has Fury. Master Yi seeks to snowball camps into a Highlander (R) dive before you build Fury, exploiting your lack of hard crowd control in human form.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Shyvana Favored","why":"Start with Emberstrike (Q) to strike the camp and the area around it, and use Inferno Aegis (W) for its shield and movement speed to stay healthy while clearing. This establishes a fast, healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Shyvana Dominant","why":"Emberstrike's (Q) auto-attack reset drastically accelerates your single-target clear and dueling potential. If Master Yi attempts an early entry into your jungle, use Inferno Aegis (W) for the shield and speed to close the gap before Alpha Strike (Q) lets him disengage, then chain Emberstrike (Q) resets to shatter his health bar. This secures your early map pressure and punishes poor invades."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Shyvana Absolute Peak","why":"Access to Molten Burst (E) completes your dueling kit: a fireball that explodes on large targets and slows them. Use it to prevent Master Yi from resetting the fight with Alpha Strike (Q), or to accelerate your clear against high-health monsters. This gives you the tools to turn most early brawls if you land the slow."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early farming thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive power-farming and dragon rushing. Keep constant tabs on Master Yi's jungle clearing lines and only intercept them if you have a clear advantage or counter-gank opportunity. Prioritize reaching your level 6 power spike over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Dragon's Descent Domination","why":"Dragon's Descent (R) transforms Shyvana, granting bonus health, an unstoppable dash, and empowered basic abilities. Land the dash to pin Master Yi against a wall, then follow with an empowered Molten Burst (E) to finish the duel before Highlander (R) grants him slow immunity to escape. This is a major power spike that turns you into a map-wide teamfight threat."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Spear of Shojin / Titanic Spike","why":"Completing your core item triggers a massive damage, health, and wave clear multiplier that completely overpowers traditional brawlers or isolated targets. Master Yi's first item is the start of the curve rather than a spike — his real jump is the second and third."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Juggernaut","why":"Shyvana transitions into a high-impact skirmish diver and juggernaut who relies on generating Fury and landing Dragon's Descent initiations. Your late-game damage leans on Dragon Form's empowered abilities, so coordinate with your team before diving for burst damage and watch for a fed Master Yi's Highlander (R) reset. Around high-tier objective pits, use Molten Burst (E)'s slow to cleanly win the smite duel and secure the objective."}],"start":"Start your clear at the Raptor or Red Buff camp, using Emberstrike (Q) to strike the camp and its surrounding area and Inferno Aegis (W) for the shield and movement speed to keep the path fast. Master Yi clears very fast with Alpha Strike and Wuju Style, and he will be a level ahead if nobody interrupts him.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using Molten Burst (E) to slow it and speed up the kill, establishing river control. You can solo early Dragons behind Scalemail's defensive bonuses if Master Yi is visibly committed to a top-lane gank. Master Yi avoids scuttle contests before six — he takes the free crab and gives up the contested one.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the moment they spawn, using Emberstrike (Q) to clear them rapidly. Use Molten Burst (E) to slow and run down Master Yi if he tries to contest, catching him off-guard mid-clear. Master Yi has strong single-target objective damage but a weak pit fight, so contest his smites with crowd control.","invade":"Walk boldly into Master Yi's jungle the moment you confirm they are engaged with a low-health camp. Lead with Molten Burst (E) to slow them, then pop Inferno Aegis (W) for the shield and delayed detonation to force a high-damage melee duel before he can Alpha Strike (Q) away. Master Yi is one of the best invade targets in the game before six, since Meditate only delays the outcome.","watch":"Watch for sudden counter-invades if you burn Inferno Aegis (W) early while clearing a camp, leaving you without its shield and movement speed against a snowballed Master Yi. This leaves you without your main defensive tool to cleanly turn a fight around. Alpha Strike makes him untargetable for its duration; hold your key ability for after it ends, not during.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using wards to safely trace Master Yi's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming to reach your item and Dragon's Descent power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable frontline threat. Master Yi is weakest from one to five, and everything he does later depends on how badly that window went.","split":"You can split push exceptionally well due to your massive wave clear, pulling enemies to side lanes while you generate Fury. In teamfights, you must be the primary engager, utilizing Dragon's Descent to dive straight into the enemy formation and unleash your AoE damage. Master Yi side lanes at full build and answering him alone there is a mistake, however far ahead you are.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies by launching your Dragon's Descent over walls to secure high-priority backline access. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely burning their frontline tanks and instantly shredding their backline carriers with empowered Molten Burst. Master Yi does not pick; he cleans up, so the danger is a fight your team half-loses rather than an ambush.","win":"Generate an unanswerable early-to-mid game gold lead through successful power farming and absolute maximum dragon control. Close the match by relentlessly driving through teamfights in dragon form, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable juggernaut before they can scale their defensive items. Master Yi wins by farming to full build and then resetting Highlander through a fight nobody can chain-stun."},"Olaf":{"tldr":"Olaf's Reckless Swing (E) true damage and Ragnarok (R) CC-immunity make him a genuine duel threat once he's ahead, so play around Inferno Aegis (W)'s shield and Molten Burst (E)'s slow rather than trading blind. Shyvana's Fury-fueled clear speed and Emberstrike (Q) auto-attack resets let her out-farm and out-duel him early, and Dragon's Descent (R) turns her into a real dive threat at 6. Olaf looks to out-gank your early tempo and stack Undertow (Q) resets before you hit your power spikes; you have no hard CC of your own outside Dragon Form.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Shyvana Favored","why":"Start with Inferno Aegis (W) for the shield, movement speed, and delayed explosion to get through the first camp quickly and safely — a zero-cost, recastable Emberstrike (Q) on the way makes this one of the faster AoE-adjacent starts in the jungle, while Olaf's Undertow (Q) only resets on a successful axe pickup. This gives you a fast, healthy path through your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Shyvana Dominant","why":"Unlocking Emberstrike (Q) gives you an auto-attack reset that speeds up single-target clears and dueling. If Olaf makes an uncalculated early entry into your jungle, use Inferno Aegis (W) to stick to him before taking the duel and resetting your attacks with Q to shred his health bar. This backs up your early map pressure and punishes poor invades."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Shyvana Absolute Peak","why":"Access to Molten Burst (E) rounds out your dueling kit with a slowing fireball that explodes on large targets, letting you hold range on Olaf's Reckless Swing true damage or speed through high-health monsters. Landing the slow lets you turn most early brawls in your favor."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early farming thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive power-farming and dragon rushing. Keep constant tabs on Olaf's jungle clearing lines and only intercept them if you have a clear advantage or counter-gank opportunity. Prioritize reaching your level 6 power spike over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Dragon's Descent Domination","why":"Dragon's Descent (R) transforms you into a Dragon, granting bonus health, an unstoppable dash, and empowered basic abilities. Leap onto Olaf and follow up with empowered Molten Burst (E) for a fight-ending burst — but note Ragnarok (R) makes him immune to your fear-on-path CC while he's active. This spike still turns you into a real teamfight threat who can dictate the shape of a fight through raw damage."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Spear of Shojin / Titanic Spike","why":"Completing your core item triggers a massive damage, health, and wave clear multiplier that completely overpowers traditional brawlers or isolated targets. Olaf's first item is a large jump — the true damage plus lifesteal turns extended fights into ones he simply does not lose."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Juggernaut","why":"Shyvana becomes a high-impact skirmish diver who relies on generating Fury and landing Dragon's Descent initiations. Her late-game damage scaling is AoE-focused, so coordinate with your team before diving in — Olaf's Ragnarok still lets him fight through soft CC. Use Molten Burst's slow to control the contesting jungler around objective pits and secure the takedown."}],"start":"Start your clear at the Raptor or Red Buff camp, using Inferno Aegis (W)'s shield and delayed detonation to get through camps quickly and safely. Olaf clears very fast with Undertow resets and leaves camps healthier than he entered them, so he is always available to fight.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using Molten Burst (E) to slow and chip it down for a clean contest. You can solo early Dragons safely behind your Scalemail passive resistances if Olaf is confirmed away ganking top lane. Contest scuttle against Olaf only from full health, since his sustain and true damage beat most of the roster at that timer.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the moment they spawn, using Inferno Aegis (W) to clear them quickly. Use Molten Burst (E) to slow and punish Olaf if he contests, catching him mid-clear. Olaf is a strong objective duellist because Reckless Swing ignores resistances and his health stays high through the fight.","invade":"Walk into Olaf's jungle once you confirm he is committed to a low-health camp. Lead with Molten Burst (E) to slow him, then use Inferno Aegis (W)'s shield and burst to win the duel before his Reckless Swing (E) true damage and Berserker Rage sustain can turn it around. Invade Olaf above half health and never inside his slow, because a low-health 1v1 in his own jungle is the fight he wants.","watch":"Watch for counter-invades if you burn Inferno Aegis (W) early while clearing a camp — you lose its shield and movement speed and have no other escape or engage tool until it's back up, which is when Olaf's Undertow (Q) slow can pin you down. The axe on the ground is the tell — an Olaf who cannot pick it back up has a long cooldown and no slow.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using wards to safely trace Olaf's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming to reach your item and Dragon's Descent power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable frontline threat. Olaf is weakest against distance: he has no gap-closer, so a wall between you and the axe line takes the fight away.","split":"You can split push exceptionally well due to your massive wave clear, pulling enemies to side lanes while you generate Fury. In teamfights, you must be the primary engager, utilizing Dragon's Descent to dive straight into the enemy formation and unleash your AoE damage. Olaf side lanes strongly and takes any 1v1 there, but he cannot answer a fight on the other side of the map.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies by launching your Dragon's Descent over walls to secure high-priority backline access. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely burning their frontline tanks and instantly shredding their backline carriers with empowered Molten Burst. Olaf does not pick; he runs people down, so the danger is a slow landing rather than an ambush.","win":"Generate an unanswerable early-to-mid game gold lead through successful power farming and absolute maximum dragon control. Close the match by relentlessly driving through teamfights in dragon form, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable juggernaut before they can scale their defensive items. Olaf wins by invading, snowballing early, and diving through crowd control before anyone can scale past him."},"Qiyana":{"tldr":"Qiyana can lock you down with Elemental Wrath (Q) and dash in with Terrashape (W)/Audacity (E) for a burst all-in, so play around Inferno Aegis (W)'s shield and Molten Burst (E)'s slow rather than trading blind. Shyvana's Fury-fueled clear speed and Emberstrike (Q) auto-attack resets let her out-farm and out-duel Qiyana early, and Dragon's Descent (R) turns her into a real dive threat at 6. Qiyana looks to out-gank your early tempo and set up Supreme Display of Talent (R) before you hit your power spikes; you have no hard CC of your own outside Dragon Form.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Shyvana Favored","why":"Start with Inferno Aegis (W) for the shield, movement speed, and delayed explosion to get through the first camp quickly and safely — with every basic ability free to cast and Emberstrike (Q) hitting true damage once it's up, this is a genuinely fast, healthy start compared to Qiyana's mana-gated single-target Terrashape/Audacity combo."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Shyvana Dominant","why":"Unlocking Emberstrike (Q) gives you an auto-attack reset that speeds up single-target clears and dueling. If Qiyana makes an uncalculated early entry into your jungle, use Inferno Aegis (W) to stick to her before taking the duel and resetting your attacks with Q to shred her health bar. This backs up your early map pressure and punishes poor invades."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Shyvana Absolute Peak","why":"Access to Molten Burst (E) rounds out your dueling kit with a slowing fireball that explodes on large targets, letting you control Qiyana's dash range or speed through high-health monsters. Landing the slow lets you turn most early brawls in your favor."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early farming thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive power-farming and dragon rushing. Keep constant tabs on Qiyana's jungle clearing lines and only intercept them if you have a clear advantage or counter-gank opportunity. Prioritize reaching your level 6 power spike over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Dragon's Descent Domination","why":"Dragon's Descent (R) transforms you into a Dragon, granting bonus health, an unstoppable dash, and empowered basic abilities. Leap onto Qiyana before she can dash away and follow up with empowered Molten Burst (E) for a fight-ending burst — just watch for Supreme Display of Talent (R) stunning your team first. This spike turns you into a real teamfight threat who can dictate the shape of a fight through raw damage."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Spear of Shojin / Titanic Spike","why":"Completing your core item triggers a massive damage, health, and wave clear multiplier that completely overpowers traditional brawlers or isolated targets. Qiyana's first item is the threshold where the element combo goes from a heavy trade to a full deletion."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Juggernaut","why":"Shyvana becomes a high-impact skirmish diver who relies on generating Fury and landing Dragon's Descent initiations. Her late-game damage scaling is AoE-focused, so coordinate with your team before diving in, since Qiyana can still burst a diver down or stun your engage with her R. Use Molten Burst's slow to control the contesting jungler around objective pits and secure the takedown."}],"start":"Start your clear at the Raptor or Red Buff camp, using Inferno Aegis (W)'s shield and delayed detonation to get through camps quickly and safely. Qiyana clears at a fair pace once she has an element, and she picks her element around where she intends to path.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using Molten Burst (E) to slow and chip it down for a clean contest. You can solo early Dragons safely behind your Scalemail passive resistances if Qiyana is confirmed away ganking top lane. Qiyana contests scuttle with the river element, where the slow makes the trade one-sided before it starts.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the moment they spawn, using Inferno Aegis (W) to clear them quickly. Use Molten Burst (E) to slow and punish Qiyana if she contests, catching her mid-clear. Qiyana has poor objective damage and no way to hold a pit, so her smite fights are the weakest part of her game.","invade":"Walk into Qiyana's jungle once you confirm she is committed to a low-health camp. Lead with Molten Burst (E) to slow her, then use Inferno Aegis (W)'s shield and burst to win the duel — but bait out Elemental Wrath (Q) first, since it can stun or slow you depending on her element. Qiyana escapes an invade with Terrashape and Audacity, so cut the wall she is heading for rather than chasing.","watch":"Watch for counter-invades if you burn Inferno Aegis (W) early while clearing a camp — you lose its shield and movement speed and have no other escape or engage tool until it's back up, which is exactly when Qiyana's Terrashape (W)/Audacity (E) dashes can catch you. Track which element she is holding — brush means stealth, river means slow, and rock means the wall stun is armed.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using wards to safely trace Qiyana's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming to reach your item and Dragon's Descent power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable frontline threat. Qiyana is weakest in open ground away from terrain, where the ultimate has nothing to stun against.","split":"You can split push exceptionally well due to your massive wave clear, pulling enemies to side lanes while you generate Fury. In teamfights, you must be the primary engager, utilizing Dragon's Descent to dive straight into the enemy formation and unleash your AoE damage. Qiyana side lanes to look for a solo kill rather than to push, and the wall-hop makes her hard to punish for it.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies by launching your Dragon's Descent over walls to secure high-priority backline access. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely burning their frontline tanks and instantly shredding their backline carriers with empowered Molten Burst. Qiyana picks are a brush-stealth Q onto someone alone, so sweep the bushes on the side she was last seen.","win":"Generate an unanswerable early-to-mid game gold lead through successful power farming and absolute maximum dragon control. Close the match by relentlessly driving through teamfights in dragon form, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable juggernaut before they can scale their defensive items. Qiyana wins by one-shotting a carry and using the wall stun to make the follow-up fight unwinnable."},"Shyvana":{"tldr":"This is a mirror matchup: both sides have identical Emberstrike (Q), Inferno Aegis (W), Molten Burst (E), and Dragon's Descent (R). Nothing in the kit favors either side — the matchup is decided by who clears faster through better pathing, who lands Molten Burst first in a skirmish, and who reaches Fury and Dragon Form first.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Even","why":"Both sides open with the identical Emberstrike (Q) true-damage cleave and Inferno Aegis (W) shield, so neither Shyvana clears faster or healthier by kit alone. Route choice and execution decide who reaches level 3 first."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Even","why":"Both sides unlock the same Emberstrike (Q) recast for an auto-attack reset. If the enemy Shyvana invades, a duel comes down to who has more Fury and Inferno Aegis (W) available, not a kit advantage — this window is even."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Even","why":"Both sides gain Molten Burst (E) at the same level, so whoever lands the slow first controls the duel. With an identical full kit online for both junglers, this route window is even rather than a peak for either side."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early farming thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive power-farming and dragon rushing. Keep constant tabs on Shyvana's jungle clearing lines and only intercept them if you have a clear advantage or counter-gank opportunity. Prioritize reaching your level 6 power spike over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Even","why":"Both sides gain the identical Dragon's Descent (R) transform at level 6, with the same dash, fear, and empowered basic abilities in Dragon Form. Whoever hits level 6 first with more Fury banked gets the first strike, but the kit itself gives neither side an edge."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Spear of Shojin / Titanic Spike","why":"Completing your core item triggers a massive damage, health, and wave clear multiplier that completely overpowers traditional brawlers or isolated targets. Camps die fast now, letting you instantly clear out both sides before forcing highly aggressive neutral pit takes. This priority shatters their capacity to duel you evenly in the river."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Juggernaut","why":"Shyvana transitions into a high-impact skirmish diver and unstoppable juggernaut who relies entirely on generating Fury and landing massive Dragon's Descent initiations. Your late-game raw damage scaling shifts into a purely AoE-focused line, making it imperative to coordinate with your team before diving into extreme burst damage. Maintain extreme technical precision around high-tier objective pits to cleanly melt the contesting smiter with your E mark and secure the objective."}],"start":"Start your clear at the Raptor or Red Buff camp, using Inferno Aegis (W) for its shield and burst explosion against grouped monsters to keep your clear healthy. Since the enemy Shyvana has the identical tool, your route efficiency and level 4 timing decide who comes out ahead, not the kit.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using Molten Burst (E) to slow it and drop its health bar for river control. Both junglers can solo early Dragons using identical Scalemail passive stacks, so whoever arrives first and hits Molten Burst first typically wins the crab.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the exact moment they spawn, using your Inferno Aegis AoE to clear them rapidly. Use your E to mark and run down Shyvana if they dare to contest, catching them off-guard mid-clear.","invade":"Only invade the enemy Shyvana's jungle when you catch her clearly low from a camp — her Inferno Aegis shield and Molten Burst slow are identical to yours, so an even-health fight is a coinflip, not a favorable duel.","watch":"Watch for counter-invades if you exhaust Inferno Aegis early while clearing a camp, leaving you without its shield and movement speed. The enemy Shyvana faces the exact same cooldown window, so track her Inferno Aegis usage as closely as your own.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using wards to safely trace the enemy Shyvana's routes. Since her Dragon's Descent hits the same power spike as yours, catching up means out-farming her to your item spikes rather than out-fighting her early.","split":"You can split push exceptionally well due to your massive wave clear, pulling enemies to side lanes while you generate Fury. In teamfights, you must be the primary engager, utilizing Dragon's Descent to dive straight into the enemy formation and unleash your AoE damage.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies by launching your Dragon's Descent over walls to secure high-priority backline access. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, burning through their frontline tanks and shredding their backline carriers with empowered Molten Burst in Dragon Form.","win":"Since the enemy jungler has an identical kit, win by out-farming her to your Dragon's Descent spike and item timings, then close the match by winning teamfights in Dragon Form before she reaches the same power level."},"Trundle":{"tldr":"Trundle adjusts your early mechanical tempo by requiring high precision over Molten Burst (E) slows and Inferno Aegis (W) shield timing. Shyvana commands skirmish velocities through Emberstrike (Q) auto-attack resets and true damage, deploying Dragon's Descent (R) to force fights once she has Fury. Trundle has no hard CC either, but he blocks paths with Pillar of Ice, sustains through Frozen Domain and King's Tribute, and can shred your resistances mid-fight with Subjugate.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Shyvana Favored","why":"Start with Emberstrike (Q) for a true-damage attack that hits both the target and the surrounding area, and use Inferno Aegis (W) for its shield to keep your clear healthy. Trundle's Chomp (Q) is single-target only, so Shyvana's opening camps go faster."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Shyvana Dominant","why":"The recast on Emberstrike (Q) gives you an auto-attack reset that accelerates your single-target clear and dueling potential. If Trundle attempts an uncalculated early entry into your jungle, activate Inferno Aegis (W) for the shield and speed to stick to him before taking a close-quarters duel using your Q reset to shatter his health bar — his Chomp saps your attack damage, so watch your trade. This secures your early map pressure and punishes poor invades."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Shyvana Absolute Peak","why":"Access to Molten Burst (E) completes your dueling kit with a fireball that explodes on large targets and slows them. Use it to keep Trundle from kiting behind his own Pillar of Ice, or burst through large monsters to accelerate your clear speed."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early farming thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive power-farming and dragon rushing. Keep constant tabs on Trundle's jungle clearing lines and only intercept them if you have a clear advantage or counter-gank opportunity. Prioritize reaching your level 6 power spike over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Dragon's Descent Domination","why":"Dragon's Descent (R) transforms Shyvana into Dragon Form with empowered basics and a dash-leap. Trundle's Subjugate steals a growing percent of Shyvana's health, armor and magic resist during the fight, which cuts into her tanky stat stacking, so a longer trade favors him more than the burst window right after Shyvana's engage. Land your damage fast rather than fighting through the full duration of his ultimate."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Spear of Shojin / Titanic Spike","why":"Completing your core item triggers a massive damage, health, and wave clear multiplier that completely overpowers traditional brawlers or isolated targets. Trundle's first item is a real threshold — the sustain plus stat theft makes long fights ones he cannot lose."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Juggernaut","why":"Shyvana transitions into a high-impact skirmish diver who relies on generating Fury and landing Dragon's Descent initiations. Trundle's Subjugate directly punishes stacking resistances by stealing a percent of them mid-fight, so coordinate burst damage with your team rather than tanking through a prolonged trade with him. Maintain precision around high-tier objective pits to cleanly melt the contesting smiter with Molten Burst and secure the objective."}],"start":"Start your clear at the Raptor or Red Buff camp, using Inferno Aegis (W) for its shield and burst explosion against grouped monsters to keep your clear healthy. Trundle clears fast in Frozen Domain and heals off King's Tribute, so he arrives at every crossing at full health.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using Molten Burst (E) to slow it and drop its health bar for river control — watch for Trundle dropping Pillar of Ice to block your path to the pit. You can solo early Dragons using your Scalemail passive resistance stacks if Trundle is visible ganking top lane. Contest scuttle against Trundle outside his Frozen Domain, because inside it the attack speed and stat steal decide the duel.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the exact moment they spawn, using Inferno Aegis (W) to shield through their damage while you clear. Use Molten Burst (E) to slow and run down Trundle if he dares to contest, catching him off-guard mid-clear. Trundle is an excellent objective duellist because Subjugate drains health and resistances at exactly the moment it matters.","invade":"Walk boldly into Trundle's jungle the moment you confirm he is engaged with a low-health camp. Lead with Molten Burst to slow him, popping Inferno Aegis for the shield and speed to close a melee duel before he can drop Frozen Domain to out-sustain you. Trundle invades and wins, so an invade against him only works with numbers or with him already low.","watch":"Watch for sudden counter-invades if you exhaust Inferno Aegis early while clearing a camp, leaving you without its shield and speed right as Trundle can wall off your escape with Pillar of Ice or land Chomp to sap your attack damage mid-fight. The pillar is the tell — once it is placed your retreat line is gone, so move before it goes down and not after.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using wards to safely trace Trundle's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming to reach your item and Dragon's Descent power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable frontline threat. Trundle is weakest outside his Frozen Domain, where the attack speed bonus does not apply and his damage is ordinary.","split":"You can split push exceptionally well due to your massive wave clear, pulling enemies to side lanes while you generate Fury. In teamfights, you must be the primary engager, utilizing Dragon's Descent to dive straight into the enemy formation and unleash your AoE damage. Trundle side lanes very well and takes towers fast, and answering him alone in his own zone rarely works.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies by launching your Dragon's Descent over walls to secure high-priority backline access. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, burning through their frontline tanks and shredding their backline carriers with empowered Molten Burst in Dragon Form. Trundle picks are a pillar behind you at a choke, so cross corridors with a dash available or not at all.","win":"Generate an unanswerable early-to-mid game gold lead through successful power farming and absolute maximum dragon control. Close the match by relentlessly driving through teamfights in dragon form, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable juggernaut before they can scale their defensive items. Trundle wins by taking every duel and every objective and stealing the stats that would have answered him."},"Udyr":{"tldr":"Udyr threatens your early camps with Blazing Stampede (E), a stun on his first hit per target plus a speed boost that lets him run you down before you build Fury. Shyvana answers with Emberstrike (Q) for on-hit auto resets, Inferno Aegis (W) for a shield and delayed AoE burst, and Dragon's Descent (R) to force a fight once she has 100 Fury. Udyr wants to catch you off-cooldown mid-camp and stack stance swaps for chip damage; Shyvana wants to trade through his shield with true damage and win the fight once Dragon's Descent is up.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Shyvana Favored","why":"Start with Inferno Aegis (W) for its shield and delayed AoE detonation, using auto attacks in between to keep the camp's health dropping quickly. This gives a fast, safe path across your starting camps while Udyr, on cooldown-gated stances, clears slightly slower."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Shyvana Dominant","why":"Unlocking Emberstrike (Q) gives an on-hit auto-attack reset that speeds up single-target clear and dueling. If Udyr invades early, use Inferno Aegis's speed to stick to him and reset autos with Q — his Iron Mantle shield only covers two hits, so a sustained trade favors you."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Shyvana Absolute Peak","why":"Molten Burst (E) adds a slow that locks Udyr into trades rather than letting him disengage with Blazing Stampede's speed boost. Landing it turns an even brawl into a favorable one, and it doubles as burst on large jungle camps to speed up your clear."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities are strong enough now to clear quickly and let you push for tempo, but Udyr's stance swaps make him a genuine skirmish threat at any moment. Track his jungle pathing and only intercept when you have priority; hold camps as needed to hit level 6 without forcing a bad fight."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Dragon's Descent Domination","why":"Dragon's Descent (R) gives Dragon Form, a leap that forces enemies to flee plus empowered basics, and only needs 100 Fury to activate rather than a cooldown. Landing the leap into a wall setup lets you chain empowered Molten Burst before Udyr can Blazing Stampede away. This is your first real breakpoint against him — before it, he can disengage; after, he mostly can't."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Spear of Shojin / Titanic Spike","why":"Your first completed item pushes your damage and clear speed well past Udyr's, letting you clear both jungle halves faster and contest objective pits more aggressively. He can no longer win a straight river duel once you're a full item ahead. Udyr's first item is where the awakened stance rotations stop being a heavy trade and start being a kill."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Juggernaut","why":"Shyvana is a high-impact skirmish diver who relies on generating Fury and landing Dragon's Descent to start fights. Coordinate with your team before diving, since your damage is AoE-focused rather than single-target burst. Around late objective pits, use Molten Burst's slow on the contesting smiter to secure the kill on the objective."}],"start":"Start your clear at the Raptor or Red Buff camp, using Inferno Aegis's delayed detonation to speed up the AoE portion of the camp. Track Udyr's opening camp choice so you know which side of the jungle he's clearing toward before you commit to a full loop. Udyr clears extremely fast with stance swaps and shields himself through it, so he is rarely below full health.","scuttle":"Contest the scuttle crab when it spawns; Molten Burst's slow lets you keep range advantage on a stance-swapping brawler like Udyr while your on-hit Q chips it down. If Udyr is committed to a top-lane gank, this is a window to solo an early Dragon using Scalemail's stacked resistances. Contest scuttle against Udyr before he has two stance points, because the awakened trade pattern beats most kits at level three.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the moment they spawn; Inferno Aegis clears the wave of grubs quickly. If Udyr contests, Molten Burst's slow keeps him from disengaging once he's committed his stance swaps. Udyr is a strong objective duellist with high sustained damage but no way to zone you out of the pit.","invade":"Invade while Udyr is mid-camp and low on his own sustain (Iron Mantle on cooldown). Lead with Molten Burst to slow him, then pop Inferno Aegis so the shield and delayed burst carry you through the trade before he can stack stance-swap attack speed. Udyr wins most invades fought straight, so the answer is to take his camps while he is on the far side.","watch":"Watch for counter-invades if you burn Inferno Aegis early on a camp — you lose its movement speed and shield, and Udyr's Blazing Stampede stun plus movement speed lets him catch and stun you before you can reset. Blazing Stampede only travels in a straight line, so breaking the line with terrain removes the stun entirely.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, give up contested scuttle crabs and ward to track Udyr's routes instead of fighting him blind. Prioritize clearing to your first item and level 6 so Dragon's Descent's true damage and Fury tankiness can carry you back into fights. Udyr is weakest against distance and walls, since he has no ranged threat and no way to catch a champion who leaves.","split":"You can split push exceptionally well due to your massive wave clear, pulling enemies to side lanes while you generate Fury. In teamfights, you must be the primary engager, utilizing Dragon's Descent to dive straight into the enemy formation and unleash your AoE damage. Udyr side lanes strongly and takes towers quickly, but he cannot answer a fight happening anywhere else.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies by launching your Dragon's Descent over walls to secure high-priority backline access. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely burning their frontline tanks and instantly shredding their backline carriers with empowered Molten Burst. Udyr does not pick; he runs you down, so the danger is being caught in a corridor rather than being ambushed.","win":"Build a gold and Dragon-control lead by out-clearing Udyr and contesting scuttle and objectives with Molten Burst's slow and Inferno Aegis's AoE. Close games by forcing fights in Dragon Form, where Dragon's Descent's true-damage Q and zone control outlast Udyr's stance-swap sustain. Udyr wins by taking every fight he can reach and every objective he can stand next to before the carries scale."},"Vi":{"tldr":"Vi's real threat is Cease and Desist (R), a long-range dash that grounds and knocks up its target — that's her gank and her all-in, and it isn't live until level 6. Before that she has to catch you with Vault Breaker (Q), a short dash that knocks back. Shyvana clears with Emberstrike (Q) resets and Inferno Aegis (W) AoE, and uses Molten Burst (E) to slow Vi off her dash before Dragon's Descent (R) is available at 100 Fury.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Shyvana Favored","why":"Start with Inferno Aegis (W) to secure a fast area-of-effect clear, utilizing the movement speed and continuous magic damage to melt the camp. Execute basic attacks to extend Inferno Aegis's duration, ensuring maximum efficiency. This establishes an exceptionally fast and healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Shyvana Dominant","why":"Unlocking Emberstrike (Q) provides an impactful auto-attack reset that drastically accelerates your single-target clear and dueling potential. If Vi attempts an uncalculated early entry into your jungle, activate Inferno Aegis to stick to them before taking an aggressive close-quarters duel using your Q reset to shatter their health bar. This completely secures your early map pressure and punishes poor invades."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Shyvana Absolute Peak","why":"Access to Molten Burst (E) completes your lethal dueling portfolio by introducing a versatile fireball that marks enemies for percent-health damage. Use this tool to instantly secure your damage trades or melt through high-health monsters to accelerate your clear speed. This grants an elite operational flexibility allowing you to turn almost any early brawl if you land your mark accurately."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early farming thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive power-farming and dragon rushing. Keep constant tabs on Vi's jungle clearing lines and only intercept them if you have a clear advantage or counter-gank opportunity. Prioritize reaching your level 6 power spike over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Dragon's Descent Domination","why":"Dragon's Descent (R) introduces an immense combat transformation that provides massive bonus health, an unstoppable dash, and empowered area-of-effect abilities. Execute a precise ultimate activation to drag Vi into a wall, unleashing your empowered Molten Burst for a clean kill. This massive mechanical spike turns you into an absolute map-wide teamfight threat who dictates the shape of every fight through raw damage."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Spear of Shojin / Titanic Spike","why":"Completing your core item triggers a massive damage, health, and wave clear multiplier that completely overpowers traditional brawlers or isolated targets. Vi's first item is where the charge-and-shred pattern stops being a chunk and starts closing out squishier junglers."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Juggernaut","why":"Shyvana transitions into a high-impact skirmish diver and unstoppable juggernaut who relies entirely on generating Fury and landing massive Dragon's Descent initiations. Your late-game raw damage scaling shifts into a purely AoE-focused line, making it imperative to coordinate with your team before diving into extreme burst damage. Maintain extreme technical precision around high-tier objective pits to cleanly melt the contesting smiter with your E mark and secure the objective."}],"start":"Start your clear at the Raptor or Red Buff camp, using Inferno Aegis's AoE detonation to speed up the clear. Vi clears quickly with Denting Blows shred and the passive shield keeps her healthy, so she paths without recalling early.","scuttle":"Contest the scuttle crab as it spawns; Molten Burst's slow keeps Vi from Vault Breaker-ing away once she's committed. If Vi is confirmed on a top-lane gank, that's a window to solo an early Dragon behind Scalemail's stacked resistances. Vi contests scuttle with a charged Vault Breaker, which wins the crab by moving you off it as much as by damage.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the moment they spawn; Inferno Aegis clears them quickly. If Vi contests, Molten Burst's slow punishes her for engaging without her ultimate. Vi has good objective damage from the armour shred but cannot hold a pit once her charge is spent.","invade":"Invade while Vi is mid-camp before level 6, when she has no answer beyond Vault Breaker's short dash. Lead with Molten Burst to slow her, then use Inferno Aegis's shield and speed to close and finish the trade. Vi escapes an invade with the same charge she engages with, so the punish window is right after that Q lands.","watch":"Watch for Cease and Desist the instant Vi hits 6 — it's a long-range grounding dash that shuts down your own dash and hard-CCs you, so don't get caught out of position on cooldowns you'd need to disengage. The charge-up is visible and slow — step behind a camp while it winds and the whole engage is wasted.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, give up contested scuttle crabs and ward to trace Vi's routes rather than fight blind — her point-and-click ultimate makes a bad engage costly. Prioritize farming to your first item and level 6, where Dragon's Descent's tankiness and true damage let you re-enter fights. Vi is weakest with her Q on cooldown, when she is a melee champion with no gap-closer and no escape.","split":"You can split push exceptionally well due to your massive wave clear, pulling enemies to side lanes while you generate Fury. In teamfights, you must be the primary engager, utilizing Dragon's Descent to dive straight into the enemy formation and unleash your AoE damage. Vi side lanes adequately but would rather be near her team, where a point-and-click lockdown starts a fight.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies by launching your Dragon's Descent over walls to secure high-priority backline access. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely burning their frontline tanks and instantly shredding their backline carriers with empowered Molten Burst. Vi picks are Cease and Desist on whoever she can reach, and it cannot be dodged, so respect the health threshold.","win":"Build a gold and Dragon-control lead by out-clearing Vi before her ultimate comes online, and avoid isolated skirmishes once she hits level 6. Close games by forcing fights in Dragon Form, where your true-damage Q and zone control outlast her burst. Vi wins by locking down the one target her team needs removed and shredding the armour off it."},"Xin Zhao":{"tldr":"Xin Zhao has a real hard CC threat from level 1: Three Talon Strike (Q) knocks up on his third attack, and Audacious Charge (E) is a slowing dash to close the gap. His ultimate, Crescent Guard, also knocks back and grants damage immunity from outside its radius. Shyvana answers with Emberstrike (Q) resets and Molten Burst (E) to slow him before he can land the Q knockup, saving Inferno Aegis's shield for when he commits to the dash.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Shyvana Favored","why":"Start with Inferno Aegis (W) to secure a fast area-of-effect clear, utilizing the movement speed and continuous magic damage to melt the camp. Execute basic attacks to extend Inferno Aegis's duration, ensuring maximum efficiency. This establishes an exceptionally fast and healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Shyvana Dominant","why":"Unlocking Emberstrike (Q) provides an impactful auto-attack reset that drastically accelerates your single-target clear and dueling potential. If Xin Zhao attempts an uncalculated early entry into your jungle, activate Inferno Aegis to stick to them before taking an aggressive close-quarters duel using your Q reset to shatter their health bar. This completely secures your early map pressure and punishes poor invades."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Shyvana Absolute Peak","why":"Access to Molten Burst (E) completes your lethal dueling portfolio by introducing a versatile fireball that marks enemies for percent-health damage. Use this tool to instantly secure your damage trades or melt through high-health monsters to accelerate your clear speed. This grants an elite operational flexibility allowing you to turn almost any early brawl if you land your mark accurately."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early farming thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive power-farming and dragon rushing. Keep constant tabs on Xin Zhao's jungle clearing lines and only intercept them if you have a clear advantage or counter-gank opportunity. Prioritize reaching your level 6 power spike over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Dragon's Descent Domination","why":"Dragon's Descent (R) introduces an immense combat transformation that provides massive bonus health, an unstoppable dash, and empowered area-of-effect abilities. Execute a precise ultimate activation to drag Xin Zhao into a wall, unleashing your empowered Molten Burst for a clean kill. This massive mechanical spike turns you into an absolute map-wide teamfight threat who dictates the shape of every fight through raw damage."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Spear of Shojin / Titanic Spike","why":"Completing your core item triggers a massive damage, health, and wave clear multiplier that completely overpowers traditional brawlers or isolated targets. Xin Zhao's first item is a sharp jump — the knock-up combo goes from a strong trade to a kill on most junglers."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Juggernaut","why":"Shyvana transitions into a high-impact skirmish diver and unstoppable juggernaut who relies entirely on generating Fury and landing massive Dragon's Descent initiations. Your late-game raw damage scaling shifts into a purely AoE-focused line, making it imperative to coordinate with your team before diving into extreme burst damage. Maintain extreme technical precision around high-tier objective pits to cleanly melt the contesting smiter with your E mark and secure the objective."}],"start":"Start your clear at the Raptor or Red Buff camp, using Inferno Aegis's AoE detonation to speed up the clear. Xin Zhao clears fast and heals off Determination, so he reaches three healthy and looking for the first fight.","scuttle":"Contest the scuttle crab as it spawns, but watch his Three Talon Strike stacks — the third auto knocks you up and can flip the fight. If he's committed to a top-lane gank, that's a window to solo an early Dragon behind Scalemail's stacked resistances. Contest scuttle against Xin Zhao only with a health lead — the challenge poke into the knock-up is one of the best level-three duels.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the moment they spawn; Inferno Aegis clears them quickly. If Xin Zhao contests, use Molten Burst's slow to keep him from stacking his Q autos for the knockup. Xin Zhao is a strong objective duellist and Crescent Guard can remove everyone but you from the pit fight.","invade":"Invade while Xin Zhao is mid-camp and can't safely stack Three Talon Strike on you. Lead with Molten Burst to slow him, then use Inferno Aegis's shield and speed to close out the trade before he lands his knockup. Xin Zhao wins invades fought straight, so the answer is to take his camps rather than to meet him at one.","watch":"Watch for sudden counter-invades if you exhaust your Inferno Aegis early while clearing a camp, leaving you without bonus movement speed. This leaves you temporarily without your primary gap-closing and escape tool to execute a clean turnaround. The third Q hit is the knock-up — disengage after the second, and the combo has nothing left to chain.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, give up contested scuttle crabs and ward to trace Xin Zhao's routes rather than fight him blind. Prioritize farming to your first item and level 6 so Dragon's Descent's tankiness and true damage let you re-enter fights. Xin Zhao is weakest against ranged kiting once his charge is spent, since he has nothing else that closes a gap.","split":"You can split push exceptionally well due to your massive wave clear, pulling enemies to side lanes while you generate Fury. In teamfights, you must be the primary engager, utilizing Dragon's Descent to dive straight into the enemy formation and unleash your AoE damage. Xin Zhao side lanes well early but falls behind the real side laners once full builds arrive.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies by launching your Dragon's Descent over walls to secure high-priority backline access. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely burning their frontline tanks and instantly shredding their backline carriers with empowered Molten Burst. Xin Zhao picks are an Audacious Charge from a bush into the knock-up, so respect river bushes on his side.","win":"Build a gold and Dragon-control lead by out-clearing Xin Zhao and slowing him with Molten Burst before he can stack Three Talon Strike on you. Close games by forcing fights in Dragon Form, where your true-damage Q and zone control outlast his single-target burst — and be ready to disengage if he lands his ultimate's knockback. Xin Zhao wins by snowballing the early game and using Crescent Guard to make one late fight a 1v1 he chose."},"Amumu":{"tldr":"Amumu leans on Despair (W)'s percent-health drain and the hard engage of Bandage Toss (Q) into Curse of the Sad Mummy (R). Shyvana answers with strong dueling from Emberstrike (Q) auto-attack resets and the shield off Inferno Aegis (W), then closes fights with Dragon's Descent (R). Amumu wants to hook you out with Bandage Toss before you have Fury banked, punishing grouped clears with Despair's drain.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Shyvana Favored","why":"Open with Inferno Aegis (W) for the shield, movement speed, and delayed AoE explosion, which is your only point at level 1 but clears a camp safely on its own. Amumu's single point in Bandage Toss or Despair doesn't threaten a clean level-1 duel or camp contest. This establishes a fast, healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Shyvana Dominant","why":"Unlocking Emberstrike (Q) gives you an auto-attack reset that also hits nearby enemies, sharply raising your dueling damage. If Amumu tries an early invade, Inferno Aegis's shield blunts his Bandage Toss burst long enough for your Q resets to win the fight. This secures your early map pressure and punishes poor invades."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Shyvana Absolute Peak","why":"Access to Molten Burst (E) adds a slowing fireball that explodes on large targets, letting you lock a target down for a full trade or speed through tougher camps. Landing the slow means Amumu can't Bandage Toss his way out of a duel he's already losing. This gives you the tools to win most early brawls you commit to."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early farming thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive power-farming and dragon rushing. Keep constant tabs on Amumu's jungle clearing lines and only intercept them if you have a clear advantage or counter-gank opportunity. Prioritize reaching your level 6 power spike over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Dragon's Descent Domination","why":"Dragon's Descent (R) grants massive bonus health, an unstoppable dash, and empowered basic abilities. Landing the dash to pin Amumu against a wall lets you follow up with empowered Molten Burst's fire trail for a quick kill window. This spike turns you into a genuine map-wide skirmish and teamfight threat who can run down Amumu's poke-and-drain play instead of getting worn down by it."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Spear of Shojin / Titanic Spike","why":"Completing your core item triggers a massive damage, health, and wave clear multiplier that completely overpowers traditional brawlers or isolated targets. Amumu's first completed item makes Despair and Tantrum clear at real speed and turns his engage into a genuine team-fight threat."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Juggernaut","why":"Shyvana transitions into a high-impact skirmish diver who relies on generating Fury and landing Dragon's Descent initiations. Your late-game damage scaling shifts into an AoE-focused line, so coordinate with your team before diving in rather than fighting alone. Around objective pits, use Molten Burst's slow to keep Amumu locked out of range while you secure the smite."}],"start":"Start at Red Buff or Raptors, using Inferno Aegis's shield and delayed explosion to clear safely at level 1. Track whether Amumu shows near your side of the map before committing to a full clear, since his Bandage Toss range makes contested camps risky. Amumu opens on a buff and clears steadily off Despair and Tantrum, so he is rarely far from his own camps before three.","scuttle":"Contest the scuttle crab using Molten Burst (E) to slow it while you finish it off with Emberstrike (Q) resets. Amumu's Bandage Toss can hook him in from 1100 range to steal or contest the crab, so don't commit fully until you've confirmed his position. Amumu contests scuttle with the Bandage Toss, not with damage — if the bandage misses he loses the crab and the trade.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs, using Inferno Aegis's AoE explosion to clear the pack quickly. If Amumu shows to contest, Molten Burst's slow lets you catch and punish him before he can Bandage Toss away. Amumu is a fast grub and drake taker because Despair burns percent health, but he needs a body between him and your smite.","invade":"Walk into Amumu's jungle once you confirm he's committed to a low-health camp. Lead with Molten Burst to slow him, then pop Inferno Aegis for the shield and AoE explosion so he can't walk away from the duel. Amumu has no dash and no escape, so an invade that starts outside bandage range is close to free.","watch":"Watch for Bandage Toss (Q) — a 1100-range hook that stuns and drags you to Amumu — if you're caught out of position right after using Inferno Aegis on a camp, since you'll be without your shield and speed boost for the follow-up fight. Watch the Bandage Toss cooldown — with it down Amumu is a slow melee champion with no way to start anything.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using wards to safely trace Amumu's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming to reach your item and Dragon's Descent power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable frontline threat. Amumu is weakest between one and five, when his own duel is nothing and his ganks still need a laner to hold the target.","split":"You can split push exceptionally well due to your massive wave clear, pulling enemies to side lanes while you generate Fury. In teamfights, you must be the primary engager, utilizing Dragon's Descent to dive straight into the enemy formation and unleash your AoE damage. Amumu cannot hold a side lane; leaving him alone on a wave concedes almost nothing.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies by launching your Dragon's Descent over walls to secure high-priority backline access. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely burning their frontline tanks and instantly shredding their backline carriers with empowered Molten Burst. Amumu picks come from fog bandages at river entrances, so hug the wall he cannot line up against.","win":"Generate an unanswerable early-to-mid game gold lead through successful power farming and absolute maximum dragon control. Close the match by relentlessly driving through teamfights in dragon form, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable juggernaut before they can scale their defensive items. Amumu wins by landing one Curse of the Sad Mummy on two or more people and letting his team clean up."},"Hecarim":{"tldr":"Hecarim threatens with Devastating Charge (E) knockback bursts and Spirit of Dread (W) healing off nearby damage, both of which scale hard with his Warpath move-speed passive. Shyvana out-clears with Emberstrike (Q) true-damage resets and Inferno Aegis (W) shielding, closing distance before Hecarim can build up speed. Hecarim wants to build Warpath stacks and run you down with Devastating Charge; Shyvana wants to shield through the trade and punish with true damage that ignores his resistance stacking.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Shyvana Favored","why":"Open camps with Inferno Aegis (W) for its shield, movement speed, and delayed detonation, then extend with Emberstrike (Q) auto-resets once it's up. This establishes a fast, healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Shyvana Dominant","why":"Unlocking Emberstrike (Q) gives you an auto-attack reset that accelerates your single-target clear and dueling, and its true damage ignores the resistances Hecarim's Spirit of Dread grants him. If Hecarim attempts an early invade, use Inferno Aegis's shield to absorb his Devastating Charge and Emberstrike's reset to shatter his health bar."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Shyvana Absolute Peak","why":"Molten Burst (E) rounds out your kit as a slowing fireball that explodes on hitting a large target, giving you a way to stick to a duel or clear high-health monsters faster. Against Hecarim this slow blunts his Devastating Charge approach, buying time for Emberstrike's true damage to work through his Spirit of Dread resistances."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early farming thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive power-farming and dragon rushing. Keep constant tabs on Hecarim's jungle clearing lines and only intercept them if you have a clear advantage or counter-gank opportunity. Prioritize reaching your level 6 power spike over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Dragon's Descent Domination","why":"Dragon's Descent (R) transforms you into a Dragon with bonus health, an unstoppable dash, and empowered basic abilities, causing enemies it hits to flee in fear — a direct answer to Hecarim's own Onslaught of Shadows fear if you land it first. Follow up with empowered Molten Burst and Emberstrike, whose true damage keeps cutting through his resistance stacking."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Spear of Shojin / Titanic Spike","why":"Completing your core item triggers a massive damage, health, and wave clear multiplier that completely overpowers traditional brawlers or isolated targets. Hecarim's first item is where the Rampage spam stops being clear speed and starts being a real health bar of damage."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Juggernaut","why":"Shyvana transitions into a high-impact skirmish diver who relies on generating Fury and landing Dragon's Descent initiations. Coordinate with your team before diving, since Hecarim's Onslaught of Shadows can catch a scattered team just as easily as yours can. Around objective pits, use Molten Burst's slow and Emberstrike's true damage to control the smiter duel against Hecarim."}],"start":"Start your clear at the Raptor or Red Buff camp, using Inferno Aegis (W)'s shield, movement speed, and delayed detonation to speed through camps. Hecarim clears fast with stacked Rampage and Spirit of Dread sustain, and his passive means he is always moving.","scuttle":"Contest the scuttle crab with Emberstrike (Q) resets, respecting Devastating Charge's knockback if Hecarim shows up mid-fight — the damage scales with how far he's traveled, so a longer approach hits harder. You can solo early Dragon using your passive Scalemail stacks if Hecarim is visible ganking top lane. Hecarim wins scuttle fights by arriving with speed already built, so the crab is usually decided before you see him.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the moment they spawn, using Inferno Aegis's detonation and Emberstrike resets to clear them rapidly. If Hecarim contests, watch for a Devastating Charge knockback interrupting your smite or clear. Hecarim takes objectives quickly off Rampage stacks and can disengage a lost smite fight faster than most.","invade":"Walk into Hecarim's jungle once you confirm he's committed to a low-health camp elsewhere. Lead with Inferno Aegis's shield, since Spirit of Dread can heal Hecarim back through your damage if the fight drags on. Hecarim is hard to catch in an invade — the passive speed and Devastating Charge mean he leaves whenever he chooses.","watch":"Watch for Hecarim building Warpath stacks off bonus move speed before committing to Devastating Charge — the longer he's been moving, the harder the knockback hits. Emberstrike's true damage ignores his Spirit of Dread resistance buff, and Dragon's Descent's own fear answers his engage once you reach Dragon Form. Watch how fast he is moving: a Hecarim already at speed is committed to a charge, and the knock-back is coming.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, avoid contesting scuttle crabs where a fast-moving Hecarim can run you down with Devastating Charge. Funnel into farming to reach your first item and Dragon's Descent power spike before forcing fights. Hecarim is weakest in a confined space with no runway, where Devastating Charge cannot build to anything.","split":"You can split push exceptionally well due to your massive wave clear, pulling enemies to side lanes while you generate Fury. In teamfights, you must be the primary engager, utilizing Dragon's Descent to dive straight into the enemy formation and unleash your AoE damage. Hecarim side lanes to build a lead and rotates faster than anyone answering him, so the wave is never the whole story.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies by launching your Dragon's Descent over walls to secure high-priority backline access. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely burning their frontline tanks and instantly shredding their backline carriers with empowered Molten Burst. Hecarim picks are a long charge out of fog into a knock-back against a wall, so keep open ground behind you.","win":"Generate an unanswerable early-to-mid game gold lead through successful power farming and absolute maximum dragon control. Close the match by relentlessly driving through teamfights in dragon form, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable juggernaut before they can scale their defensive items. Hecarim wins by reaching your back line before the fight has shape, usually through an Onslaught of Shadows fear."},"Jarvan IV":{"tldr":"Jarvan IV threatens with Dragon Strike (Q) into Demacian Standard (E) combos for armor shred and knockup-adjacent burst, and Cataclysm (R) to wall you in for a duel. Shyvana out-clears with Emberstrike (Q) resets and Inferno Aegis (W) shielding, and her no-cost recastable Q gives her one of the fastest early clears in the jungle against J4's mana-gated tools. Jarvan IV wants to catch you with an early all-in before your clear snowballs; Shyvana wants to out-farm him and force the fight only once Dragon's Descent (R) is up.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Shyvana Favored","why":"Open camps with Inferno Aegis (W) for its shield, movement speed, and delayed detonation, then extend with Emberstrike (Q) auto-resets once it's up. Emberstrike is a no-cost, no-cooldown-gated-by-mana recastable true-damage attack, giving you one of the fastest early clears in the jungle against Jarvan IV's single mana-cost E flag."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Shyvana Dominant","why":"Unlocking Emberstrike (Q) gives you an auto-attack reset that accelerates your single-target clear and dueling. If Jarvan IV attempts an uncalculated early entry into your jungle, use Inferno Aegis to stick to him before taking an aggressive close-quarters duel with Emberstrike's reset to shatter his health bar. This punishes a poorly timed invade before he can land a clean Dragon Strike."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Shyvana Absolute Peak","why":"Molten Burst (E) rounds out your kit as a slowing fireball that explodes on hitting a large target, giving you a way to stick to a duel or clear high-health monsters faster. Against Jarvan IV this slow helps you stay on top of him after he lands Dragon Strike, instead of letting him disengage and reset the trade."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early farming thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive power-farming and dragon rushing. Keep constant tabs on Jarvan IV's jungle clearing lines and only intercept them if you have a clear advantage or counter-gank opportunity. Prioritize reaching your level 6 power spike over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Dragon's Descent Domination","why":"Dragon's Descent (R) transforms you into a Dragon with bonus health, an unstoppable dash, and empowered basic abilities, causing enemies it hits to flee in fear. This lets you contest a Cataclysm arena on even footing rather than getting walled in defenseless, following up with empowered Molten Burst and Emberstrike."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Spear of Shojin / Titanic Spike","why":"Completing your core item triggers a massive damage, health, and wave clear multiplier that completely overpowers traditional brawlers or isolated targets. Jarvan IV's first item makes the flag-drag rotation a genuine kill threat rather than a set-up for someone else."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Juggernaut","why":"Shyvana transitions into a high-impact skirmish diver who relies on generating Fury and landing Dragon's Descent initiations. Coordinate with your team before diving, since Jarvan IV can Cataclysm you away from your team mid-fight. Around objective pits, use Molten Burst's slow and Emberstrike's damage to control the smiter duel against Jarvan IV."}],"start":"Start your clear at the Raptor or Red Buff camp, using Inferno Aegis (W)'s shield, movement speed, and delayed detonation to speed through camps. Jarvan IV clears steadily off the flag-drag combo and is one of the most consistent level-three arrivals in the jungle.","scuttle":"Contest the scuttle crab with Emberstrike (Q) resets, staying alert for Dragon Strike's armor shred and knockup if Jarvan IV shows up to fight for it. You can solo early Dragon using your passive Scalemail stacks if Jarvan IV is visible ganking top lane. Jarvan IV contests scuttle with the E-Q knock-up, which wins the crab and the trade if you are standing on it.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the moment they spawn, using Inferno Aegis's detonation and Emberstrike resets to clear them rapidly. If Jarvan IV contests, watch for Golden Aegis's shield and slow buying him time to land Dragon Strike. Jarvan IV takes objectives at a fair pace and can wall off the pit with Cataclysm so the smite fight never happens.","invade":"Walk into Jarvan IV's jungle once you confirm he's committed to a low-health camp elsewhere. Lead with Inferno Aegis's shield to blunt Dragon Strike's initial hit before committing to the duel. Jarvan IV can flag out of most invades, so the punish window is right after that flag has been spent.","watch":"Watch for Cataclysm (R) walling you into an arena with Jarvan IV once he hits level 6 — you have no hard CC in human form to fight your way out before Dragon's Descent, so an unfavorable Cataclysm forces a straight duel on his terms. The flag is the tell — once it is on the ground the knock-up is coming, so move off the line rather than back.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, avoid contesting scuttle crabs where Jarvan IV can land Dragon Strike and follow with Demacian Standard for free damage. Funnel into farming to reach your first item and Dragon's Descent power spike before forcing fights. Jarvan IV is weakest once the flag is on cooldown, when he has no engage, no escape and modest damage.","split":"You can split push exceptionally well due to your massive wave clear, pulling enemies to side lanes while you generate Fury. In teamfights, you must be the primary engager, utilizing Dragon's Descent to dive straight into the enemy formation and unleash your AoE damage. Jarvan IV holds a side lane adequately but would rather be near an objective his cage can decide.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies by launching your Dragon's Descent over walls to secure high-priority backline access. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely burning their frontline tanks and instantly shredding their backline carriers with empowered Molten Burst. Jarvan IV picks are a flag-drag onto someone crossing a choke, so cross with a dash available or not at all.","win":"Generate an unanswerable early-to-mid game gold lead through successful power farming and absolute maximum dragon control. Close the match by relentlessly driving through teamfights in dragon form, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable juggernaut before they can scale their defensive items. Jarvan IV wins by caging the person who matters at the start of a fight and letting his team collapse on it."},"Maokai":{"tldr":"Maokai adjusts your early tempo by requiring precise use of Molten Burst (E) slows and Inferno Aegis (W) uptime against his root chain. Shyvana commands skirmishes through power farming, using Emberstrike (Q) for auto-attack resets and Dragon's Descent (R) for area-of-effect dives once she has Fury. Maokai seeks to gank with Twisted Advance (W) before you build Fury and exploit your lack of hard crowd control in human form.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Shyvana Favored","why":"Start with Emberstrike (Q) to strike the camp and the area around it, and use Inferno Aegis (W) for its shield and movement speed to stay healthy while clearing. This establishes a fast, healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Shyvana Dominant","why":"Emberstrike's (Q) true-damage auto-attack reset and Inferno Aegis's (W) shield let you win a level-2 skirmish outright: Maokai's Bramble Smash (Q) knocks back and slows, but he lacks the burst to punish you before your Q resets shred his health bar. This secures your early map pressure and punishes poor invades."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Shyvana Absolute Peak","why":"Access to Molten Burst (E) completes your dueling kit: a fireball that explodes on large targets and slows them. Use it to lock down duels through Maokai's Bramble Smash (Q) knockback, or to accelerate your clear against high-health monsters. This gives you the tools to turn most early brawls if you land the slow."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early farming thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive power-farming and dragon rushing. Keep constant tabs on Maokai's jungle clearing lines and only intercept them if you have a clear advantage or counter-gank opportunity. Prioritize reaching your level 6 power spike over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Dragon's Descent Domination","why":"Dragon's Descent (R) transforms Shyvana, granting bonus health, an unstoppable dash, and empowered basic abilities. Land the dash to pin Maokai against a wall, then follow with an empowered Molten Burst (E) to finish the duel before Nature's Grasp (R) can turn the fight. This is a major power spike that turns you into a map-wide teamfight threat."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Spear of Shojin / Titanic Spike","why":"Completing your core item triggers a massive damage, health, and wave clear multiplier that completely overpowers traditional brawlers or isolated targets. Maokai's first item mostly buys health and cooldowns — his spike is durability, not damage, and it arrives early."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Juggernaut","why":"Shyvana transitions into a high-impact skirmish diver and juggernaut who relies on generating Fury and landing Dragon's Descent initiations. Your late-game damage leans on Dragon Form's empowered abilities, so coordinate with your team before diving for burst damage and be wary of Nature's Grasp (R) zoning your engage. Around high-tier objective pits, use Molten Burst (E)'s slow to cleanly win the smite duel against Maokai and secure the objective."}],"start":"Start your clear at the Raptor or Red Buff camp, using Emberstrike (Q) to strike the camp and its surrounding area and Inferno Aegis (W) for the shield and movement speed to keep the path fast. Maokai clears comfortably with Sap Magic sustain and saplings, and he is close to full health at every crossing.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using Molten Burst (E) to slow it and speed up the kill, establishing river control. You can solo early Dragons behind Scalemail's defensive bonuses if Maokai is visibly committed to a top-lane gank. Maokai contests scuttle with roots rather than damage — the crab goes to whoever he can hold in place.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the moment they spawn, using Emberstrike (Q) to clear them rapidly. Use Molten Burst (E) to slow and run down Maokai if he tries to contest, catching him off-guard mid-clear. Maokai is a slow objective taker but an excellent pit defender, since Nature's Grasp arrives before the fight does.","invade":"Walk boldly into Maokai's jungle the moment you confirm they are engaged with a low-health camp. Lead with your Molten Burst to mark them, popping Inferno Aegis to secure an inescapable high-damage melee duel. Maokai survives most invades on sustain alone, so an invade only pays if it takes the camp rather than the kill.","watch":"Watch for sudden counter-invades if you burn Inferno Aegis (W) early while clearing a camp, leaving you without its shield and movement speed against Twisted Advance (W)'s untargetable root gank. This leaves you without your main defensive tool to cleanly turn a fight around. Twisted Advance is the engage; while he is untargetable in it the root is already decided, so pre-move instead.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using wards to safely trace Maokai's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming to reach your item and Dragon's Descent power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable frontline threat. Maokai is weakest as a damage threat — he can hold you but rarely kills you without a laner arriving.","split":"You can split push exceptionally well due to your massive wave clear, pulling enemies to side lanes while you generate Fury. In teamfights, you must be the primary engager, utilizing Dragon's Descent to dive straight into the enemy formation and unleash your AoE damage. Maokai does not side lane meaningfully; his value is holding the river and the pits for his team.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies by launching your Dragon's Descent over walls to secure high-priority backline access. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely burning their frontline tanks and instantly shredding their backline carriers with empowered Molten Burst. Maokai picks are a sapling into a root at a choke, so break line of sight rather than walking the bush line.","win":"Generate an unanswerable early-to-mid game gold lead through successful power farming and absolute maximum dragon control. Close the match by relentlessly driving through teamfights in dragon form, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable juggernaut before they can scale their defensive items. Maokai wins by making the river his zone and starting every objective fight with a wall of roots."},"Nunu & Willump":{"tldr":"Nunu & Willump can knock you up with Biggest Snowball Ever! (W) and root you with Snowball Barrage (E) to set up ganks, so play around Inferno Aegis (W)'s shield and Molten Burst (E)'s slow to avoid getting locked down before you can respond. Shyvana out-clears and out-scales through Fury-fueled duels and Emberstrike (Q) auto-attack resets, then Dragon's Descent (R) turns her into a real dive threat. Nunu & Willump looks to snowball-gank your early tempo and set up Absolute Zero (R) before you hit your power spikes; you have no hard CC of your own outside Dragon Form.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Shyvana Favored","why":"Start with Inferno Aegis (W) for the shield, movement speed, and delayed explosion to get through the first camp quickly and safely. This gives you a fast, healthy path through your starting camps while Fury builds toward Emberstrike (Q)."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Shyvana Dominant","why":"Unlocking Emberstrike (Q) gives you an auto-attack reset that speeds up single-target clears and dueling. If Nunu & Willump makes an uncalculated early entry into your jungle, use Inferno Aegis (W) to stick to them before taking the duel and resetting your attacks with Q to shred their health bar. This backs up your early map pressure and punishes poor invades."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Shyvana Absolute Peak","why":"Access to Molten Burst (E) rounds out your dueling kit with a slowing fireball that explodes on large targets, letting you close on Nunu & Willump before they can wind up Biggest Snowball Ever! (W), or speed through high-health monsters. Landing the slow lets you turn most early brawls in your favor."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early farming thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive power-farming and dragon rushing. Keep constant tabs on Nunu & Willump's jungle clearing lines and only intercept them if you have a clear advantage or counter-gank opportunity. Prioritize reaching your level 6 power spike over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Dragon's Descent Domination","why":"Dragon's Descent (R) transforms you into a Dragon, granting bonus health, an unstoppable dash, and empowered basic abilities. Leap onto Nunu or Willump and follow up with empowered Molten Burst (E) for a fight-ending burst. Watch for Nunu & Willump's own Absolute Zero (R) channel — interrupt it if you can. This spike turns you into a real teamfight threat who can dictate the shape of a fight through raw damage."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Spear of Shojin / Titanic Spike","why":"Completing your core item triggers a massive damage, health, and wave clear multiplier that completely overpowers traditional brawlers or isolated targets. Nunu & Willump's first item mostly buys durability and cooldowns, so his spike is objective control rather than damage."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Juggernaut","why":"Shyvana becomes a high-impact skirmish diver who relies on generating Fury and landing Dragon's Descent initiations. Her late-game damage scaling is AoE-focused, so coordinate with your team before diving in, especially around Nunu & Willump's Absolute Zero (R) zone control. Use Molten Burst's slow to control the contesting jungler around objective pits and secure the takedown."}],"start":"Start your clear at the Raptor or Red Buff camp, using Inferno Aegis (W)'s shield and delayed detonation to get through camps quickly and safely. Nunu & Willump clears fast with Consume and never drops below half health, so his early map presence is constant.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using Molten Burst (E) to slow and chip it down for a clean contest. You can solo early Dragons safely behind your Scalemail passive resistances if Nunu & Willump is confirmed away ganking top lane. Nunu & Willump does not win a scuttle duel, but Consume cheats the crab out from under you in a race.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the moment they spawn, using Inferno Aegis (W) to clear them quickly. Use Molten Burst (E) to slow and punish Nunu & Willump if they contest, catching them mid-clear. Nunu & Willump is the best objective thief in the game — never let a pit reach smite range with him nearby.","invade":"Walk into Nunu & Willump's jungle once you confirm they are committed to a low-health camp. Lead with Molten Burst (E) to slow them, then use Inferno Aegis (W)'s shield and burst to win the duel before Willump can root you with Snowball Barrage (E). Nunu & Willump loses almost any invade fought honestly, since the kit is gank pressure rather than duel pressure.","watch":"Watch for counter-invades if you burn Inferno Aegis (W) early while clearing a camp — you lose its shield and movement speed and have no other escape or engage tool until it's back up, which is exactly when a Biggest Snowball Ever! (W) knockup can catch you out of position. The snowball is the tell — a rolling Nunu is committed, and stepping out of the line takes the knock-up away.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using wards to safely trace Nunu & Willump's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming to reach your item and Dragon's Descent power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable frontline threat. Nunu & Willump is weakest in a straight 1v1 at every stage of the game, which is why he never wants one.","split":"You can split push exceptionally well due to your massive wave clear, pulling enemies to side lanes while you generate Fury. In teamfights, you must be the primary engager, utilizing Dragon's Descent to dive straight into the enemy formation and unleash your AoE damage. Nunu & Willump does not side lane; he shadows objectives and waits for a smite fight he can cheat.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies by launching your Dragon's Descent over walls to secure high-priority backline access. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely burning their frontline tanks and instantly shredding their backline carriers with empowered Molten Burst. Nunu & Willump picks come from a long snowball out of fog, so cross river where the roll cannot build.","win":"Generate an unanswerable early-to-mid game gold lead through successful power farming and absolute maximum dragon control. Close the match by relentlessly driving through teamfights in dragon form, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable juggernaut before they can scale their defensive items. Nunu & Willump wins by stealing objectives and channelling Absolute Zero into a fight nobody can leave."},"Rammus":{"tldr":"Rammus can taunt you into his Defensive Ball Curl damage reflection and slow you with Powerball (Q), so respect Inferno Aegis (W)'s shield and Molten Burst (E)'s slow rather than auto-attacking blind into his stacked resistances. Shyvana's Fury-fueled clear speed and Emberstrike (Q) true damage cut through Rammus's armor/MR stacking, and Dragon's Descent (R) — a real hard-CC dash — turns her into a serious dive threat at 6. Rammus looks to taunt-gank your early tempo before you hit your power spikes; you have no hard CC of your own outside Dragon Form.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Shyvana Favored","why":"Start with Inferno Aegis (W) for the shield, movement speed, and delayed explosion to get through the first camp quickly and safely. This gives you a fast, healthy path through your starting camps while Fury builds toward Emberstrike (Q)."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Shyvana Dominant","why":"Unlocking Emberstrike (Q) gives you an auto-attack reset that speeds up single-target clears and dueling, and its true damage bypasses whatever resistances Rammus has stacked. If Rammus makes an uncalculated early entry into your jungle — Powerball (Q) only slows on impact, it doesn't knock back — use Inferno Aegis (W) to stick to him before taking the duel and resetting your attacks with Q to shred his health bar. This backs up your early map pressure and punishes poor invades."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Shyvana Absolute Peak","why":"Access to Molten Burst (E) rounds out your dueling kit with a slowing fireball that explodes on large targets, letting you keep Rammus from closing distance with Powerball or speed through high-health monsters. Landing the slow lets you turn most early brawls in your favor."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early farming thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive power-farming and dragon rushing. Keep constant tabs on Rammus's jungle clearing lines and only intercept them if you have a clear advantage or counter-gank opportunity. Prioritize reaching your level 6 power spike over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Dragon's Descent Domination","why":"Dragon's Descent (R) transforms you into a Dragon, granting bonus health, an unstoppable dash, and empowered basic abilities, giving you a hard-CC engage of your own. Leap onto Rammus and follow up with empowered Molten Burst (E) for a fight-ending burst. This spike turns you into a real teamfight threat who can dictate the shape of a fight through raw damage."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Spear of Shojin / Titanic Spike","why":"Completing your core item triggers a massive damage, health, and wave clear multiplier that completely overpowers traditional brawlers or isolated targets. Rammus' first item is an armour spike more than a damage one, which is exactly what makes him harder to fight."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Juggernaut","why":"Shyvana becomes a high-impact skirmish diver who relies on generating Fury and landing Dragon's Descent initiations. Her late-game damage scaling is AoE-focused, so coordinate with your team before diving in — Emberstrike's true damage still helps against a fully stacked Rammus. Use Molten Burst's slow to control the contesting jungler around objective pits and secure the takedown."}],"start":"Start your clear at the Raptor or Red Buff camp, using Inferno Aegis (W)'s shield and delayed detonation to get through camps quickly and safely. Rammus clears steadily off Defensive Ball Curl and Frenzying Taunt, and he takes very little damage doing it.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using Molten Burst (E) to slow and chip it down for a clean contest — watch for Rammus rolling in with Powerball (Q), which slows on impact but does not knock back. You can solo early Dragons safely behind your Scalemail passive resistances if Rammus is confirmed away ganking top lane. Rammus contests scuttle by rolling in and taunting — the crab is decided by whether you can leave, not by damage.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the moment they spawn, using Inferno Aegis (W) to clear them quickly. Use Molten Burst (E) to slow and punish Rammus if he contests, catching him mid-clear. Rammus takes objectives slowly but defends a pit well, since a taunt at smite range ends the contest.","invade":"Walk into Rammus's jungle once you confirm he is committed to a low-health camp. Lead with Molten Burst (E) to slow him, then use Inferno Aegis (W)'s shield and Emberstrike (Q) true damage to punch through Defensive Ball Curl's stacked resistances and win the duel. Rammus is a bad invade target for any auto-attack champion, because the reflect turns your damage back on you.","watch":"Watch for counter-invades if you burn Inferno Aegis (W) early while clearing a camp — you lose its shield and movement speed and have no other escape or engage tool until it's back up, which is exactly when Frenzying Taunt (E) can lock you into a bad fight. While the ball is curled, stop attacking — every auto you throw into it is damage you take instead.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using wards to safely trace Rammus's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming to reach your item and Dragon's Descent power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable frontline threat. Rammus is weakest against ability damage and against anything that does not need to auto-attack him at all.","split":"You can split push exceptionally well due to your massive wave clear, pulling enemies to side lanes while you generate Fury. In teamfights, you must be the primary engager, utilizing Dragon's Descent to dive straight into the enemy formation and unleash your AoE damage. Rammus does not side lane; he rolls between objectives and lanes where the taunt has follow-up.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies by launching your Dragon's Descent over walls to secure high-priority backline access. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely burning their frontline tanks and instantly shredding their backline carriers with empowered Molten Burst. Rammus picks are a Powerball out of fog into a taunt, so watch for the roll starting long before it reaches you.","win":"Generate an unanswerable early-to-mid game gold lead through successful power farming and absolute maximum dragon control. Close the match by relentlessly driving through teamfights in dragon form, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable juggernaut before they can scale their defensive items. Rammus wins by locking down the person your team most needs held and surviving everything sent at him."},"Rek'Sai":{"tldr":"Rek'Sai can burrow past wards to gank and knock you up with Un-burrow (W), then finish with true-damage Furious Bite (E), so respect Inferno Aegis (W)'s shield and Molten Burst (E)'s slow rather than trading blind. Shyvana's Fury-fueled clear speed and Emberstrike (Q) auto-attack resets let her out-farm and out-duel Rek'Sai early, and Dragon's Descent (R) turns her into a real dive threat at 6. Rek'Sai looks to tunnel-gank your early tempo and set up Void Rush (R) executes before you hit your power spikes; you have no hard CC of your own outside Dragon Form.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Shyvana Favored","why":"Start with Inferno Aegis (W) for the shield, movement speed, and delayed explosion to get through the first camp quickly and safely. This gives you a fast, healthy path through your starting camps while Fury builds toward Emberstrike (Q)."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Shyvana Dominant","why":"Unlocking Emberstrike (Q) gives you an auto-attack reset that speeds up single-target clears and dueling. If Rek'Sai makes an uncalculated early entry into your jungle, use Inferno Aegis (W) to stick to her before taking the duel and resetting your attacks with Q to shred her health bar. This backs up your early map pressure and punishes poor invades."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Shyvana Absolute Peak","why":"Access to Molten Burst (E) rounds out your dueling kit with a slowing fireball that explodes on large targets, letting you cut off Rek'Sai's Burrow mobility or speed through high-health monsters. Landing the slow lets you turn most early brawls in your favor."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early farming thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive power-farming and dragon rushing. Keep constant tabs on Rek'Sai's jungle clearing lines and only intercept them if you have a clear advantage or counter-gank opportunity. Prioritize reaching your level 6 power spike over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Dragon's Descent Domination","why":"Dragon's Descent (R) transforms you into a Dragon, granting bonus health, an unstoppable dash, and empowered basic abilities. Leap onto Rek'Sai before she can Void Rush away and follow up with empowered Molten Burst (E) for a fight-ending burst. This spike turns you into a real teamfight threat who can dictate the shape of a fight through raw damage."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Spear of Shojin / Titanic Spike","why":"Completing your core item triggers a massive damage, health, and wave clear multiplier that completely overpowers traditional brawlers or isolated targets. Rek'Sai's first item is a real jump in duel power, but it is also close to the end of her scaling curve."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Juggernaut","why":"Shyvana becomes a high-impact skirmish diver who relies on generating Fury and landing Dragon's Descent initiations. Her late-game damage scaling is AoE-focused, so coordinate with your team before diving in — Rek'Sai's Void Rush (R) can still pick off an isolated ally. Use Molten Burst's slow to control the contesting jungler around objective pits and secure the takedown."}],"start":"Start your clear at the Raptor or Red Buff camp, using Inferno Aegis (W)'s shield and delayed detonation to get through camps quickly and safely. Track Rek'Sai's starting camp and clear direction where you can, so you can path efficiently toward your level 4 full clear. Rek'Sai clears fast on Fury-fuelled attacks and Furious Bite, and her tunnels make her map speed better than her clear suggests.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using Molten Burst (E) to slow and chip it down for a clean contest. You can solo early Dragons safely behind your Scalemail passive resistances if Rek'Sai is confirmed away ganking top lane. Rek'Sai wins scuttle contests off an Unburrow knock-up, which lands from a direction the crab fight does not expect.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the moment they spawn, using Inferno Aegis (W) to clear them quickly. Use Molten Burst (E) to slow and punish Rek'Sai if she contests, catching her mid-clear. Rek'Sai is a strong objective duellist because Furious Bite is true damage and Void Rush executes the contest.","invade":"Walk into Rek'Sai's jungle once you confirm she is committed to a low-health camp. Lead with Molten Burst (E) to slow her, then use Inferno Aegis (W)'s shield and burst to win the duel before her Furious Bite (E) true damage stacks up at max Fury. Rek'Sai invades through her own tunnel network, so the entrances she has dug are the map you actually need warded.","watch":"Watch for Rek'Sai tunneling in from an unwarded angle if you burn Inferno Aegis (W) early while clearing a camp — you lose its shield and movement speed and have no other engage or disengage tool until it's back up, and Molten Burst's slow is your only answer to her Void Rush (R) escape or execute. Burrowed Rek'Sai shows tremors, not a champion — if she is burrowed near you the knock-up is already in range.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using wards to safely trace Rek'Sai's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming to reach your item and Dragon's Descent power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable frontline threat. Rek'Sai is weakest late, when the early tunnel pressure stops mattering and her damage falls behind the carries.","split":"You can split push exceptionally well due to your massive wave clear, pulling enemies to side lanes while you generate Fury. In teamfights, you must be the primary engager, utilizing Dragon's Descent to dive straight into the enemy formation and unleash your AoE damage. Rek'Sai side lanes to keep tunnel pressure rather than to push, so the wave is rarely why she is there.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies by launching your Dragon's Descent over walls to secure high-priority backline access. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely burning their frontline tanks and instantly shredding their backline carriers with empowered Molten Burst. Rek'Sai picks are an Unburrow from an unwarded tunnel, so sweep the exits rather than the bushes.","win":"Generate an unanswerable early-to-mid game gold lead through successful power farming and absolute maximum dragon control. Close the match by relentlessly driving through teamfights in dragon form, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable juggernaut before they can scale their defensive items. Rek'Sai wins by converting an early tunnel-tempo lead into objectives before her scaling runs out."},"Sejuani":{"tldr":"Sejuani adjusts your early mechanical tempo by requiring high precision over Molten Burst (E) slows and Inferno Aegis (W) shield timing. Shyvana commands skirmish velocities through Emberstrike (Q) auto-attack resets and true damage, deploying Dragon's Descent (R) to force fights once she has Fury. Sejuani seeks to knock you up with Arctic Assault, freeze and stun you with Permafrost once you're Frost-stacked, and land Glacial Prison to lock down a duel before you build Fury.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Shyvana Favored","why":"Shyvana's Emberstrike (Q) hits both the target and the surrounding area with true damage on her next attack, and Molten Burst (E) explodes on large monsters, giving her one of the fastest, healthiest clears in the jungle. Sejuani's clear leans on Winter's Wrath (W) and has no true-damage or AoE tool this early, so Shyvana's opening camps go faster and healthier."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Shyvana Dominant","why":"The recast on Emberstrike (Q) gives you an auto-attack reset that accelerates your single-target clear and dueling potential. If Sejuani attempts an uncalculated early entry into your jungle, activate Inferno Aegis (W) for the shield and speed to stick to her before taking a close-quarters duel using your Q reset to shatter her health bar — she has no hard CC until Permafrost stacks up at level 3. This secures your early map pressure and punishes poor invades."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Shyvana Absolute Peak","why":"Access to Molten Burst (E) completes your dueling kit with a fireball that explodes on large targets and slows them. Use it to keep Sejuani in range for a duel or burst through large monsters to accelerate your clear speed. This is also the level Sejuani unlocks Permafrost, so if you let her stack Frost on you first, the duel window swings back toward her."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early farming thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive power-farming and dragon rushing. Keep constant tabs on Sejuani's jungle clearing lines and only intercept them if you have a clear advantage or counter-gank opportunity. Prioritize reaching your level 6 power spike over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Even","why":"Dragon's Descent (R) transforms Shyvana into Dragon Form with empowered basics and a dash that scatters enemies in her path, but Sejuani also comes online at 6 with Glacial Prison, a long-range stun-and-slow-zone ultimate that can lock Shyvana down before she closes the gap. Whoever lands their engage first controls this window, so it reads as an even, priority-dependent breakpoint rather than a Shyvana blowout."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Spear of Shojin / Titanic Spike","why":"Completing your core item triggers a massive damage, health, and wave clear multiplier that completely overpowers traditional brawlers or isolated targets. Sejuani's first item is a durability spike — she does not start killing you, she starts being impossible to remove."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Juggernaut","why":"Shyvana transitions into a high-impact skirmish diver and unstoppable juggernaut who relies entirely on generating Fury and landing massive Dragon's Descent initiations. Your late-game raw damage scaling shifts into a purely AoE-focused line, making it imperative to coordinate with your team before diving into extreme burst damage. Maintain extreme technical precision around high-tier objective pits to cleanly melt the contesting smiter with your E mark and secure the objective."}],"start":"Start your clear at the Raptor or Red Buff camp, using Inferno Aegis (W) for its shield and burst explosion against grouped monsters to keep your clear healthy. Sejuani clears steadily with Winter's Wrath and Fury of the North keeps her health high, so she paths without recalling.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using Molten Burst (E) to slow it and drop its health bar for river control. You can solo early Dragons using your Scalemail passive resistance stacks if Sejuani is visible ganking top lane. Sejuani contests scuttle with Permafrost stuns rather than damage — she wins the crab when a laner is with her.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the exact moment they spawn, using your Inferno Aegis AoE to clear them rapidly. Use your E to mark and run down Sejuani if they dare to contest, catching them off-guard mid-clear. Sejuani takes objectives slowly but holds a pit very well, since Glacial Prison starts the fight on her terms.","invade":"Walk boldly into Sejuani's jungle the moment you confirm she is engaged with a low-health camp and her Frost Armor is down from taking damage. Lead with Molten Burst to slow her, popping Inferno Aegis for the shield and speed to close a melee duel before she can land Arctic Assault. Sejuani survives an invade far better than she wins one; expect her to walk out rather than die in it.","watch":"Watch for sudden counter-invades if you exhaust Inferno Aegis early while clearing a camp, leaving you without its shield and speed right as Sejuani's Arctic Assault can knock you up into a Permafrost stun or a Glacial Prison lockdown. Her passive frost armour is the tell — a Sejuani who has not taken damage recently is much harder to burst.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using wards to safely trace Sejuani's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming to reach your item and Dragon's Descent power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable frontline threat. Sejuani is weakest as a solo damage threat, since almost everything she does needs a teammate to finish.","split":"You can split push exceptionally well due to your massive wave clear, pulling enemies to side lanes while you generate Fury. In teamfights, you must be the primary engager, utilizing Dragon's Descent to dive straight into the enemy formation and unleash your AoE damage. Sejuani does not side lane; she plays the river and the pits, where a cross-map stun is worth more than a wave.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies by launching your Dragon's Descent over walls to secure high-priority backline access. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, burning through their frontline tanks and shredding their backline carriers with empowered Molten Burst in Dragon Form. Sejuani picks are a long Glacial Prison from fog, so keep terrain between you and the direction she disappeared.","win":"Generate an unanswerable early-to-mid game gold lead through successful power farming and absolute maximum dragon control. Close the match by relentlessly driving through teamfights in dragon form, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable juggernaut before they can scale their defensive items. Sejuani wins by landing one cross-map stun into a fight her team is already positioned for."},"Skarner":{"tldr":"Skarner adjusts your early mechanical tempo by requiring high precision over Molten Burst (E) slows and Inferno Aegis (W) shield timing. Shyvana commands skirmish velocities through Emberstrike (Q) auto-attack resets and true damage, deploying Dragon's Descent (R) to force fights once she has Fury. Skarner seeks to slam you into a wall with Ixtal's Impact, ground your dashes with Shattered Earth, and out-tank the trade before you build Fury.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Shyvana Favored","why":"Start with Emberstrike (Q) for a true-damage attack that hits both the target and the surrounding area, and use Inferno Aegis (W) for its shield to keep your clear healthy. Skarner's mana-based kit has no AoE clear tool this early, giving Shyvana the faster opening."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Shyvana Dominant","why":"The recast on Emberstrike (Q) gives you an auto-attack reset that accelerates your single-target clear and dueling potential. If Skarner attempts an uncalculated early entry into your jungle, activate Inferno Aegis (W) for the shield and speed to stick to him before taking a close-quarters duel using your Q reset to shatter his health bar. This secures your early map pressure and punishes poor invades."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Shyvana Absolute Peak","why":"Access to Molten Burst (E) completes your dueling kit with a fireball that explodes on large targets and slows them. Use it to keep Skarner from closing with Ixtal's Impact, or burst through large monsters to accelerate your clear speed."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early farming thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive power-farming and dragon rushing. Keep constant tabs on Skarner's jungle clearing lines and only intercept them if you have a clear advantage or counter-gank opportunity. Prioritize reaching your level 6 power spike over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Dragon's Descent Domination","why":"Dragon's Descent (R) transforms Shyvana into Dragon Form with empowered basics and a dash-leap. That dash is not truly unstoppable, though — Skarner's Shattered Earth (Q) grounds dashes and can shut it down if he lands it first, and his own Impale (R) is a suppress that can lock Shyvana down in return. Land Dragon's Descent before he grounds you, and follow with empowered Molten Burst for the duel."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Spear of Shojin / Titanic Spike","why":"Completing your core item triggers a massive damage, health, and wave clear multiplier that completely overpowers traditional brawlers or isolated targets. Skarner's first item buys the health and cooldowns that let him survive the answer to his own engage."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Juggernaut","why":"Shyvana transitions into a high-impact skirmish diver and unstoppable juggernaut who relies entirely on generating Fury and landing massive Dragon's Descent initiations. Your late-game raw damage scaling shifts into a purely AoE-focused line, making it imperative to coordinate with your team before diving into extreme burst damage. Maintain extreme technical precision around high-tier objective pits to cleanly melt the contesting smiter with your E mark and secure the objective."}],"start":"Start your clear at the Raptor or Red Buff camp, using Inferno Aegis (W) for its shield and burst explosion against grouped monsters to keep your clear healthy. Skarner clears comfortably with boulder poke and Seismic Bastion shields, and he keeps his health through the whole path.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using Molten Burst (E) to slow it and drop its health bar for river control. You can solo early Dragons using your Scalemail passive resistance stacks if Skarner is visible ganking top lane. Skarner contests scuttle with the wall stun — near terrain the crab fight is his, and in the open it is not.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the exact moment they spawn, using Inferno Aegis (W) to shield through their damage while you clear. Use Molten Burst (E) to slow and run down Skarner if he dares to contest, catching him off-guard mid-clear. Skarner holds a pit well because Impale removes whoever steps up to contest the smite.","invade":"Walk boldly into Skarner's jungle the moment you confirm they are engaged with a low-health camp. Lead with your Molten Burst to mark them, popping Inferno Aegis to secure an inescapable high-damage melee duel. Skarner is durable enough to survive an invade but slow enough that he cannot punish one that leaves.","watch":"Watch for sudden counter-invades if you exhaust Inferno Aegis early while clearing a camp, leaving you without its shield and speed. Also track Skarner's Shattered Earth (Q), which grounds and shuts off dashes — that can deny Dragon's Descent's leap if he lands it first. Terrain is the tell — Skarner near a wall has a stun, and Skarner in the open river has a slow and nothing else.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using wards to safely trace Skarner's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming to reach your item and Dragon's Descent power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable frontline threat. Skarner is weakest in open ground, where Ixtal's Impact cannot connect anyone to a wall.","split":"You can split push exceptionally well due to your massive wave clear, pulling enemies to side lanes while you generate Fury. In teamfights, you must be the primary engager, utilizing Dragon's Descent to dive straight into the enemy formation and unleash your AoE damage. Skarner does not side lane; he plays the pits, where a suppression on the right target decides the objective.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies by launching your Dragon's Descent over walls to secure high-priority backline access. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, burning through their frontline tanks and shredding their backline carriers with empowered Molten Burst in Dragon Form. Skarner picks are an Impale on whoever walks a corridor first, so never lead a rotation through a choke.","win":"Generate an unanswerable early-to-mid game gold lead through successful power farming and absolute maximum dragon control. Close the match by relentlessly driving through teamfights in dragon form, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable juggernaut before they can scale their defensive items. Skarner wins by dragging one key target into his team at the start of every objective fight."},"Volibear":{"tldr":"Volibear's Thundering Smash (Q) is a stun-on-hit gap closer with no level gate, so his gank and invade threat is live from level 1, not just after 6. Shyvana clears with Emberstrike (Q) resets and Inferno Aegis (W) AoE, and Molten Burst (E) slows him from range before he can close with Thundering Smash. Both champions have hard CC in this matchup — Volibear's stun and Shyvana's Dragon's Descent — so the fight comes down to who lands their engage first.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Shyvana Favored","why":"Start with Inferno Aegis (W) to secure a fast area-of-effect clear, utilizing the movement speed and continuous magic damage to melt the camp. Execute basic attacks to extend Inferno Aegis's duration, ensuring maximum efficiency. This establishes an exceptionally fast and healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Shyvana Dominant","why":"Unlocking Emberstrike (Q) provides an impactful auto-attack reset that drastically accelerates your single-target clear and dueling potential. If Volibear attempts an uncalculated early entry into your jungle, activate Inferno Aegis to stick to them before taking an aggressive close-quarters duel using your Q reset to shatter their health bar. This completely secures your early map pressure and punishes poor invades."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Shyvana Absolute Peak","why":"Access to Molten Burst (E) completes your lethal dueling portfolio by introducing a versatile fireball that marks enemies for percent-health damage. Use this tool to instantly secure your damage trades or melt through high-health monsters to accelerate your clear speed. This grants an elite operational flexibility allowing you to turn almost any early brawl if you land your mark accurately."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early farming thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive power-farming and dragon rushing. Keep constant tabs on Volibear's jungle clearing lines and only intercept them if you have a clear advantage or counter-gank opportunity. Prioritize reaching your level 6 power spike over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Dragon's Descent Domination","why":"Dragon's Descent (R) introduces an immense combat transformation that provides massive bonus health, an unstoppable dash, and empowered area-of-effect abilities. Execute a precise ultimate activation to drag Volibear into a wall, unleashing your empowered Molten Burst for a clean kill. This massive mechanical spike turns you into an absolute map-wide teamfight threat who dictates the shape of every fight through raw damage."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Spear of Shojin / Titanic Spike","why":"Completing your core item triggers a massive damage, health, and wave clear multiplier that completely overpowers traditional brawlers or isolated targets. Volibear's first item is where the bite trades stop being even and start ending with your health bar gone."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Juggernaut","why":"Shyvana transitions into a high-impact skirmish diver and unstoppable juggernaut who relies entirely on generating Fury and landing massive Dragon's Descent initiations. Your late-game raw damage scaling shifts into a purely AoE-focused line, making it imperative to coordinate with your team before diving into extreme burst damage. Maintain extreme technical precision around high-tier objective pits to cleanly melt the contesting smiter with your E mark and secure the objective."}],"start":"Start your clear at the Raptor or Red Buff camp, using Inferno Aegis's AoE detonation to speed up the clear. Volibear clears fast off Frenzied Maul healing and the passive attack speed, and he leaves camps close to full.","scuttle":"Contest the scuttle crab as it spawns, but respect Thundering Smash's range — if Volibear lands the stun first he can follow with Frenzied Maul's heal and win the extended fight. If he's committed to a top-lane gank, that's a window to solo an early Dragon behind Scalemail's stacked resistances. Volibear takes most scuttle contests — the stun into the maul bite pattern is one of the better level-three duels.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the moment they spawn; Inferno Aegis clears them quickly. If Volibear contests, use Molten Burst's slow at range rather than closing into his Thundering Smash stun. Volibear is a strong objective duellist and can disable a tower afterwards, so a won pit fight costs you twice.","invade":"Invade only when you can confirm Thundering Smash is down — landing it during your invade flips the trade in his favor. Lead with Molten Burst to slow him, then use Inferno Aegis's shield and speed to close out the duel. Volibear wins most invades fought straight, and the Q stun means walking away from him is not simple.","watch":"Watch for Thundering Smash on any approach — it's a stun with no cooldown-tier level gate, so Volibear can punish you for walking into range at any point in the game. Thundering Smash has to run at you in a straight line, so a wall or a camp between you removes the stun.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, give up contested scuttle crabs and ward to trace Volibear's routes rather than fight him blind. Prioritize farming to your first item and level 6 so Dragon's Descent's tankiness and true damage let you re-enter fights. Volibear is weakest against ranged kiting, since he has no way to close a gap before six.","split":"You can split push exceptionally well due to your massive wave clear, pulling enemies to side lanes while you generate Fury. In teamfights, you must be the primary engager, utilizing Dragon's Descent to dive straight into the enemy formation and unleash your AoE damage. Volibear side lanes strongly and takes towers fast, which makes ignoring the wave expensive.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies by launching your Dragon's Descent over walls to secure high-priority backline access. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely burning their frontline tanks and instantly shredding their backline carriers with empowered Molten Burst. Volibear picks are a Stormbringer leap onto a group, so spread off the landing marker rather than running from it.","win":"Build a gold and Dragon-control lead by out-clearing Volibear and slowing him off Thundering Smash before he can close. Close games by forcing fights in Dragon Form, where your true-damage Q and zone control outlast his sustain from Frenzied Maul. Volibear wins by brawling through the mid game and using the ultimate to take towers and objectives on the same trip."},"Warwick":{"tldr":"Warwick's Jaws of the Beast (Q) deals percent-max-health damage and heals him, which punishes a high-HP target like Shyvana in a straight duel. His Blood Hunt (W) gives him a speed boost to chase anything under 50% health, and Infinite Duress (R) is a point-blank suppression once he hits 6. Shyvana answers with Emberstrike (Q) resets and Molten Burst (E) to slow him before he closes, since her own true damage on Q ignores the sustain Warwick's passive and Q provide.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Shyvana Favored","why":"Start with Inferno Aegis (W) to secure a fast area-of-effect clear, utilizing the movement speed and continuous magic damage to melt the camp. Execute basic attacks to extend Inferno Aegis's duration, ensuring maximum efficiency. This establishes an exceptionally fast and healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Shyvana Dominant","why":"Unlocking Emberstrike (Q) provides an impactful auto-attack reset that drastically accelerates your single-target clear and dueling potential. If Warwick attempts an uncalculated early entry into your jungle, activate Inferno Aegis to stick to them before taking an aggressive close-quarters duel using your Q reset to shatter their health bar. This completely secures your early map pressure and punishes poor invades."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Shyvana Absolute Peak","why":"Access to Molten Burst (E) completes your lethal dueling portfolio by introducing a versatile fireball that marks enemies for percent-health damage. Use this tool to instantly secure your damage trades or melt through high-health monsters to accelerate your clear speed. This grants an elite operational flexibility allowing you to turn almost any early brawl if you land your mark accurately."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early farming thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive power-farming and dragon rushing. Keep constant tabs on Warwick's jungle clearing lines and only intercept them if you have a clear advantage or counter-gank opportunity. Prioritize reaching your level 6 power spike over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Dragon's Descent Domination","why":"Dragon's Descent (R) introduces an immense combat transformation that provides massive bonus health, an unstoppable dash, and empowered area-of-effect abilities. Execute a precise ultimate activation to drag Warwick into a wall, unleashing your empowered Molten Burst for a clean kill. This massive mechanical spike turns you into an absolute map-wide teamfight threat who dictates the shape of every fight through raw damage."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Spear of Shojin / Titanic Spike","why":"Completing your core item triggers a massive damage, health, and wave clear multiplier that completely overpowers traditional brawlers or isolated targets. Warwick's first item is where the healing stops being sustain and starts being more than your damage can out-pace."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Juggernaut","why":"Shyvana transitions into a high-impact skirmish diver and unstoppable juggernaut who relies entirely on generating Fury and landing massive Dragon's Descent initiations. Your late-game raw damage scaling shifts into a purely AoE-focused line, making it imperative to coordinate with your team before diving into extreme burst damage. Maintain extreme technical precision around high-tier objective pits to cleanly melt the contesting smiter with your E mark and secure the objective."}],"start":"Start your clear at the Raptor or Red Buff camp, using Inferno Aegis's AoE detonation to speed up the clear. Warwick's Eternal Hunger sustains him below 50% health, so track his route rather than assume you'll simply out-heal him. Warwick clears fast and heals through everything off Eternal Hunger, so he is at full health at every crossing.","scuttle":"Contest the scuttle crab as it spawns, but be careful going low — Blood Hunt gives Warwick bonus speed and attack speed against anyone under 50% health. If he's committed to a top-lane gank, that's a window to solo an early Dragon behind Scalemail's stacked resistances. Contest scuttle against Warwick from full health, since his sustain out-lasts what most junglers can put out at that timer.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the moment they spawn; Inferno Aegis clears them quickly. If Warwick contests, keep him at range with Molten Burst's slow rather than trading Q for Q, since his heal-back on Jaws of the Beast scales with your health. Warwick is a strong objective duellist with the Q percent-health damage and can hold the pit through a long fight.","invade":"Invade while Warwick is mid-camp and above 50% health, so Blood Hunt isn't giving him bonus speed to escape or counter-engage. Lead with Molten Burst to slow him, then use Inferno Aegis's shield and speed to close out the trade quickly. Invade Warwick above fifty percent health, since below it his sustain and Blood Hunt win the fight your invade was meant to end.","watch":"Watch for Infinite Duress the moment Warwick hits 6 — it's a suppression that locks you down at melee range, so don't get caught alone with no escape once he has it up. Blood Hunt tracks anyone below half; if your health drops he gets speed toward you and you will not out-run it.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, give up contested scuttle crabs and ward to trace Warwick's routes, especially once your team is under 50% health and vulnerable to Blood Hunt. Prioritize farming to your first item and level 6 so Dragon's Descent's tankiness and true damage let you re-enter fights. Warwick is weakest above fifty percent health, where the tracking does not apply and his damage is much lower.","split":"You can split push exceptionally well due to your massive wave clear, pulling enemies to side lanes while you generate Fury. In teamfights, you must be the primary engager, utilizing Dragon's Descent to dive straight into the enemy formation and unleash your AoE damage. Warwick side lanes adequately but his real value is answering any low-health fight anywhere on the map.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies by launching your Dragon's Descent over walls to secure high-priority backline access. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely burning their frontline tanks and instantly shredding their backline carriers with empowered Molten Burst. Warwick picks are an Infinite Duress onto whoever is lowest, so recall earlier than feels necessary against him.","win":"Build a gold and Dragon-control lead by out-clearing Warwick and avoiding low-health duels where his passive and Q sustain flip the fight. Close games by forcing fights in Dragon Form, where your true damage on Q cuts through his healing and his Infinite Duress can be baited onto a teammate instead. Warwick wins by suppressing a carry for the length of a fight while out-healing everything sent back at him."},"Zac":{"tldr":"Zac's Stretching Strikes (Q) is a knockup pull with no level gate — it's his gank and invade tool from level 1. Unstable Matter (W) deals percent-max-health damage that punishes a high-HP target like Shyvana, and Cell Division lets him revive as bloblets if he dies with fragments on the field. Shyvana answers with Emberstrike (Q) resets, whose true damage ignores his health-percent burst, and Molten Burst (E) to slow him before he can land the Q pull.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Shyvana Favored","why":"Start with Inferno Aegis (W) to secure a fast area-of-effect clear, utilizing the movement speed and continuous magic damage to melt the camp. Execute basic attacks to extend Inferno Aegis's duration, ensuring maximum efficiency. This establishes an exceptionally fast and healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Shyvana Dominant","why":"Unlocking Emberstrike (Q) provides an impactful auto-attack reset that drastically accelerates your single-target clear and dueling potential. If Zac attempts an uncalculated early entry into your jungle, activate Inferno Aegis to stick to them before taking an aggressive close-quarters duel using your Q reset to shatter their health bar. This completely secures your early map pressure and punishes poor invades."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Shyvana Absolute Peak","why":"Access to Molten Burst (E) completes your lethal dueling portfolio by introducing a versatile fireball that marks enemies for percent-health damage. Use this tool to instantly secure your damage trades or melt through high-health monsters to accelerate your clear speed. This grants an elite operational flexibility allowing you to turn almost any early brawl if you land your mark accurately."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early farming thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive power-farming and dragon rushing. Keep constant tabs on Zac's jungle clearing lines and only intercept them if you have a clear advantage or counter-gank opportunity. Prioritize reaching your level 6 power spike over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Dragon's Descent Domination","why":"Dragon's Descent (R) introduces an immense combat transformation that provides massive bonus health, an unstoppable dash, and empowered area-of-effect abilities. Execute a precise ultimate activation to drag Zac into a wall, unleashing your empowered Molten Burst for a clean kill. This massive mechanical spike turns you into an absolute map-wide teamfight threat who dictates the shape of every fight through raw damage."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Spear of Shojin / Titanic Spike","why":"Completing your core item triggers a massive damage, health, and wave clear multiplier that completely overpowers traditional brawlers or isolated targets. Zac's first item is a durability spike, so he does not start killing you — he starts surviving whatever you send."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Juggernaut","why":"Shyvana transitions into a high-impact skirmish diver and unstoppable juggernaut who relies entirely on generating Fury and landing massive Dragon's Descent initiations. Your late-game raw damage scaling shifts into a purely AoE-focused line, making it imperative to coordinate with your team before diving into extreme burst damage. Maintain extreme technical precision around high-tier objective pits to cleanly melt the contesting smiter with your E mark and secure the objective."}],"start":"Start your clear at the Raptor or Red Buff camp, using Inferno Aegis's AoE detonation to speed up the clear. Zac clears steadily with blob sustain and Unstable Matter, and he never has to recall for health before six.","scuttle":"Contest the scuttle crab as it spawns, but stay alert for Stretching Strikes — if Zac lands the pull he can throw you into the crab or himself for extra damage. If he's committed to a top-lane gank, that's a window to solo an early Dragon behind Scalemail's stacked resistances. Zac contests scuttle with a Slingshot from fog rather than a duel — the crab goes to whoever gets knocked up.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the moment they spawn; Inferno Aegis clears them quickly. If Zac contests, watch his Q cooldown before committing — Molten Burst's slow helps you close if it's down. Zac takes objectives slowly but survives a pit fight better than most, and the passive blob buys a second attempt.","invade":"Invade only when you can confirm Stretching Strikes is down — landing it flips a bad position for Zac into a good one. Lead with Molten Burst to slow him, then use Inferno Aegis's shield to soak the percent-health burst from Unstable Matter. Zac loses a straight invade but is hard to kill in one, so an invade against him usually costs more time than it earns.","watch":"Watch for Stretching Strikes on any approach — it's a knockup pull with no cooldown-tier level gate, so Zac can punish you for walking into range at any point in the game. The Slingshot charge is visible from far away — ward the walls he charges from and the engage never lands.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, give up contested scuttle crabs and ward to trace Zac's routes rather than fight him blind. Prioritize farming to your first item and level 6 so Dragon's Descent's tankiness and true damage let you re-enter fights. Zac is weakest when his E is on cooldown and his passive blob has already been used, which is a long window.","split":"You can split push exceptionally well due to your massive wave clear, pulling enemies to side lanes while you generate Fury. In teamfights, you must be the primary engager, utilizing Dragon's Descent to dive straight into the enemy formation and unleash your AoE damage. Zac does not side lane; he lurks near objectives where a fog engage is worth more than a wave.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies by launching your Dragon's Descent over walls to secure high-priority backline access. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely burning their frontline tanks and instantly shredding their backline carriers with empowered Molten Burst. Zac picks are a long-range Slingshot into a group, so hold a dash for the landing rather than for the follow-up.","win":"Build a gold and Dragon-control lead by out-clearing Zac and slowing him off Stretching Strikes before he can close. Close games by forcing fights in Dragon Form, where true damage on Q cuts through his percent-health burst and his revive from Cell Division only delays the inevitable. Zac wins by starting objective fights from fog with his whole team behind him and two health bars in front."},"Brand":{"tldr":"Brand punishes approaches from range with Sear (Q), which stuns once a target is ablaze, and can snowball a duel with Pillar of Flame (W) and Conflagration (E) stacking Blaze. Shyvana answers with Emberstrike (Q) auto-attack resets and the shield off Inferno Aegis (W), then closes with Dragon's Descent (R). Brand wants to land Sear from max range before you reach him, since he has no escape once you're on top of him in melee.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Shyvana Favored","why":"Open with Inferno Aegis (W) for the shield, movement speed, and delayed AoE explosion, your only point at level 1 but enough to clear a camp safely. Brand's single point in Sear or Pillar of Flame threatens poke but not a level-1 duel or camp contest. This establishes a fast, healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Shyvana Dominant","why":"Unlocking Emberstrike (Q) gives you an auto-attack reset that also hits nearby enemies, sharply raising your dueling damage. If Brand tries an early invade, Inferno Aegis's shield absorbs his Sear and Pillar of Flame poke long enough for your Q resets to close and win the fight. This secures your early map pressure and punishes poor invades."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Shyvana Absolute Peak","why":"Access to Molten Burst (E) adds a slowing fireball that explodes on large targets, letting you close down and lock Brand into a duel he can't kite out of, since he has no dash. Landing the slow before he can stack Blaze onto a stun is what wins the trade. This gives you the tools to win most early brawls you commit to."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early farming thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive power-farming and dragon rushing. Keep constant tabs on Brand's jungle clearing lines and only intercept them if you have a clear advantage or counter-gank opportunity. Prioritize reaching your level 6 power spike over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Dragon's Descent Domination","why":"Dragon's Descent (R) grants massive bonus health, an unstoppable dash, and empowered basic abilities. Landing the dash onto Brand skips his Sear range advantage entirely, letting you follow with empowered Molten Burst's fire trail before Pyroclasm can bounce off you. This spike turns you into a genuine map-wide skirmish and teamfight threat against a champion with no personal escape."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Spear of Shojin / Titanic Spike","why":"Completing your core item triggers a massive damage, health, and wave clear multiplier that completely overpowers traditional brawlers or isolated targets. Brand's first item makes the Sear-into-Conflagration rotation a full health bar on a squishy, with no auto-attacks needed."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Juggernaut","why":"Shyvana transitions into a high-impact skirmish diver who relies on generating Fury and landing Dragon's Descent initiations. Your late-game damage scaling shifts into an AoE-focused line, so coordinate with your team before diving in rather than eating a full Pyroclasm bounce alone. Around objective pits, use Molten Burst's slow to close on Brand before he can stack Blaze on your smiter."}],"start":"Start at Red Buff or Raptors, using Inferno Aegis's shield and delayed explosion to clear safely at level 1. Track whether Brand shows near your side of the map before committing to a full clear, since Sear's 1050 range can poke you down from well outside your own reach. Brand clears with Pillar of Flame and Conflagration and is healthy at three, but the clear costs him most of his mana.","scuttle":"Contest the scuttle crab using Molten Burst (E) to slow it while you finish it off with Emberstrike (Q) resets. If Brand shows to contest, close the distance fast — he has no dash or blink, so once you're in melee range his Sear stun is his only way to save the trade. Brand wins a scuttle contest at range rather than at the crab — he will chip you off it instead of fighting for it.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs, using Inferno Aegis's AoE explosion to clear the pack quickly. If Brand shows to contest, Molten Burst's slow lets you close in and punish him before Pillar of Flame's delay resolves. Brand melts objectives with the Blaze percent-health burn, so treat any pit he has vision on as contested.","invade":"Walk into Brand's jungle once you confirm he's committed to a low-health camp. Lead with Molten Burst to slow him, then pop Inferno Aegis for the shield and AoE explosion before he can land a stunning Sear. Brand has no dash at all, so an invade that arrives from two directions ends with him dead in his own jungle.","watch":"Watch for Sear (Q) landing from 1050 range while you're mid-camp — if it stuns you (Blaze already on you from earlier poke), Brand can follow with Pillar of Flame and Conflagration for a full stack burst before you can close the gap. Watch his mana and the Sear cooldown — a Brand without the stun cannot start anything on you.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using wards to safely trace Brand's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming to reach your item and Dragon's Descent power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable frontline threat. Brand is weakest when Sear is on cooldown and there is only one target near him for Conflagration to bounce from.","split":"You can split push exceptionally well due to your massive wave clear, pulling enemies to side lanes while you generate Fury. In teamfights, you must be the primary engager, utilizing Dragon's Descent to dive straight into the enemy formation and unleash your AoE damage. Brand does not hold a side lane; he shoves it and leaves, so answer the wave and not the champion.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies by launching your Dragon's Descent over walls to secure high-priority backline access. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely burning their frontline tanks and instantly shredding their backline carriers with empowered Molten Burst. Brand picks are a Sear from fog into the full rotation, so break line of sight at the entrances he likes.","win":"Generate an unanswerable early-to-mid game gold lead through successful power farming and absolute maximum dragon control. Close the match by relentlessly driving through teamfights in dragon form, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable juggernaut before they can scale their defensive items. Brand wins by dragging fights next to his laners where Pyroclasm can bounce, and by burning objectives faster than you can."},"Fiddlesticks":{"tldr":"Fiddlesticks threatens with Terrify (Q) fear from stealth and Reap (E) silences, then deletes isolated targets with Bountiful Harvest (W) execute drain. Shyvana out-clears with Emberstrike (Q) auto-attack resets and Inferno Aegis (W) shielding, using Molten Burst (E) to stick in duels. Fiddlesticks wants to path unseen and burst you down before Dragon's Descent (R) comes online; Shyvana wants to out-tempo the clear and force fights before Fiddlesticks can set up from stealth.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Shyvana Favored","why":"Open camps with Inferno Aegis (W) for its shield, movement speed, and delayed detonation, then extend with Emberstrike (Q) auto-attack resets once it's up. This establishes a fast, healthy path across your starting camps before Fiddlesticks can scout your route."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Shyvana Dominant","why":"Unlocking Emberstrike (Q) gives you an auto-attack reset that accelerates your single-target clear and dueling. If Fiddlesticks commits to an early invade, use Inferno Aegis's shield to survive Terrify's fear and Emberstrike's reset to shred their health bar in the close-quarters duel. This punishes a poorly timed invade before Fiddlesticks has Reap online."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Shyvana Favored","why":"Molten Burst (E) rounds out your kit as a slowing fireball that explodes on hitting a large target, giving you a reliable way to stick to a duel or a camp. This closes out your early clear and lets you punish a Fiddlesticks who overextends without vision, though their Reap silence can still cut you off mid-combo."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early farming thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive power-farming and dragon rushing. Keep constant tabs on Fiddlesticks's jungle clearing lines and only intercept them if you have a clear advantage or counter-gank opportunity. Prioritize reaching your level 6 power spike over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Dragon's Descent Domination","why":"Dragon's Descent (R) transforms you into a Dragon with bonus health, an unstoppable dash, and empowered basic abilities, causing enemies it hits to flee in fear. Landing it on Fiddlesticks before they can open Crowstorm from range turns the fight, letting you follow up with empowered Molten Burst and Emberstrike for a quick kill."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Spear of Shojin / Titanic Spike","why":"Completing your core item triggers a massive damage, health, and wave clear multiplier that completely overpowers traditional brawlers or isolated targets. Fiddlesticks' first item turns the drain and the fear into a real health bar of damage rather than a delay."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Juggernaut","why":"Shyvana transitions into a high-impact skirmish diver who relies on generating Fury and landing Dragon's Descent initiations. Coordinate with your team before diving, since Fiddlesticks's Crowstorm can delete you if you're caught out of position; around objective pits, use Molten Burst's slow to control the smiter duel."}],"start":"Start your clear at the Raptor or Red Buff camp, using Inferno Aegis (W)'s shield, movement speed, and delayed detonation to speed through camps. Track whether Fiddlesticks shows in lane brush early, since a stealthed Fiddlesticks who doesn't show is likely already pathing toward your side of the map. Fiddlesticks clears safely off Bountiful Harvest and effigies, and his path is built around never being where you look.","scuttle":"Contest the scuttle crab with Emberstrike (Q) resets to burn its health down fast, but hold Inferno Aegis's shield in reserve in case a stealthed Fiddlesticks lands Terrify mid-fight. You can solo early Dragon using your passive Scalemail stacks if Fiddlesticks is confirmed elsewhere on the map. Fiddlesticks avoids a straight scuttle contest before six and would rather bluff you off the crab with an effigy.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the moment they spawn, using Inferno Aegis's detonation and Emberstrike resets to clear them rapidly. Watch for Fiddlesticks appearing from stealth to Reap-silence your smiter mid-contest rather than expecting them to show openly. Fiddlesticks drains objectives quickly, but the channel means any interruption costs him the whole attempt.","invade":"Walk into Fiddlesticks's jungle once you confirm they're committed to a low-health camp elsewhere, since Fiddlesticks has no reliable escape outside Crowstorm's long cooldown. Lead with Inferno Aegis's shield to survive a surprise Terrify fear before committing to the duel. Fiddlesticks is very weak to an early invade — he has no mobility and the drain is cancelled by any damage.","watch":"Watch for Fiddlesticks approaching unseen — Terrify's fear triggers off unseen or stealthed spell damage, and Reap's center silence can cut off your Inferno Aegis or Dragon's Descent mid-engage. Ward your camps, since Fiddlesticks scouts and threatens through stealth rather than open lane pressure. The effigies are free information: a fake Fiddlesticks in your jungle usually means the real one is somewhere else.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, avoid contesting scuttle or camps near brush where a stealthed Fiddlesticks can open with Terrify. Farm safely toward your first item and Dragon's Descent power spike before looking for fights. Fiddlesticks is weakest before six, when Terrify is his only real answer and his clear has left him low.","split":"You can split push exceptionally well due to your massive wave clear, pulling enemies to side lanes while you generate Fury. In teamfights, you must be the primary engager, utilizing Dragon's Descent to dive straight into the enemy formation and unleash your AoE damage. Fiddlesticks does not side lane; he sits in fog near an objective and waits for the fight to come to him.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies by launching your Dragon's Descent over walls to secure high-priority backline access. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely burning their frontline tanks and instantly shredding their backline carriers with empowered Molten Burst. Fiddlesticks picks are a Crowstorm out of unwarded fog, so deep wards are worth more than any item against him.","win":"Generate an unanswerable early-to-mid game gold lead through successful power farming and absolute maximum dragon control. Close the match by relentlessly driving through teamfights in dragon form, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable juggernaut before they can scale their defensive items. Fiddlesticks wins by landing one unseen ultimate into a grouped fight, which is why vision is the entire counter-play."},"Gragas":{"tldr":"Gragas threatens with Body Slam (E) stuns and Drunken Rage (W) auto-reset burst, healing off Happy Hour every time he casts. Shyvana out-clears with Emberstrike (Q) resets and Inferno Aegis (W) shielding, but has no hard CC in human form to punish a Gragas all-in until Dragon's Descent (R) comes online. Gragas wants to catch you mid-clear with Body Slam and trade freely; Shyvana wants to farm through it and force the fight only after reaching Dragon Form.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Shyvana Favored","why":"Open camps with Inferno Aegis (W) for its shield, movement speed, and delayed detonation, then extend with Emberstrike (Q) auto-resets once it's up. This establishes a fast, healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Gragas Favored","why":"Gragas has both Body Slam's stun and Drunken Rage's auto-reset burst online at level 2, while Shyvana's kit is still just no-cost autos and a shield with no hard CC of her own. If Gragas commits to an early invade he can stun and burst you before you can punish him back, so avoid forcing this fight and instead farm toward Dragon's Descent."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Shyvana Absolute Peak","why":"Molten Burst (E) rounds out your kit as a slowing fireball that explodes on hitting a large target, giving you a way to stick to a duel or clear high-health monsters faster. It's still a straight fireball, not a stun, so Gragas's Body Slam can beat you to the punch if he lands it first."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early farming thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive power-farming and dragon rushing. Keep constant tabs on Gragas's jungle clearing lines and only intercept them if you have a clear advantage or counter-gank opportunity. Prioritize reaching your level 6 power spike over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Dragon's Descent Domination","why":"Dragon's Descent (R) transforms you into a Dragon with bonus health, an unstoppable dash, and empowered basic abilities, causing enemies it hits to flee in fear. This is the first point in the matchup where you can force a fight on Gragas rather than farm around his Body Slam, following up with empowered Molten Burst and Emberstrike."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Spear of Shojin / Titanic Spike","why":"Completing your core item triggers a massive damage, health, and wave clear multiplier that completely overpowers traditional brawlers or isolated targets. Gragas' first item makes the Barrel Roll into Body Slam combo a real chunk instead of a nuisance, on a short cooldown."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Juggernaut","why":"Shyvana transitions into a high-impact skirmish diver who relies on generating Fury and landing Dragon's Descent initiations. Coordinate with your team before diving, since a well-timed Gragas Explosive Cask (R) knockback can scatter your team off a bad engage. Around objective pits, use Molten Burst's slow to control the smiter duel against Gragas."}],"start":"Start your clear at the Raptor or Red Buff camp, using Inferno Aegis (W)'s shield, movement speed, and delayed detonation to speed through camps. Gragas clears comfortably off Barrel Roll and Happy Hour sustain, and he reaches the river with health to spend.","scuttle":"Contest the scuttle crab with Emberstrike (Q) resets, but respect Body Slam's stun range if Gragas shows up to fight for it — he can lock you down and finish the trade with Drunken Rage. You can solo early Dragon using your passive Scalemail stacks if Gragas is visible ganking top lane. Gragas contests scuttle with the Body Slam knock-back — he does not need to win the fight, only to move you off it.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the moment they spawn, using Inferno Aegis's detonation and Emberstrike resets to clear them rapidly. If Gragas contests, be wary of his Body Slam stun locking you down for a follow-up Drunken Rage trade. Gragas is an awkward objective fighter alone, but with Explosive Cask he can simply remove you from the pit.","invade":"Walk into Gragas's jungle once you confirm he's committed to a low-health camp elsewhere. Lead with Inferno Aegis's shield before committing, since Gragas can still Body Slam-stun you out of a clean invade if he turns to fight. Gragas escapes an invade with Body Slam through a wall, so the punish window is the seconds after that E lands.","watch":"Watch for Gragas's Body Slam (E) stun range and his Drunken Rage (W) auto-reset burst — both are online at level 2, before you have any hard CC of your own to answer with. A landed Body Slam into Drunken Rage can burn a large chunk of your health with nothing to punish it back except a raw duel. Watch the Body Slam cooldown — it is his engage and his escape, and he cannot have both at once.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, avoid contesting scuttle crabs where Gragas can land Body Slam for free. Funnel into farming to reach your first item and Dragon's Descent power spike, since you have no hard CC to trade with before then. Gragas is weakest with his E down, since his damage without the slam engage is poke and nothing else.","split":"You can split push exceptionally well due to your massive wave clear, pulling enemies to side lanes while you generate Fury. In teamfights, you must be the primary engager, utilizing Dragon's Descent to dive straight into the enemy formation and unleash your AoE damage. Gragas shoves a side lane with Barrel Roll and rotates rather than duelling, so he is often two waves from the fight.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies by launching your Dragon's Descent over walls to secure high-priority backline access. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely burning their frontline tanks and instantly shredding their backline carriers with empowered Molten Burst. Gragas picks are a Cask from over a wall that separates one person from the group at a choke.","win":"Generate an unanswerable early-to-mid game gold lead through successful power farming and absolute maximum dragon control. Close the match by relentlessly driving through teamfights in dragon form, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable juggernaut before they can scale their defensive items. Gragas wins by displacing whoever matters most at the moment the fight starts, usually around an objective pit."},"Ivern":{"tldr":"Ivern doesn't fight for camps directly — Friend of the Forest frees them for gold once a grove grows, so his early jungle presence is about setting up ganks, not dueling. His real threat is Rootcaller (Q), a long-range dash-enabling root that lets his laner crash onto you. Shyvana out-clears with Emberstrike (Q) resets and Inferno Aegis (W) shielding, and has no hard CC of her own in human form to answer a landed root before Dragon's Descent (R) comes online.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Shyvana Favored","why":"Open camps with Inferno Aegis (W) for its shield, movement speed, and delayed detonation, then extend with Emberstrike (Q) auto-resets once it's up. Ivern isn't clearing camps the same way — he's growing groves to free them later — so a fast, healthy clear denies him easy targets outright."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Shyvana Dominant","why":"Unlocking Emberstrike (Q) gives you an auto-attack reset that accelerates your single-target clear and dueling. Ivern himself has no intention of dueling you directly — his threat at this stage is Rootcaller setting up his laner, not a solo invade, so this window is about clearing efficiently rather than punishing Ivern in a 1v1."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Shyvana Absolute Peak","why":"Molten Burst (E) rounds out your kit as a slowing fireball that explodes on hitting a large target, giving you a way to close a duel or clear high-health monsters faster. It doesn't help against a root you can't see coming, so staying warded against Rootcaller matters more than the raw damage upgrade here."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early farming thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive power-farming and dragon rushing. Keep constant tabs on Ivern's jungle clearing lines and only intercept them if you have a clear advantage or counter-gank opportunity. Prioritize reaching your level 6 power spike over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Dragon's Descent Domination","why":"Dragon's Descent (R) transforms you into a Dragon with bonus health, an unstoppable dash, and empowered basic abilities, causing enemies it hits to flee in fear. This is your first hard-CC answer to Ivern's Rootcaller setups — landing it lets you punish the ally he dashes in rather than getting run down yourself."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Spear of Shojin / Titanic Spike","why":"Completing your core item triggers a massive damage, health, and wave clear multiplier that completely overpowers traditional brawlers or isolated targets. Ivern's first item mostly buys shield strength and cooldowns — his spike is what his carry does, not what he does."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Juggernaut","why":"Shyvana transitions into a high-impact skirmish diver who relies on generating Fury and landing Dragon's Descent initiations. Coordinate with your team before diving, since Ivern's Daisy! (R) sentinel can tank and punish an isolated dive if you commit alone. Around objective pits, use Molten Burst's slow to control the smiter duel — Ivern himself typically isn't the one contesting it directly."}],"start":"Start your clear at the Raptor or Red Buff camp, using Inferno Aegis (W)'s shield, movement speed, and delayed detonation to speed through camps. Ivern rarely shows in lane early since he's usually already in the jungle growing groves — ward your own camps rather than expecting to scout him through lane arrivals. Ivern clears without fighting at all — Friend of the Forest frees camps on a timer, so his level and gold are always on schedule.","scuttle":"Contest the scuttle crab with Emberstrike (Q) resets; Ivern himself won't duel you for it, but watch for his laner arriving off a Rootcaller root before you commit fully. You can solo early Dragon using your passive Scalemail stacks if Ivern and his laner are both committed elsewhere. Ivern does not contest scuttle on his own; if he shows at the crab, a laner is already in the river with him.","topObj":"Take early Void Grubs uncontested where possible — Ivern can't fight you for them, he can only free them once a grove has grown on the camp, so a fast take denies him the option outright. Ivern takes objectives with a free smite and Daisy as the body, so treat a pit he has vision on as a two-body fight.","invade":"Invading Ivern rarely finds him alone in a fight — he can't be attacked by non-epic monsters and won't duel you over a camp, so look instead to deny groves he's growing or to punish him if his laner is caught rotating with him. Ivern loses any invade he is caught alone in — the kit is a root and a shield, with nothing that trades back.","watch":"Watch for Rootcaller (Q) — its long range lets Ivern root you from a distance and dash an ally straight onto you, and you have no hard CC of your own in human form to answer that combo before Dragon's Descent. Watch the marked camps: a freed camp on your side of the map means Ivern has already been there and left.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, ward your own camps rather than contesting scuttle in the open, since a Rootcaller root can pull a fed laner onto you before you can react. Funnel into farming to reach your first item and Dragon's Descent power spike before forcing fights. Ivern is weakest in any straight 1v1, at every level, which is why he never takes one voluntarily.","split":"You can split push exceptionally well due to your massive wave clear, pulling enemies to side lanes while you generate Fury. In teamfights, you must be the primary engager, utilizing Dragon's Descent to dive straight into the enemy formation and unleash your AoE damage. Ivern does not hold a side lane; he shields whoever does and makes that lane a 2v1 you have to answer with numbers.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies by launching your Dragon's Descent over walls to secure high-priority backline access. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely burning their frontline tanks and instantly shredding their backline carriers with empowered Molten Burst. Ivern picks are a Rootcaller onto a target his team can reach, so stay outside root range of his brush.","win":"Generate an unanswerable early-to-mid game gold lead through successful power farming and absolute maximum dragon control. Close the match by relentlessly driving through teamfights in dragon form, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable juggernaut before they can scale their defensive items. Ivern wins by making his carry unkillable and his jungle free, which is a tempo lead rather than a fighting one."},"Karthus":{"tldr":"Karthus pokes from range with Lay Waste (Q), which deals bonus damage to isolated targets, and slows/shreds MR with Wall of Pain (W) if you walk through it. Shyvana out-clears with Emberstrike (Q) resets and Inferno Aegis (W) shielding, using Dragon's Descent (R) to close the distance Karthus wants to keep. Karthus wants to poke your clear down from safety and land a global Requiem (R) execute; Shyvana wants to shield through the poke and force a melee fight before Requiem can finish her off.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Shyvana Favored","why":"Open camps with Inferno Aegis (W) for its shield, movement speed, and delayed detonation, then extend with Emberstrike (Q) auto-resets once it's up. This establishes a fast, healthy path across your starting camps before Karthus has mana to poke you consistently."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Shyvana Dominant","why":"Unlocking Emberstrike (Q) gives you an auto-attack reset that accelerates your single-target clear and dueling. If Karthus attempts an early invade, use Inferno Aegis to close the gap and Emberstrike's reset to shatter his health bar before his Lay Waste poke can wear you down from range."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Shyvana Absolute Peak","why":"Molten Burst (E) rounds out your kit as a slowing fireball that explodes on hitting a large target, giving you a way to close a duel or clear high-health monsters faster. It's a projectile Karthus can walk away from at range, so use it to punish him if he overextends rather than expecting it to force a fight."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early farming thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive power-farming and dragon rushing. Keep constant tabs on Karthus's jungle clearing lines and only intercept them if you have a clear advantage or counter-gank opportunity. Prioritize reaching your level 6 power spike over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Dragon's Descent Domination","why":"Dragon's Descent (R) transforms you into a Dragon with bonus health, an unstoppable dash, and empowered basic abilities, causing enemies it hits to flee in fear — the closing tool you need against a Karthus who otherwise just kites and pokes. Landing it also interrupts a channeling Requiem if you can reach him in time. Follow up with empowered Molten Burst and Emberstrike for the kill."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Spear of Shojin / Titanic Spike","why":"Completing your core item triggers a massive damage, health, and wave clear multiplier that completely overpowers traditional brawlers or isolated targets. Karthus' first item is where Lay Waste stops chipping and starts taking a third of a health bar per cast."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Juggernaut","why":"Shyvana transitions into a high-impact skirmish diver who relies on generating Fury and landing Dragon's Descent initiations. Coordinate with your team before diving, since a global Requiem can finish off a low-health teammate the moment you commit elsewhere. Around objective pits, use Molten Burst's slow and Emberstrike's damage to close the smiter duel quickly before Karthus's Lay Waste chips you down."}],"start":"Start your clear at the Raptor or Red Buff camp, using Inferno Aegis (W)'s shield, movement speed, and delayed detonation to speed through camps. Karthus has the fastest clear in the game with Lay Waste and Defile, and he will always be a level ahead if left alone.","scuttle":"Contest the scuttle crab with Emberstrike (Q) resets, staying aware that Lay Waste hits harder if you're caught isolated from allies while fighting for it. You can solo early Dragon using your passive Scalemail stacks if Karthus is visible ganking top lane. Karthus rarely contests a scuttle directly — he takes the free one and out-farms you for the one he skipped.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the moment they spawn, using Inferno Aegis's detonation and Emberstrike resets to clear them rapidly. Karthus rarely contests this in melee, so expect Lay Waste poke from range rather than a direct fight. Karthus melts objectives with Defile, so a pit he reaches first is usually gone before you arrive.","invade":"Walk into Karthus's jungle once you confirm he's committed to a low-health camp elsewhere. Lead with Inferno Aegis's shield to soak his Lay Waste poke before you close the gap for a melee duel he can't easily escape. Karthus is very weak to an early invade, but Death Defied means killing him still costs you the following seconds.","watch":"Watch for a global Requiem (R) at level 6 threatening to execute you from anywhere on the map after its 3-second channel — Requiem is interruptible by any CC, so a landed Dragon's Descent fear or a teammate's CC on Karthus can shut it down if you're near him. Watch Wall of Pain: once it is placed he is committed to the fight, and before that he is only farming.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, avoid fighting isolated where Lay Waste's bonus damage and a low-health Requiem execute can pick you off. Funnel into farming to reach your first item and Dragon's Descent power spike before forcing fights. Karthus is weakest at melee range, where the isolated Lay Waste bonus does not apply and he cannot walk away.","split":"You can split push exceptionally well due to your massive wave clear, pulling enemies to side lanes while you generate Fury. In teamfights, you must be the primary engager, utilizing Dragon's Descent to dive straight into the enemy formation and unleash your AoE damage. Karthus shoves side waves faster than anyone but has no answer to a champion who arrives while he is doing it.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies by launching your Dragon's Descent over walls to secure high-priority backline access. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely burning their frontline tanks and instantly shredding their backline carriers with empowered Molten Burst. Karthus does not pick; he finishes, so the danger is the recall you take at low health rather than the fight.","win":"Generate an unanswerable early-to-mid game gold lead through successful power farming and absolute maximum dragon control. Close the match by relentlessly driving through teamfights in dragon form, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable juggernaut before they can scale their defensive items. Karthus wins by out-farming the map and cashing that lead into Requiem finishes on fights he was never in."},"Lillia":{"tldr":"Lillia adjusts your early tempo by requiring precise use of Molten Burst (E) slows and Inferno Aegis (W) uptime against her ranged poke. Shyvana commands skirmishes through power farming, using Emberstrike (Q) for auto-attack resets and Dragon's Descent (R) for area-of-effect dives once she has Fury. Lillia seeks to poke and kite you with Swirlseed (E) and Blooming Blows (Q) before you can close the distance.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Shyvana Favored","why":"Start with Emberstrike (Q) to strike the camp and the area around it, and use Inferno Aegis (W) for its shield and movement speed to stay healthy while clearing. This establishes a fast, healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Shyvana Dominant","why":"Emberstrike's (Q) auto-attack reset drastically accelerates your single-target clear and dueling potential. If Lillia attempts an early entry into your jungle, use Inferno Aegis (W) for the shield and speed to close the gap through her Swirlseed (E) poke, then chain Emberstrike (Q) resets to shatter her health bar. This secures your early map pressure and punishes poor invades."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Shyvana Absolute Peak","why":"Access to Molten Burst (E) completes your dueling kit: a fireball that explodes on large targets and slows them. Use it to close the gap on Lillia before she can kite with Blooming Blows (Q) move speed, or to accelerate your clear against high-health monsters. This gives you the tools to turn most early brawls if you land the slow."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early farming thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive power-farming and dragon rushing. Keep constant tabs on Lillia's jungle clearing lines and only intercept them if you have a clear advantage or counter-gank opportunity. Prioritize reaching your level 6 power spike over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Dragon's Descent Domination","why":"Dragon's Descent (R) transforms Shyvana, granting bonus health, an unstoppable dash, and empowered basic abilities. Land the dash to pin Lillia against a wall before she can kite away, then follow with an empowered Molten Burst (E) to finish the duel — watch for Lilting Lullaby (R) turning the fight around with a sleep. This is a major power spike that turns you into a map-wide teamfight threat."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Spear of Shojin / Titanic Spike","why":"Completing your core item triggers a massive damage, health, and wave clear multiplier that completely overpowers traditional brawlers or isolated targets. Lillia's first item is when the passing damage becomes real — she stops chipping and starts out-pacing health bars."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Juggernaut","why":"Shyvana transitions into a high-impact skirmish diver and juggernaut who relies on generating Fury and landing Dragon's Descent initiations. Your late-game damage leans on Dragon Form's empowered abilities, so coordinate with your team before diving for burst damage and be wary of Lilting Lullaby (R) sleeping your engage. Around high-tier objective pits, use Molten Burst (E)'s slow to cleanly win the smite duel and secure the objective."}],"start":"Start your clear at the Raptor or Red Buff camp, using Emberstrike (Q) to strike the camp and its surrounding area and Inferno Aegis (W) for the shield and movement speed to keep the path fast. Lillia clears fast and safely, kiting camps with Blooming Blows, and takes very little damage getting to three.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using Molten Burst (E) to slow it and speed up the kill, establishing river control. You can solo early Dragons behind Scalemail's defensive bonuses if Lillia is visibly committed to a top-lane gank. Lillia contests scuttle by out-running you to it rather than by fighting, and leaves the moment you commit.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the moment they spawn, using Emberstrike (Q) to clear them rapidly. Use Molten Burst (E) to slow and run down Lillia if she tries to contest, closing the gap before her Swirlseed (E) can poke you off. Lillia has good objective damage but a poor pit fight, so contest her smite attempts with a body rather than a race.","invade":"Walk boldly into Lillia's jungle the moment you confirm they are engaged with a low-health camp. Lead with your Molten Burst to mark them, popping Inferno Aegis to secure an inescapable high-damage melee duel. Lillia is hard to invade because the passive movement speed means she is gone before the second person arrives.","watch":"Watch for sudden counter-invades if you burn Inferno Aegis (W) early while clearing a camp, leaving you without its shield and movement speed against Lillia's ranged poke and Lilting Lullaby (R) sleep. This leaves you without your main defensive tool to cleanly turn a fight around. Swirlseed is her commitment: once the seed lands she will fight, and before that she is only kiting.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using wards to safely trace Lillia's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming to reach your item and Dragon's Descent power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable frontline threat. Lillia is weakest against anything that can close the gap on her, since her damage is over time and not burst.","split":"You can split push exceptionally well due to your massive wave clear, pulling enemies to side lanes while you generate Fury. In teamfights, you must be the primary engager, utilizing Dragon's Descent to dive straight into the enemy formation and unleash your AoE damage. Lillia side lanes fast and disengages faster, so answering her alone with a slow champion accomplishes nothing.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies by launching your Dragon's Descent over walls to secure high-priority backline access. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely burning their frontline tanks and instantly shredding their backline carriers with empowered Molten Burst. Lillia picks come from a rolled Swirlseed down a corridor, so cross river in the open rather than through a lane.","win":"Generate an unanswerable early-to-mid game gold lead through successful power farming and absolute maximum dragon control. Close the match by relentlessly driving through teamfights in dragon form, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable juggernaut before they can scale their defensive items. Lillia wins by kiting until her dream-dust damage adds up and using the sleep to make one fight unfair."},"Morgana":{"tldr":"Morgana adjusts your early tempo by requiring precise use of Molten Burst (E) slows and Inferno Aegis (W) uptime against her Dark Binding (Q) root. Shyvana commands skirmishes through power farming, using Emberstrike (Q) for auto-attack resets and Dragon's Descent (R) for area-of-effect dives once she has Fury. Morgana seeks to out-gank your early farming tempo with Dark Binding (Q) and exploit your lack of hard crowd control in human form.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Shyvana Favored","why":"Start with Emberstrike (Q) to strike the camp and the area around it, and use Inferno Aegis (W) for its shield and movement speed to stay healthy while clearing. This establishes a fast, healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Shyvana Dominant","why":"Emberstrike's (Q) auto-attack reset drastically accelerates your single-target clear and dueling potential. If Morgana attempts an early entry into your jungle, use Inferno Aegis (W) for the shield and speed to close the gap through her Dark Binding (Q) root threat, then chain Emberstrike (Q) resets to shatter her health bar. This secures your early map pressure and punishes poor invades."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Shyvana Absolute Peak","why":"Access to Molten Burst (E) completes your dueling kit: a fireball that explodes on large targets and slows them. Use it to close the gap on Morgana before Dark Binding (Q) locks you down, or to accelerate your clear against high-health monsters. This gives you the tools to turn most early brawls if you land the slow."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early farming thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive power-farming and dragon rushing. Keep constant tabs on Morgana's jungle clearing lines and only intercept them if you have a clear advantage or counter-gank opportunity. Prioritize reaching your level 6 power spike over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Dragon's Descent Domination","why":"Dragon's Descent (R) transforms Shyvana, granting bonus health, an unstoppable dash, and empowered basic abilities. Land the dash to pin Morgana against a wall, then follow with an empowered Molten Burst (E) to finish the duel before Black Shield (E) can absorb it. This is a major power spike that turns you into a map-wide teamfight threat."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Spear of Shojin / Titanic Spike","why":"Completing your core item triggers a massive damage, health, and wave clear multiplier that completely overpowers traditional brawlers or isolated targets. Morgana's first item makes the binding-into-zone rotation a real chunk instead of a set-up for somebody else."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Juggernaut","why":"Shyvana transitions into a high-impact skirmish diver and juggernaut who relies on generating Fury and landing Dragon's Descent initiations. Your late-game damage leans on Dragon Form's empowered abilities, so coordinate with your team before diving for burst damage and watch for Soul Shackles (R) locking down your whole engage. Around high-tier objective pits, use Molten Burst (E)'s slow to cleanly win the smite duel against Morgana and secure the objective."}],"start":"Start your clear at the Raptor or Red Buff camp, using Emberstrike (Q) to strike the camp and its surrounding area and Inferno Aegis (W) for the shield and movement speed to keep the path fast. Morgana clears very fast with Tormented Shadow and stays healthy off her passive, so her early tempo is better than it looks.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using Molten Burst (E) to slow it and speed up the kill, establishing river control. You can solo early Dragons behind Scalemail's defensive bonuses if Morgana is visibly committed to a top-lane gank. Morgana contests scuttle with a Dark Binding rather than a duel — the crab is lost to the root, not to the damage.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the moment they spawn, using Emberstrike (Q) to clear them rapidly. Use Molten Burst (E) to slow and run down Morgana if she tries to contest, catching her off-guard mid-clear. Morgana takes objectives quickly with the W but has almost no answer to a contested smite fight.","invade":"Walk boldly into Morgana's jungle the moment you confirm they are engaged with a low-health camp. Lead with your Molten Burst to mark them, popping Inferno Aegis to secure an inescapable high-damage melee duel. Morgana is very weak to an early invade, since Black Shield stops the crowd control and not the damage.","watch":"Watch for sudden counter-invades if you burn Inferno Aegis (W) early while clearing a camp, leaving you without its shield and movement speed against Dark Binding (Q)'s long-range root. This leaves you without your main defensive tool to cleanly turn a fight around. Dark Binding is the whole threat; sidestep it and she has a slow zone and a shield, neither of which kills you.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using wards to safely trace Morgana's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming to reach your item and Dragon's Descent power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable frontline threat. Morgana is weakest away from her laners, where the binding has no follow-up and the ultimate has no second target.","split":"You can split push exceptionally well due to your massive wave clear, pulling enemies to side lanes while you generate Fury. In teamfights, you must be the primary engager, utilizing Dragon's Descent to dive straight into the enemy formation and unleash your AoE damage. Morgana does not side lane; she plays beside a carry, where the shield turns a 2v2 into a 2v1.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies by launching your Dragon's Descent over walls to secure high-priority backline access. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely burning their frontline tanks and instantly shredding their backline carriers with empowered Molten Burst. Morgana picks are a binding from fog into her team, so cross the river outside her range or with a body between.","win":"Generate an unanswerable early-to-mid game gold lead through successful power farming and absolute maximum dragon control. Close the match by relentlessly driving through teamfights in dragon form, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable juggernaut before they can scale their defensive items. Morgana wins by making one fight near her team unwinnable — a shield on the right person and a root on the wrong one."},"Taliyah":{"tldr":"Taliyah adjusts your early mechanical tempo by requiring high precision over Molten Burst (E) slows and Inferno Aegis (W) shield timing. Shyvana commands skirmish velocities through Emberstrike (Q) auto-attack resets and true damage, deploying Dragon's Descent (R) to force fights once she has Fury. Taliyah seeks to ground your dashes with Threaded Volley or Seismic Shove, stun you in an Unraveled Earth minefield, and kite from range before you build Fury.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Shyvana Favored","why":"Start with Emberstrike (Q) for a true-damage attack that hits both the target and the surrounding area, and use Inferno Aegis (W) for its shield to keep your clear healthy. Taliyah's mana-based ranged kit has no AoE clear tool this early, giving Shyvana the faster opening."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Shyvana Dominant","why":"The recast on Emberstrike (Q) gives you an auto-attack reset that accelerates your single-target clear and dueling potential. If Taliyah attempts an uncalculated early entry into your jungle, activate Inferno Aegis (W) for the shield and speed to close the gap on her before taking a duel using your Q reset to shatter her health bar. This secures your early map pressure and punishes poor invades."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Shyvana Absolute Peak","why":"Access to Molten Burst (E) completes your dueling kit with a fireball that explodes on large targets and slows them. Use it to close the range gap on Taliyah before she can reposition with Rock Surfing, or burst through large monsters to accelerate your clear speed."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early farming thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive power-farming and dragon rushing. Keep constant tabs on Taliyah's jungle clearing lines and only intercept them if you have a clear advantage or counter-gank opportunity. Prioritize reaching your level 6 power spike over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Dragon's Descent Domination","why":"Dragon's Descent (R) transforms Shyvana into Dragon Form with empowered basics and a dash-leap. That dash is not truly unstoppable — Taliyah's Threaded Volley (Q) or Seismic Shove (W) can ground it if she reacts in time, and her own Weaver's Wall (R) can block the path outright. Land the initiation before she can react and follow with empowered Molten Burst for the kill."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Spear of Shojin / Titanic Spike","why":"Completing your core item triggers a massive damage, health, and wave clear multiplier that completely overpowers traditional brawlers or isolated targets. Taliyah's first item makes the volley-into-shove combo a real burst instead of chip damage from range."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Juggernaut","why":"Shyvana transitions into a high-impact skirmish diver and unstoppable juggernaut who relies entirely on generating Fury and landing massive Dragon's Descent initiations. Your late-game raw damage scaling shifts into a purely AoE-focused line, making it imperative to coordinate with your team before diving into extreme burst damage. Maintain extreme technical precision around high-tier objective pits to cleanly melt the contesting smiter with your E mark and secure the objective."}],"start":"Start your clear at the Raptor or Red Buff camp, using Inferno Aegis (W) for its shield and burst explosion against grouped monsters to keep your clear healthy. Taliyah clears very fast with Threaded Volley on worked ground and rotates faster than almost anyone off her passive.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using Molten Burst (E) to slow it and drop its health bar for river control. You can solo early Dragons using your Scalemail passive resistance stacks if Taliyah is visible ganking top lane. Taliyah contests scuttle at range with the volley and the shove rather than by standing on the crab.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the exact moment they spawn, using Inferno Aegis (W) to shield through their damage while you clear. Use Molten Burst (E) to slow and run down Taliyah if she dares to contest, catching her off-guard mid-clear. Taliyah has good objective damage but a poor pit fight, so contest her smite attempts rather than racing them.","invade":"Walk boldly into Taliyah's jungle the moment you confirm she is engaged with a low-health camp. Lead with Molten Burst to slow her, popping Inferno Aegis for the shield and speed to close the gap before she can kite you with Threaded Volley. Taliyah is weak to an early invade, since Seismic Shove is her only escape and it needs to land to work.","watch":"Watch for sudden counter-invades if you exhaust Inferno Aegis early while clearing a camp, leaving you without its shield and speed. Also track Taliyah's Threaded Volley (Q) and Seismic Shove (W), which both ground and shut off dashes — that can deny Dragon's Descent's leap if she lands one first. Worked ground is visible — she does far less damage on it, so fighting on ground she has already used is safer.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using wards to safely trace Taliyah's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming to reach your item and Dragon's Descent power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable frontline threat. Taliyah is weakest at close range with the shove spent, where she has no way to make distance again.","split":"You can split push exceptionally well due to your massive wave clear, pulling enemies to side lanes while you generate Fury. In teamfights, you must be the primary engager, utilizing Dragon's Descent to dive straight into the enemy formation and unleash your AoE damage. Taliyah shoves side waves and crosses the map on her passive, so she is rarely where the wave says she is.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies by launching your Dragon's Descent over walls to secure high-priority backline access. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, burning through their frontline tanks and shredding their backline carriers with empowered Molten Burst in Dragon Form. Taliyah picks are a shove into her team or a wall behind you, so watch which direction she is facing before you cross.","win":"Generate an unanswerable early-to-mid game gold lead through successful power farming and absolute maximum dragon control. Close the match by relentlessly driving through teamfights in dragon form, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable juggernaut before they can scale their defensive items. Taliyah wins by splitting the map with Weaver's Wall and zoning the fight her team chooses to take."},"Zyra":{"tldr":"Zyra is a ranged mage with no melee clear tools of her own — Deadly Spines (Q) and Grasping Roots (E) are both long-range zoning spells, and Grasping Roots is a root at up to 1100 range that's her real threat, not a stat check. Shyvana closes the gap with Inferno Aegis's (W) speed and punishes with Emberstrike (Q) resets and true damage once she's in melee range, using Molten Burst (E) to answer Zyra's zoning from a distance.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Shyvana Favored","why":"Start with Inferno Aegis (W) to secure a fast area-of-effect clear, utilizing the movement speed and continuous magic damage to melt the camp. Execute basic attacks to extend Inferno Aegis's duration, ensuring maximum efficiency. This establishes an exceptionally fast and healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Shyvana Dominant","why":"Unlocking Emberstrike (Q) provides an impactful auto-attack reset that drastically accelerates your single-target clear and dueling potential. If Zyra attempts an uncalculated early entry into your jungle, activate Inferno Aegis to stick to them before taking an aggressive close-quarters duel using your Q reset to shatter their health bar. This completely secures your early map pressure and punishes poor invades."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Shyvana Absolute Peak","why":"Access to Molten Burst (E) completes your lethal dueling portfolio by introducing a versatile fireball that marks enemies for percent-health damage. Use this tool to instantly secure your damage trades or melt through high-health monsters to accelerate your clear speed. This grants an elite operational flexibility allowing you to turn almost any early brawl if you land your mark accurately."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early farming thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive power-farming and dragon rushing. Keep constant tabs on Zyra's jungle clearing lines and only intercept them if you have a clear advantage or counter-gank opportunity. Prioritize reaching your level 6 power spike over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Dragon's Descent Domination","why":"Dragon's Descent (R) introduces an immense combat transformation that provides massive bonus health, an unstoppable dash, and empowered area-of-effect abilities. Execute a precise ultimate activation to drag Zyra into a wall, unleashing your empowered Molten Burst for a clean kill. This massive mechanical spike turns you into an absolute map-wide teamfight threat who dictates the shape of every fight through raw damage."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Spear of Shojin / Titanic Spike","why":"Completing your core item triggers a massive damage, health, and wave clear multiplier that completely overpowers traditional brawlers or isolated targets. Zyra's first item is where plant damage stops being chip and starts removing a squishy health bar in one rotation."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Juggernaut","why":"Shyvana transitions into a high-impact skirmish diver and unstoppable juggernaut who relies entirely on generating Fury and landing massive Dragon's Descent initiations. Your late-game raw damage scaling shifts into a purely AoE-focused line, making it imperative to coordinate with your team before diving into extreme burst damage. Maintain extreme technical precision around high-tier objective pits to cleanly melt the contesting smiter with your E mark and secure the objective."}],"start":"Start your clear at the Raptor or Red Buff camp, using Inferno Aegis's AoE detonation to speed up the clear. Zyra's kit has no dedicated jungle-clear AoE, so track her route for signs she's struggling to keep pace. Zyra clears fast with plants doing the work, and she reaches three with seeds already placed where she intends to fight.","scuttle":"Contest the scuttle crab as it spawns — approach from an angle that avoids Grasping Roots' long range, since getting rooted mid-fight lets her follow up with Deadly Spines. If she's committed to a top-lane gank, that's a window to solo an early Dragon behind Scalemail's stacked resistances. Zyra contests scuttle on seeds — a crab fight over a grown plant garden is one she wins without duelling.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the moment they spawn; Inferno Aegis clears them quickly. If Zyra contests, close the distance fast — she has no answer to a melee duel once you're inside Grasping Roots' cast range. Zyra melts objectives with plant damage but has no way to survive a pit fight she does not control.","invade":"Invade her jungle when she's engaged with a camp and can't safely disengage — approach from a flank so Grasping Roots can't land cleanly, then use Inferno Aegis's speed to close and finish the duel in melee. Zyra is very weak to an early invade away from her seeds, since the kit has no escape at all.","watch":"Watch for Grasping Roots at any range up to 1100 — it's a root with no level gate, and getting hit by it while approaching lets Zyra's plants and Deadly Spines punish you before you ever reach melee range. Seeds on the ground are the whole warning — kill them on sight and her damage drops by more than half.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, give up contested scuttle crabs and ward to trace Zyra's routes, since a well-placed seed and plant can punish a blind approach. Prioritize farming to your first item and level 6 so Dragon's Descent's leap closes the gap in one motion. Zyra is weakest with no seeds down and her root on cooldown, which is when she has nothing at all.","split":"You can split push exceptionally well due to your massive wave clear, pulling enemies to side lanes while you generate Fury. In teamfights, you must be the primary engager, utilizing Dragon's Descent to dive straight into the enemy formation and unleash your AoE damage. Zyra does not side lane; she plays near her team where the plants and the knock-up cover a choke.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies by launching your Dragon's Descent over walls to secure high-priority backline access. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely burning their frontline tanks and instantly shredding their backline carriers with empowered Molten Burst. Zyra picks are a Grasping Roots through a choke into a seeded garden, so never walk a corridor she has covered.","win":"Build a gold and Dragon-control lead by out-clearing Zyra, whose kit isn't built for jungle camps, and closing distance on her before her seeds and plants stack up. Close games by forcing fights in Dragon Form, where the leap on Dragon's Descent skips past her zoning entirely. Zyra wins by covering an objective choke in plants and ending any fight taken inside it with the knock-up."},"Locke":{"tldr":"Locke is a weak-side execute jungler: he loses honest duels and farms for the totem — fight him marks-zero, punish every spent blink, and never coin-flip at sliver HP inside Purgatory.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Danger — Avoid Locke","why":"Locke clears at range but pays mana and HP for it — his level 1 duel is honest only with nail marks banked, so fight him marks-zero. take the contact trades your kit is built for."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Danger — Avoid Locke","why":"His E is engage AND escape in one button: the ten seconds after his blink are your whole trade window. take the contact trades your kit is built for."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Respect Locke","why":"Full kits online — sidestep his Ritual Nails (Q) marks and burst never adds up, while your Emberstrike (Q) recasts and Molten Burst (E) slow let you close the gap and win the honest duel."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Even — Farm & Track","why":"Levels 4-5 vs Locke are a tempo war — he wants quiet farm into his 6; tax his camps, keep his marks off you at contests, and bank the gold lead his weak duel hands you."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Even Ult Window","why":"Locke's Purgatory (R) grounds your Dragon's Descent (R) dash and executes anyone who fights below the floor inside his totem — disengage above roughly 15% health or leave the zone entirely. Outside the totem, Dragon's Descent is still one of the biggest level-6 spikes in the jungle, so treat this window as even rather than automatically yours."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Lich Bane / Shadowflame Spike","why":"His Lich Bane spike is real: the blink-consume chunk one-shots chipped squishies. Respect the first-item window, then note his E is still a one-way door — punish every spent blink. Locke's first item makes the mark consume hit for a real chunk, which is when the blink stops being a poke tool."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Shyvana Favored at Full Build","why":"Late-game Locke scales with his seal count, not his items — deny executes and his floor never rises. Focus him when E is down and never let \"almost dead\" linger inside Purgatory."}],"start":"Standard start — his level-1 clear is ranged but mana-hungry and his duel is honest at best; you dictate the early tempo, not him. Locke clears at range with Ritual Nails and banks marks while doing it, so his first scuttle arrives with stacks up.","scuttle":"Take the first scuttle contact with confidence unless you’re carrying 2-3 nail marks — his blink-consume needs stacks, so an unmarked contest is yours. Locke contests scuttle on his mark count, not his health bar — an unmarked contest is much safer than a stacked one.","topObj":"Contest grubs and herald hard pre-6; post-6, watch the totem drop — leave the pit above the execute floor and re-enter when it expires. Locke takes objectives adequately and the Purgatory totem makes a late pit fight far more dangerous than the numbers suggest.","invade":"Invade the side he shows opposite — his escape is one blink on a ten-second timer, and a Locke who spends E on a camp is a kill waiting for a ping. Locke escapes an invade with one blink on a ten-second timer, so an invade into a spent Ashen Pursuit is close to free.","watch":"Watch your own mark stacks like a cooldown: a stacked nail string into the blink-through consume deletes half a health bar — drop stacks before they mature. Watch your own mark stacks like a cooldown — a full nail string into the blink consume takes half a health bar.","weak":"If he executes a lane early, respect the seal — his floor just rose permanently. Deny the snowball by matching his ganks cross-map rather than duelling into his counter-window. Locke is weakest in the seconds after his blink is spent, when he is a ranged champion standing in melee range.","split":"He punishes greedy splitters with totem duels — send answers in pairs or shove the opposite side; solo brawls at low HP are exactly the fight he farms. Locke side lanes well because the totem punishes anyone who answers him at anything less than full health.","picks":"His picks start with a nail string from fog — hug vision lines, and treat three stacks plus his E up as a dive threat even at even HP. Locke picks start with a nail string from fog, so hug vision lines and treat three stacks as a dive threat.","win":"Win the duel war he keeps declining: invade the spent-blink windows, end fights above his floor, and starve the seal count that his whole late game is built on. Locke wins by raising his execute floor with every seal until low-health fights inside his totem stop being survivable."}};
